COPY public.lesson_grammar_notes (id, lesson_id, title, explanation, examples, order_index) FROM stdin;
631	asking_directions	Asking for directions	Use 'Hoe kom ik bij...' or 'Weet u waar... is?' to ask for directions politely on the street.	["Hoe kom ik bij het station?", "Weet u waar de bakker is?"]	0
632	asking_directions	Basic directions	Common direction words in Dutch are 'naar links' (to the left), 'naar rechts' (to the right), and 'rechtdoor' (straight ahead).	["Ga naar links bij de hoek.", "Loop rechtdoor tot de kerk."]	1
633	at_the_doctor	Expressing physical ailments with 'hebben'	In Dutch, physical symptoms like headache, fever, or pain are usually expressed using the verb 'hebben' (to have).	["Ik heb hoofdpijn.", "Heeft u ook koorts?"]	0
634	at_the_doctor	Polite form 'u'	In professional contexts like a doctor's consultation, it is standard practice to use the formal pronoun 'u'.	["Ga lekker zitten.", "Heeft u verder nog vragen?"]	1
635	at_the_market	Using the verb 'pinnen'	In the Netherlands, 'pinnen' is a very common verb that means paying with your debit card (and entering a PIN or using contactless payment). Avoid saying 'betalen met kaart' as 'pinnen' is what locals always use.	["Kan ik hier pinnen?", "Ik wil graag pinnen."]	0
636	at_the_market	Ordering with 'Doe maar...'	At markets or casual shops, you can order things politely by saying 'Doe maar...' followed by the quantity and item. It is a friendly, colloquial way of saying 'I would like...'.	["Doe maar een kilo tomaten, alstublieft.", "Doe maar vier gele bananen."]	1
637	cafe_order_basic	Polite Requests with 'Mag ik...?'	In Dutch, starting a request with 'Mag ik...?' (May I...?) is a common, polite way to ask for items or permission in shops and restaurants.	["Mag ik een glas water?", "Mag ik de menukaart zien?", "Mag ik vragen om de rekening?"]	0
638	cafe_order_basic	Offering and Choosing with 'Wilt u...?'	Servers often ask 'Wilt u...?' (Would you like...?) to offer choices. Customers can respond with 'Ja, graag' (Yes, please) or 'Nee, dank u' (No, thank you).	["Wilt u een cappuccino?", "Wilt u er suiker in?", "Wilt u de bon hebben?"]	1
639	cw_adjectives_basic	Basic Adjectives Predicate Position	When an adjective follows a verb like 'zijn' (to be) in Dutch, it remains in its basic form and does not get an extra -e ending.	["Het huis is groot.", "De kat is klein.", "De auto is snel."]	0
640	cw_animals	Articles with Nouns (De vs. Het)	Every Dutch noun uses either 'de' or 'het' as its definite article. In this lesson, hond, kat, vogel, vis, and koe use 'de', while konijn, paard, and varken use 'het'.	["de hond (the dog)", "het konijn (the rabbit)"]	0
641	cw_city_places	Articles with Places (de vs het)	In Dutch, nouns take either 'de' or 'het'. City places can be either, so it is best to learn the article with each word.	["de supermarkt", "het ziekenhuis", "de bibliotheek", "het station"]	0
642	cw_colors	Predicate Adjectives with Colors	When a color adjective comes after the verb 'zijn' (to be), it does not take an extra '-e' ending.	["De appel is rood.", "De zon is geel."]	0
643	cw_conjunctions_basic	Basic Coordinating Conjunctions	In Dutch, conjunctions like 'en', 'maar', 'of', 'want', and 'dus' connect words or full clauses. 'Want' introduces a reason without changing the subject-verb word order in the main clause.	["Ik eet, want ik heb honger.", "Hij is moe, maar hij werkt."]	0
644	cw_days_of_week	Using 'op' with days of the week	In Dutch, to say 'on Monday' or 'on Tuesday', you use the preposition 'op' followed by the day name in lowercase.	["Ik werk op maandag.", "Ik dans op vrijdag."]	0
645	cw_degree_adverbs	Degree Adverbs (Graadwoorden)	Degree adverbs modify adjectives or other adverbs to express intensity. 'Heel' and 'erg' increase intensity strongly, 'echt' adds emphasis, while 'vrij', 'best', 'nogal', and 'tamelijk' represent moderate intensity.	["Het water is heel koud.", "Het is vrij goedkoop."]	0
646	cw_direction_words	Using Prepositions of Direction in Dutch	Direction words like 'links' and 'rechts' often combine with 'naar' when indicating movement (e.g., 'naar links' = to the left). Position words like 'voor', 'achter', and 'naast' are used directly before nouns with 'het' or 'de'.	["Ga naar links. (Go to the left.)", "Het huis is naast de bank. (The house is next to the bank.)"]	0
647	cw_emotions	Expressing feelings with 'zijn'	In Dutch, emotion adjectives are commonly used with the verb 'zijn' (to be).	["Ik ben blij.", "Hij is moe."]	0
648	cw_frequency_words	Position of Frequency Words	In standard Dutch main clauses, frequency words like 'altijd', 'vaak', 'soms', 'nooit', and 'meestal' typically appear directly after the conjugated verb.	["Ik drink altijd water.", "Hij werkt meestal thuis."]	0
649	cw_health_words	Expressing Pain in Dutch	To describe pain in a body part, you can say 'Ik heb pijn in mijn...' (I have pain in my...) or '[Body part] doet pijn' ([Body part] hurts).	["Ik heb pijn in mijn hoofd.", "Mijn buik doet pijn."]	0
650	cw_linking_words	Word Order with Linking Words	When a sentence starts with a linking adverb like 'eerst', 'dan', or 'daarna', inversion occurs: the verb comes directly after the linking word, followed by the subject.	["Eerst drink ik koffie.", "Dan ga ik werken."]	0
651	cw_months	Capitalization of Months	In Dutch, the names of the months are written in lowercase letters, unlike in English, unless they appear at the beginning of a sentence.	["Het is koud in januari.", "December is de laatste maand."]	0
652	cw_nature	Articles with Nature Nouns	In Dutch, nouns use either 'de' or 'het'. Most nature words in this lesson use 'de' (de boom, de bloem, de zon, de maan, de lucht, de zee, de rivier, de berg). An exception is 'het gras'.	["de boom (the tree)", "het gras (the grass)"]	0
653	cw_negative_indefinites	Negative Words in Dutch	In Dutch, 'niemand', 'niets', 'nergens', and 'nooit' are absolute negative terms for people, things, places, and time. 'Bijna' and 'nauwelijks' express degree or limited amount.	["Er is niemand. (There is nobody.)", "Ik zie niets. (I see nothing.)", "Ik kom nooit te laat. (I never arrive late.)"]	0
654	cw_numbers_1_20	Dutch Numbers 1–20	Numbers in Dutch are essential for counting, shopping, and telling time. Numbers 13 to 19 generally end in '-tien' (with slight variations like dertien and achttien).	["drie -> dertien", "vier -> veertien", "acht -> achttien"]	0
655	cw_numbers_20_100	Dutch Compound Numbers	In Dutch, numbers above 20 place the unit first, followed by 'en' (and), and then the tens word. For example, 21 is 'eenentwintig' (one-and-twenty).	["21 = eenentwintig", "35 = vijfendertig", "48 = achtenveertig"]	0
656	cw_numbers_large	Large Numbers in Dutch	In Dutch, hundred (honderd) and thousand (duizend) are combined directly with unit digits without spaces (e.g., tweehonderd, vijfduizend). Million (miljoen) is written as a separate word (e.g., een miljoen).	["honderd (100)", "tweehonderd (200)", "duizend (1000)", "een miljoen (1,000,000)"]	0
657	cw_opposites	Adjectives in Predicate Position	In Dutch, when an adjective comes after the verb 'zijn' (to be) to describe a noun, it remains in its basic uninflected form without adding an '-e'.	["Het huis is groot. (The house is big.)", "De muis is klein. (The mouse is small.)"]	0
658	cw_ordinal_numbers	Ordinal Numbers Formation	In Dutch, ordinal numbers are formed by adding -de or -ste to the cardinal number. Numbers 1 (eerste) and 3 (derde) are irregular exceptions.	["twee -> tweede", "vier -> vierde", "acht -> achtste"]	0
659	cw_polite_basics	Informal vs. Formal Politeness	Use 'alsjeblieft' and 'dank je' with friends, family, and young people. Use 'alstublieft' and 'dank u' when addressing elders, strangers, or in professional settings.	["Een koffie, alsjeblieft. (informal)", "Een moment, alstublieft. (formal)"]	0
660	cw_polite_basics	Using 'Graag' in Responses	'Graag' means 'gladly' or 'with pleasure'. When offered something like food or drink, answering 'Ja, graag!' is the standard polite way to say 'Yes, please!'	["Koffie? - Ja, graag!", "Ik help je graag."]	1
661	cw_prepositions	Dutch Prepositions	Prepositions link nouns or pronouns to other words in a sentence, indicating time, position, or direction.	["Ik woon in Amsterdam. (I live in Amsterdam.)", "Het boek ligt op tafel. (The book lies on the table.)", "Ik ga naar huis. (I go home.)"]	0
662	cw_professions_basic	Stating Your Profession in Dutch	To state a profession in Dutch, use 'zijn' (to be) + 'een' + profession name, or simply 'zijn' + profession name.	["Hij is een leraar.", "Ik ben verpleegkundige."]	0
663	cw_pronouns	Dutch Subject Pronouns	In Dutch, personal pronouns are used to replace nouns acting as subjects. Note that 'zij' can mean both 'she' (singular) and 'they' (plural), depending on the verb conjugation.	["Zij slaapt (She sleeps)", "Zij slapen (They sleep)"]	0
664	cw_quantities	Dutch Quantity Words	Quantity words like 'veel' (much/many), 'weinig' (little/few), 'meer' (more), 'minder' (less), and 'genoeg' (enough) are placed directly before nouns or verbs to indicate amounts.	["Ik drink veel water.", "Wij hebben weinig tijd.", "Ik heb genoeg geld."]	0
665	cw_question_words	Dutch Question Words Structure	In Dutch, question words come at the very beginning of the sentence, followed immediately by the verb and then the subject.	["Waar is het station?", "Wat doe je nu?"]	0
666	cw_seasons	Nouns and Gender (de / het)	All four seasons in Dutch take the definite article 'de': de lente, de zomer, de herfst, de winter. Most weather elements also take 'de' (de zon, de regen, de wind, de sneeuw, de wolk), while weather itself takes 'het' (het weer).	["de zomer is warm", "het weer is goed"]	0
667	cw_shopping_words	Verbs in present tense: kopen and betalen	In Dutch, regular verbs drop -en to form the stem. Use the stem for 'ik' (koop, betaal) and full infinitive for 'wij' (kopen, betalen).	["Ik koop een mooi boek.", "Wij kopen vers brood."]	0
668	cw_time_telling	Half Hours in Dutch	In Dutch, 'half' refers to the upcoming hour, not the past hour. For example, 'half twee' means 01:30 (halfway to two).	["Het is half twee. (It is 1:30)", "Het is half drie. (It is 2:30)"]	0
669	cw_top_verbs	Basic Present Tense Conjugation	In Dutch, the verb changes according to the subject pronoun (ik, jij, hij/zij, wij/jullie/zij).	["Ik ben (I am) / Wij zijn (We are)", "Ik heb (I have) / Zij hebben (They have)", "Ik kan (I can) / Wij kunnen (We can)"]	0
670	cw_verbs_daily_actions	Present Tense Regular Verbs	In Dutch present tense, singular subjects drop the -en ending and add -t for hij/zij/u, while plural subjects (wij/zij/jullie) keep the full infinitive.	["Ik werk -> Hij werkt -> Wij werken", "Ik kook -> Zij kookt -> Wij koken"]	0
671	dialogue_at_pharmacy	Modal Verb 'mogen' (permission/may)	In Dutch, 'mogen' is used to ask or give permission regarding dosages and instructions.	["Hoeveel tabletten mag ik per dag nemen?", "U mag maximaal zes tabletten per dag nemen."]	0
672	dialogue_at_pharmacy	Polite form 'U'	In a formal or professional setting like a pharmacy, standard polite Dutch uses 'u' and 'uw'.	["Wat voor klachten heeft u?", "Ik doe de bon netjes in uw tasje."]	1
673	dialogue_restaurant_meal	Polite requests with 'mag ik' and 'wilt u'	In Dutch restaurants, polite requests and offers use modal verbs like 'mogen' and 'willen'. Inverting the subject and verb forms a question naturally.	["Mag ik de rekening vragen?", "Wilt u alvast iets drinken?"]	0
674	dialogue_restaurant_meal	Using 'graag' and 'alstublieft'	'Graag' means 'gladly' or 'please' when accepting or ordering items. 'Alstublieft' is used for polite ordering and when handing things over.	["Een glas plat water graag.", "Doe maar een cappuccino, alstublieft."]	1
675	grammar_adjective_basics	Adjective Position and -e Ending	In Dutch, adjectives stand before the noun. Usually, you add an '-e' ending to the adjective (e.g. 'de grote man', 'het mooie boek', 'een snelle auto').	["de grote man (the big man)", "het mooie boek (the beautiful book)"]	0
676	grammar_adjective_basics	The Exception: 'een' + HET-word	The only main exception is when an adjective stands after 'een' (or no article) in front of a singular 'het'-word. In that case, do NOT add '-e'.	["een groot huis (a big house)", "een klein kind (a small child)"]	1
677	grammar_als_conditional	Conditional Clauses with 'Als'	When starting a sentence with 'als' (if/when), the conjugated verb in that first clause is pushed to the very end. Immediately following the comma, the main clause starts with its conjugated verb (inversion). Thus, two verbs meet around the comma.	["Als ik tijd heb, ga ik fietsen.", "Als het regent, blijf ik binnen."]	0
678	grammar_comparative	Comparatives (-er dan)	To compare two things in Dutch, add '-er' to the adjective and use the word 'dan' for 'than'.	["klein -> kleiner dan", "groot -> groter dan"]	0
679	grammar_comparative	Superlatives (het -st)	To state that something is the most, add '-st' to the adjective and precede it with 'het'.	["klein -> het kleinst", "snel -> het snelst"]	1
680	grammar_conditional_zou	Conditional with zou / zouden	Use 'zou' for singular subjects (ik, jij, hij, zij) and 'zouden' for plural subjects (wij, jullie, zij). Additional verbs are placed at the end of the clause in the infinitive form.	["Ik zou graag koffie willen.", "Wij zouden graag meegaan."]	0
681	grammar_dat_clauses	Subordinate Clauses with 'dat'	In Dutch, the word 'dat' (that) introduces a subordinate clause (bijzin). In a subordinate clause, all conjugated verbs move to the end of the clause.	["Ik denk dat hij komt. (I think that he comes.)", "Ze zegt dat het koud is. (She says that it is cold.)", "Wij weten dat Jan leest. (We know that Jan reads.)"]	0
682	grammar_de_het	De vs Het Articles	Dutch nouns take either 'de' or 'het'. 'De' is used for around 70% of words, including all personal nouns and all plural nouns. 'Het' is used for neutral nouns and all diminutive words ending in '-je'.	["de man (the man)", "het huis (the house)", "de boeken (the books)", "het kopje (the cup)"]	0
683	grammar_demonstratives	Demonstrative Pronouns in Dutch	Dutch demonstrative pronouns depend on whether the noun takes 'de' or 'het', and whether the object is nearby or further away.	["de stoel -> deze stoel (this chair, near) / die stoel (that chair, far)", "het boek -> dit boek (this book, near) / dat boek (that book, far)"]	0
684	grammar_diminutives	Dutch Diminutives	To make nouns smaller, cuter, or cozier, Dutch uses the suffixes -je, -tje, or -pje. Crucially, all diminutives become 'het' words, regardless of the original noun's article.	["het boekje", "het tafeltje", "het boompje"]	0
685	grammar_directional_prepositions	Directional Prepositions Overview	Dutch prepositions indicate direction and location. 'Naar' shows movement towards a place, 'uit' indicates coming out of or country origin, 'van' marks origin/starting point, and 'bij' denotes location at or near a person/place.	["Ik ga naar de stad. (I go to the city.)", "Ik kom uit Spanje. (I come from Spain.)", "Ik ben bij de dokter. (I am at the doctor.)"]	0
686	grammar_er	Using 'er' for Existence and Location	In Dutch, 'er' translates to 'there' in expressions of existence like 'there is' or 'there are'. Unlike English, Dutch frequently replaces 'is' with posture verbs like 'staat' (standing) or 'ligt' (lying down).	["Er is een man. (There is a man.)", "Er zijn twee mannen. (There are two men.)", "Er staat een auto. (There is a car standing.)", "Er ligt een krant. (There is a newspaper lying.)"]	0
687	grammar_future_gaan	Near Future with 'gaan' + Infinitive	To express planned or upcoming actions in Dutch, combine a conjugated form of 'gaan' in the second position with an infinitive verb placed at the very end of the clause.	["Ik ga morgen werken.", "Wij gaan samen eten."]	0
688	grammar_future_zullen	Future Tense with Zullen	To talk about the future, predictions, or promises in Dutch, conjugate 'zullen' in position 2 and place the main verb in its infinitive form at the end of the sentence.	["Ik zal morgen komen.", "Het zal regenen.", "Wij zullen je helpen."]	0
689	grammar_imperative	The Imperative (De Gebiedende Wijs)	To give a command or instruction in Dutch, use only the verb stem. Do not include a subject pronoun (like 'jij' or 'u') or the infinitive '-en' ending.	["Luister naar mij! (Listen to me!)", "Kom hier! (Come here!)", "Eet je soep! (Eat your soup!)"]	0
690	grammar_imperfect_irregular	Irregular Simple Past (Onregelmatige Verleden Tijd)	Dutch irregular verbs (strong verbs) change their core vowel in the simple past tense instead of adding -te or -de.	["zijn -> was / waren", "hebben -> had / hadden", "gaan -> ging / gingen", "zien -> zag / zagen"]	0
691	grammar_imperfect_regular	't Kofschip Rule for Simple Past (OVT)	To form the simple past of regular Dutch verbs, find the verb stem (infinitive minus -en). If the last letter of the stem is in 't kofschip (t, k, f, s, ch, p), add -te (singular) or -ten (plural). If not, add -de (singular) or -den (plural).	["werken -> werk -> werkte / werkten", "wonen -> woon -> woonde / woonden"]	0
692	grammar_imperfect_vs_perfect	VTT vs OVT	VTT (Voltooide Tegenwoordige Tijd) uses hebben/zijn + past participle and is used for completed actions in spoken Dutch. OVT (Onvoltooide Verleden Tijd) uses simple past endings (-te/-de) and is common in written texts and stories.	["Ik heb gewerkt (VTT)", "Ik werkte (OVT)"]	0
693	grammar_modal_verbs	Modal Verb Sentence Structure	In Dutch sentences with a modal verb (kunnen, mogen, moeten, willen), the modal verb goes in second position and is conjugated according to the subject. The main action verb moves to the very end of the sentence in its infinitive form.	["Ik wil een appel eten.", "Hij moet nu gaan.", "Mag ik hier zitten?"]	0
694	grammar_negation	Negation: Niet vs. Geen	Use 'geen' to negate nouns preceded by 'een' or no article at all (indefinite nouns). Use 'niet' for verbs, adjectives, adverbs, possessives, and definite nouns (preceded by 'de' or 'het').	["Ik heb geen boek. (I have no book.)", "Het boek is niet goed. (The book is not good.)", "Ik werk niet. (I do not work.)"]	0
695	grammar_object_pronouns	Dutch Object Pronouns (Object voornaamwoorden)	Object pronouns replace nouns that serve as direct or indirect objects in a sentence. Subject pronouns (ik, jij, hij, zij, wij) perform the action, while object pronouns (mij/me, jou/je, hem, haar, ons, jullie, hen) receive the action.	["Ik zie mij / me. (I see me.)", "Hij helpt haar. (He helps her.)", "Zij bellen ons. (They call us.)"]	0
696	grammar_om_te	Using 'om... te' + Infinitive	In Dutch, 'om... te' expresses purpose or goal ('in order to'). 'om' introduces the reason clause, and 'te' goes directly before the full infinitive verb at the end of that clause.	["Ik studeer om Nederlands te spreken.", "Zij eet een appel om gezond te blijven."]	0
697	grammar_past_tense_zijn	Present Perfect with Zijn	In Dutch, verbs indicating movement towards a destination (like gaan, komen) as well as the verb zijn (geweest) take 'zijn' instead of 'hebben' as their auxiliary verb in the present perfect tense.	["Ik ben naar huis gegaan.", "Zij is laat gekomen.", "Wij zijn in Amsterdam geweest."]	0
698	grammar_perfect_tense	Forming the Past Participle	Most regular Dutch verbs form the past participle by adding the prefix ge- to the verb stem and an ending (-t or -d).	["gewerkt (worked)", "gekookt (cooked)"]	0
699	grammar_perfect_tense	Hebben vs Zijn	Use hebben for general activities and actions. Use zijn for movement verbs involving a change of place or direction.	["Ik heb gewerkt (I worked)", "Ik ben gegaan (I went)"]	1
700	grammar_perfect_tense	Word Order	The auxiliary verb (hebben/zijn) stands in second position, and the past participle (ge-verb) goes to the end of the clause.	["Ik heb gisteren gewerkt (I worked yesterday)"]	2
701	grammar_plural	Plural Nouns (-en vs -s)	Most standard Dutch nouns form their plural by adding -en. Nouns ending in unstressed syllables like -el, -em, -en, -er, or long vowels usually take -s.	["boek -> boeken", "hond -> honden", "tafel -> tafels", "sleutel -> sleutels"]	0
702	grammar_possessive_pronouns	Possessive Pronouns Overview	Possessive pronouns indicate ownership. In Dutch: mijn (my), jouw (your), zijn (his), haar (her), jullie (your pl.), hun (their).	["Dit is mijn boek.", "Dat is jouw fiets.", "Dit is zijn hond.", "Dat is haar kat."]	0
703	grammar_possessive_pronouns	Ons vs. Onze	Dutch has two words for 'our'. Use 'ons' with 'het' words and 'onze' with 'de' words (and all plural nouns).	["ons huis (het huis)", "onze auto (de auto)"]	1
704	grammar_prepositions_place	Voorzetsels van Plaats (Prepositions of Place)	Prepositions of place describe where an object or person is located relative to something else. Common basic Dutch prepositions include: in (inside), op (on top), onder (under), boven (above), voor (in front of), achter (behind), naast (next to), and tussen (between).	["De sleutel zit in de tas.", "De kat zit onder de stoel.", "Ik sta naast mijn vriend."]	0
705	grammar_prepositions_time	Time Prepositions in Dutch	Different time units require specific prepositions: 'om' for exact times, 'op' for days of the week and dates, 'in' for months, years, and seasons, 'van...tot' for ranges, and 'over' for relative future times.	["om vier uur (at four o'clock)", "op dinsdag (on Tuesday)", "in de zomer (in summer)", "van acht tot vijf (from eight to five)", "over tien minuten (in ten minutes)"]	0
706	grammar_present_tense	Present Tense Conjugation	To conjugate regular Dutch verbs in the present tense: start with the stem (infinitive minus -en). For 'ik', use the stem. For 'jij', 'hij', 'zij' (singular), add -t to the stem. For plural pronouns ('wij', 'jullie', 'zij'), use the full infinitive.	["Ik werk (I work)", "Jij werkt (You work)", "Wij werken (We work)"]	0
707	grammar_questions	Inversion in Questions	To turn a statement into a question, swap the subject and the verb (Subject + Verb -> Verb + Subject).	["Hij woont hier -> Woont hij hier?", "Zij drinkt koffie -> Drinkt zij koffie?"]	0
708	grammar_questions	Dropping -t with 'jij'	When 'jij' or 'je' follows the verb directly in a question, the verb loses its '-t' ending.	["Jij werkt -> Werk jij?", "Jij leest -> Lees jij?"]	1
709	grammar_reflexive_verbs	Reflexive Pronouns (Wederkerige voornaamwoorden)	Reflexive verbs require a reflexive pronoun that matches the subject of the sentence.	["ik -> me", "jij / u -> je / u", "hij / zij / het -> zich", "wij -> ons", "jullie -> je", "zij (plural) -> zich"]	0
710	grammar_relative_clauses	Relative Pronouns: Die vs Dat	Use 'die' for DE-words and all plurals. Use 'dat' for HET-words. In relative clauses, verbs move to the end of the clause.	["De man die hier woont.", "Het boek dat ik lees.", "De kinderen die spelen."]	0
711	grammar_separable_verbs	Separable Verbs (Scheidbare werkwoorden)	In standard main clauses, Dutch separable verbs split: the conjugated main verb stays in position 2, while the prefix moves to the very end of the clause.	["Ik bel mijn moeder op.", "Wij nemen de hond mee.", "Hij wast de borden af."]	0
712	grammar_want_omdat	Want vs Omdat word order	'Want' is a coordinating conjunction, which means it joins two main clauses together. The word order in the second clause remains standard: Subject + Verb + Rest. 'Omdat' is a subordinating conjunction, which introduces a subordinate clause. This kicks the working verb to the very end of the clause.	["Ik werk niet want ik ben ziek. (I do not work because I am sick.)", "Ik werk niet omdat ik ziek ben. (I do not work because I am sick.)"]	0
713	grammar_word_order	Subject-Verb-Object (SVO)	In standard Dutch main clauses, the sentence structure follows Subject + Verb + Object. The conjugated verb is strictly placed in second position.	["Ik drink koffie.", "Hij leest een boek.", "Wij maken een foto."]	0
714	grammar_zijn_hebben	Irregular Verbs: Zijn and Hebben	In Dutch, 'zijn' (to be) and 'hebben' (to have) are irregular verbs in the present tense. Singular forms change with each pronoun, while all plural pronouns (wij, jullie, zij) use the standard infinitive verb.	["Ik ben / Ik heb", "Jij bent / Jij hebt", "Hij/Zij/Het is / Hij/Zij/Het heeft", "Wij zijn / Wij hebben"]	0
715	hotel_checkin	Polite Pronoun 'U'	In Dutch, 'u' is used as a polite form of 'you' when addressing strangers, older people, or in formal professional services like hotels. 'Je' or 'jij' is used for friends and family.	["Heeft u een reservering?", "Ik wens u een heel fijn verblijf!"]	0
716	hotel_checkin	Inversion in Questions	To make a question in Dutch, the verb moves to the first position, and the subject follows right after. This is called inversion.	["Heeft u een auto bij u?", "Moet ik het pasje tegen de deur houden?"]	1
717	hotel_checkin	Compound Nouns	Dutch frequently joins nouns together to form a single compound word, unlike English which usually keeps them separate.	["kamersleutel (kamer + sleutel)", "parkeergarage (parkeren + garage)", "identiteitsbewijs (identiteit + s + bewijs)"]	2
718	intro_name_origin	The verb 'zijn' (to be)	In Dutch, 'zijn' is used to talk about your name, age, or nationality.	["Ik ben Sanne.", "Ik ben dertig jaar oud."]	0
719	intro_name_origin	Question Words: Waar and Hoe	'Waar' means 'where' and 'hoe' means 'how'. Use them at the start of a question.	["Waar kom je vandaan?", "Hoe heet jij?"]	1
720	making_appointment	Polite requests with 'graag' and 'willen'	In Dutch, 'ik wil graag' (I would like to) is a polite way to make a request or schedule something.	["Ik wil graag een afspraak maken.", "Ik zou graag wat informatie willen."]	0
721	making_appointment	Time Expressions with 'om' and days of the week	Specific times use 'om' (at) and days of the week do not need a preposition when specifying a day for an appointment.	["Dinsdag om tien uur.", "Donderdagmiddag om drie uur."]	1
722	phone_call_basic	Answering the Phone in Dutch	When answering the phone in Dutch, people state their name using 'Met [Name]' or include their company name.	["Met Daan.", "Goedemorgen, kantoor Jansen. Met Daan."]	0
723	phone_call_basic	Asking to Speak to Someone	To ask for someone on the phone, use 'Ik wil graag [Name] spreken' or ask if they are present using 'Is [Name] aanwezig?'.	["Ik zou graag Thomas willen spreken.", "Is Thomas aanwezig vandaag?"]	1
724	supermarket_help	Asking where items are: Waar staat / Waar ligt?	In Dutch, standing objects (bottles, boxes, upright items) use 'staan' ('Waar staat de melk?'), while lying objects (flat packages, cheese, fruit) often use 'liggen' ('Waar ligt de kaas?').	["Waar staat het brood?", "Waar ligt de kaas?"]	0
725	supermarket_help	Paying by card with 'pinnen'	'Pinnen' is a very common Dutch verb meaning to pay electronically using a debit card.	["Kan ik pinnen?", "U kunt hier pinnen."]	1
726	train_ticket	Modal Verb 'Willen' (To want)	In Dutch, 'willen' is used to express desire. In questions, the verb comes first when asking 'Wilt u...?' (Do you want...?).	["Ik wil een treinkaartje kopen.", "Wilt u een enkele reis?"]	0
727	train_ticket	Asking Questions with Question Words	Questions starting with question words (waar, hoe laat, hoeveel) put the verb directly after the question word.	["Waar wilt u naartoe?", "Hoe laat is het nu?"]	1
728	vocab_alphabet_pronunciation	Articles in Dutch (De vs. Het)	Dutch nouns use either 'de' or 'het' as the definite article ('the'). Most nouns take 'de', while neuter nouns take 'het'.	["de appel (the apple)", "het boek (the book)", "de fiets (the bicycle)", "het ei (the egg)"]	0
729	vocab_at_restaurant	Using 'graag' when ordering	In Dutch restaurants, use 'graag' (gladly/please) with verbs to make polite requests.	["Ik wil graag bestellen. (I would like to order.)", "Mag ik de rekening, alstublieft? (May I have the bill, please?)"]	0
730	vocab_banking_money	Using 'Pinnen' in the Netherlands	In the Netherlands, 'pinnen' is a very common verb meaning to pay with a debit card using a PIN code.	["Kan ik hier pinnen?", "Ik wil graag pinnen."]	0
731	vocab_banking_money	Plural of Amounts	When stating amounts of money, 'euro' stays singular after numbers.	["tien euro", "twintig euro"]	1
732	vocab_body_parts	Possessive Pronoun 'mijn'	'Mijn' means 'my' in English and stays the same regardless of gender or singular/plural in basic sentences.	["mijn hoofd (my head)", "mijn hand (my hand)"]	0
733	vocab_celebrations	Capitalization in Dutch Nouns	In Dutch, common nouns such as 'verjaardag' or 'feest' are written in lowercase, unlike standard German.	["het feest", "mijn verjaardag"]	0
734	vocab_city_navigation	Articles with city nouns (de vs het)	In Dutch, nouns take 'de' or 'het'. 'De straat', 'de brug', and 'de tunnel' take 'de', while 'het plein' and 'het kruispunt' take 'het'.	["de straat", "het plein", "het kruispunt"]	0
735	vocab_city_navigation	Separable verb: oversteken	The verb 'oversteken' (to cross) splits in present tense main clauses. 'Over' moves to the end of the clause.	["Wij steken de straat over."]	1
736	vocab_clothing	Nouns and Gender (De vs. Het)	In Dutch, most clothing items use 'de', except for English loanwords like 'het shirt'.	["de broek (the pants)", "de jas (the coat)", "het shirt (the shirt)"]	0
737	vocab_clothing_shopping	Using 'te' before adjectives	In Dutch, 'te' placed before an adjective means 'too', as in 'te groot' (too big) or 'te klein' (too small).	["Deze broek is te klein. (These trousers are too small.)", "Het hemd is te groot. (The shirt is too big.)"]	0
738	vocab_cooking	Nouns with 'de' and 'het'	In Dutch, every noun is either a 'de' word or a 'het' word. Kitchen utensils vary: 'de pan', 'de pot', 'de vork', 'de lepel', but 'het mes', 'het bord', 'het recept'.	["de pan (the pan)", "het mes (the knife)"]	0
739	vocab_digital_daily	Separable Verbs: opladen	The verb 'opladen' is a separable verb. In standard present tense sentences, 'laad' comes earlier and 'op' goes to the end of the clause.	["Ik laad mijn telefoon op.", "De batterij laadt nu op."]	0
740	vocab_family	Possessive Pronoun: mijn	Use 'mijn' to mean 'my' before any family member noun.	["Mijn vader", "Mijn moeder", "Mijn zus"]	0
741	vocab_food_drinks	Basic Food Verbs: Eten & Drinken	In Dutch, 'eten' means 'to eat' and 'drinken' means 'to drink'. For the subject 'ik' (I), use 'eet' and 'drink'.	["Ik eet brood. (I eat bread.)", "Ik drink water. (I drink water.)"]	0
742	vocab_greetings	Time-based Greetings	In Dutch, standard formal and informal greetings often incorporate the time of day: 'morgen' (morning), 'middag' (afternoon), and 'avond' (evening).	["Goedemorgen! (Good morning!)", "Goedemiddag! (Good afternoon!)", "Goedenavond! (Good evening!)"]	0
743	vocab_health_body	Using 'zijn' vs 'hebben' for illness	Use 'zijn' with adjectives like 'ziek' (Ik ben ziek), but use 'hebben' with nouns like 'pijn' or 'koorts' (Ik heb pijn / Het kind heeft koorts).	["Ik ben ziek.", "Ik heb pijn.", "Hij heeft koorts."]	0
744	vocab_home_rooms	Nouns and Articles (De vs. Het)	In Dutch, nouns take either 'de' or 'het'. Most rooms and furniture items taught today take 'de' (de keuken, de slaapkamer, de woonkamer, de tafel, de stoel), except 'bed', which takes 'het' (het bed).	["de keuken (the kitchen)", "het bed (the bed)"]	0
745	vocab_nature_outdoor	Articles with Nature Nouns	In Dutch, nouns take either 'de' or 'het'. 'De' is used for 'natuur', 'polder', and plural nouns like 'vogels'. 'Het' is used for 'bos', 'strand', and 'duin'.	["de natuur (the nature)", "het bos (the forest)", "het strand (the beach)", "de vogels (the birds)"]	0
746	vocab_polite_expressions	Formal vs. Informal Politeness	In Dutch, politeness depends highly on whether you are using the informal 'je' or formal 'u'. For 'thank you', 'dank je wel' is informal, while 'dank u wel' is formal. Similarly, 'alsjeblieft' contains 'je' and is informal, whereas 'alstublieft' contains 'u' and is formal.	["Dank je wel, mijn vriend. (Thank you, my friend. - Informal)", "Dank u wel, meneer. (Thank you, sir. - Formal)"]	0
747	vocab_school_items	Articles with School Items (De vs Het)	In Dutch, every noun is either a 'de' word or a 'het' word. Learn each noun with its definite article.	["het boek (the book)", "het potlood (the pencil)", "het schrift (the notebook)", "de pen (the pen)", "de tas (the bag)", "de gum (the eraser)"]	0
748	vocab_sports_hobbies	Verbs as Hobbies	In Dutch, activity verbs like 'fietsen' (to cycle), 'zwemmen' (to swim), and 'lezen' (to read) are used in the present tense to express your hobbies.	["Ik fiets elke dag. (I cycle every day.)", "Ik lees een boek. (I read a book.)"]	0
749	vocab_time	Position of Time Expressions	In Dutch sentences, time adverbs like 'vandaag', 'morgen', or 'gisteren' typically follow the main verb in basic sentence structures.	["Ik werk vandaag niet.", "Gisteren was ik thuis."]	0
750	vocab_transport	Articles for Transport Words	Most vehicles in Dutch use 'de' (de trein, de bus, de fiets, de auto, de tram, de metro). One main exception is 'het vliegtuig' (the airplane).	["de trein (the train)", "de fiets (the bicycle)", "het vliegtuig (the airplane)"]	0
751	vocab_transport	Traveling 'by' a vehicle	To express traveling by a transport mode in Dutch, use the preposition 'met' followed by the article and vehicle.	["met de trein (by train)", "met de bus (by bus)"]	1
752	vocab_vegetables_fruit	Definite Articles with Vegetables and Fruits	In Dutch, all the singular fruits and vegetables introduced in this lesson use the definite article 'de' (e.g., de appel, de banaan, de tomaat, de wortel, de ui, de sla, de peer).	["de banaan (the banana)", "de tomaat (the tomato)"]	0
753	vocab_weather	Describing Weather with 'Het is'	In Dutch, weather conditions are often described using 'Het is...' (It is...).	["Het is koud.", "Het is warm.", "Het is bewolkt."]	0
754	vocab_work_office	Nouns with Het and De in the Office	In Dutch, nouns take either 'de' or 'het'. Words like 'kantoor' and 'salaris' are het-words (het kantoor, het salaris), while 'baas', 'collega', 'vergadering', and 'sollicitatie' are de-words (de baas, de vergadering).	["het kantoor (the office)", "de vergadering (the meeting)"]	0
755	weather_chat	Expressing weather with 'Het is' and 'Het regent'	In Dutch, impersonal weather conditions use 'het' as the subject.	["Het is warm vandaag.", "Het regent al de hele ochtend.", "Het waait hard buiten."]	0
756	weather_chat	Inversion in questions and after time/place indicators	When starting a sentence with a time element or location, the verb comes before the subject (verb-second rule).	["Vandaag schijnt de zon.", "Over een halfuur komt er regen.", "Vanmorgen was het erg fris."]	1
\.
