4\nNow let's look at hypotheticals.\n\n32\n00:01:51.124 --> 00:01:54.984\nTalking about fun ideas or plans!\n\n33\n00:01:55.024 --> 00:01:58.384\nThat would be very nice!\n\n34\n00:01:58.404 --> 00:02:01.024\nThat would be very nice!\n\n35\n00:02:01.044 --> 00:02:04.164\nNow let's move to plural words.\n\n36\n00:02:04.184 --> 00:02:08.245\nWe use zouden for plural subjects.\n\n37\n00:02:08.305 --> 00:02:12.165\nWe would like to come along.\n\n38\n00:02:12.185 --> 00:02:14.285\nWe would like to come along.\n\n39\n00:02:14.305 --> 00:02:16.905\nCan you guess the plural form?\n\n40\n00:02:16.965 --> 00:02:20.625\nHow do we say: they would?\n\n41\n00:02:20.685 --> 00:02:24.045\nHere is the full sentence:\n\n42\n00:02:24.105 --> 00:02:30.525\nThey would go to school.\n\n43\n00:02:30.545 --> 00:02:33.546\nThey would go to school.\n\n44\n00:02:33.566 --> 00:02:36.066\nLet me repeat the pattern now.\n\n45\n00:02:36.106 --> 00:02:39.566\nRepeat after me each time!\n\n46\n00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:45.006\nI would ask that.\n\n47\n00:02:45.146 --> 00:02:47.686\nI would ask that.\n\n48\n00:02:47.746 --> 00:02:53.146\nYou would ask that.\n\n49\n00:02:53.206 --> 00:02:56.206\nYou would ask that.\n\n50\n00:02:56.266 --> 00:03:02.107\nWe would ask that.\n\n51\n00:03:02.147 --> 00:03:05.107\nWe would ask that.\n\n52\n00:03:05.187 --> 00:03:10.687\nThey would ask that.\n\n53\n00:03:10.727 --> 00:03:13.667\nThey would ask that.\n\n54\n00:03:13.687 --> 00:03:17.567\nNow let's look at common mistakes.\n\n55\n00:03:17.647 --> 00:03:23.707\nMistake one: using zou for plural.\n\n56\n00:03:23.727 --> 00:03:28.487\nDon't say: Wij zou gaan.\n\n57\n00:03:28.527 --> 00:03:34.088\nThat is wrong because wij is plural!\n\n58\n00:03:34.168 --> 00:03:38.308\nAlways use zouden with wij.\n\n59\n00:03:38.368 --> 00:03:42.268\nWe would go.\n\n60\n00:03:42.288 --> 00:03:44.988\nWe would go.\n\n61\n00:03:45.088 --> 00:03:49.568\nMistake two: wrong word order.\n\n62\n00:03:49.648 --> 00:03:53.548\nDon't say: Ik zou willen koffie.\n\n63\n00:03:53.588 --> 00:03:57.409\nPut the infinitive verb at the end!\n\n64\n00:03:57.469 --> 00:04:01.289\nI would like coffee.\n\n65\n00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:04.189\nI would like coffee.\n\n66\n00:04:04.229 --> 00:04:07.149\nHere is your memory tip:\n\n67\n00:04:07.189 --> 00:04:13.389\nSingular gets zou, plural gets zouden!\n\n68\n00:04:13.489 --> 00:04:17.209\nOther verbs go to the end.\n\n69\n00:04:17.249 --> 00:04:21.109\nTime for our spoken recap!\n\n70\n00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:26.890\nToday we learned: zou and zouden.\n\n71\n00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:31.850\nToday we learned: zou and zouden.\n\n72\n00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:35.350\nUse them to be polite!\n\n73\n00:04:35.390 --> 00:04:37.070\nGoodbye!\n\n74\n00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:37.560\nGoodbye!\n
grammar_dat_clauses	nl	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:05.840\nImagine you are chatting with Dutch friends.\n\n2\n00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.180\nYou want to give your opinion.\n\n3\n00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.720\nOr report what someone else said.\n\n4\n00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.240\nYou need the Dutch word dat.\n\n5\n00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.401\nIn English, dat means that.\n\n6\n00:00:22.421 --> 00:00:24.921\nLaten we beginnen!\n\n7\n00:00:25.041 --> 00:00:27.481\nLet us begin!\n\n8\n00:00:27.521 --> 00:00:31.201\nWhen you use the word dat,\n\n9\n00:00:31.341 --> 00:00:35.181\nsomething special happens to the verb.\n\n10\n00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.641\nThe verb goes to the end!\n\n11\n00:00:38.681 --> 00:00:43.241\nIn English, we say: I think that...\n\n12\n00:00:43.301 --> 00:00:47.621\nIn Dutch, we do the same.\n\n13\n00:00:47.641 --> 00:00:50.902\nLet us start with a simple sentence.\n\n14\n00:00:50.982 --> 00:00:54.422\nHij werkt hard.\n\n15\n00:00:54.482 --> 00:00:57.762\nHe works hard.\n\n16\n00:00:57.802 --> 00:01:02.122\nNow let us add: I think that.\n\n17\n00:01:02.202 --> 00:01:06.422\nIk denk dat hij werkt.\n\n18\n00:01:06.622 --> 00:01:10.542\nI think that he works.\n\n19\n00:01:10.582 --> 00:01:14.142\nNotice where the verb werkt goes.\n\n20\n00:01:14.222 --> 00:01:18.042\nIt goes to the very end!\n\n21\n00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:21.602\nLet us try another one.\n\n22\n00:01:21.682 --> 00:01:25.663\nZe komt morgen.\n\n23\n00:01:25.723 --> 00:01:28.983\nShe comes tomorrow.\n\n24\n00:01:29.003 --> 00:01:33.403\nNow add: She says that.\n\n25\n00:01:33.443 --> 00:01:37.763\nZe zegt dat ze komt.\n\n26\n00:01:37.803 --> 00:01:41.643\nShe says that she comes.\n\n27\n00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:45.063\nLet us make it longer!\n\n28\n00:01:45.123 --> 00:01:49.983\nZe zegt dat ze morgen komt.\n\n29\n00:01:50.003 --> 00:01:54.003\nShe says that she comes tomorrow.\n\n30\n00:01:54.023 --> 00:01:57.504\nCan you guess the next sentence?\n\n31\n00:01:57.564 --> 00:02:01.564\nHow do you say: I think that...\n\n32\n00:02:01.584 --> 00:02:04.684\n...he is at home?\n\n33\n00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:09.164\nFirst, think of: He is home.\n\n34\n00:02:09.224 --> 00:02:12.444\nHij is thuis.\n\n35\n00:02:12.484 --> 00:02:15.264\nHe is home.\n\n36\n00:02:15.284 --> 00:02:19.484\nNow add: Ik denk dat...\n\n37\n00:02:19.504 --> 00:02:23.984\nThe verb is moves to the end!\n\n38\n00:02:24.064 --> 00:02:28.544\nIk denk dat hij thuis is.\n\n39\n00:02:28.684 --> 00:02:31.485\nI think that he is home.\n\n40\n00:02:31.505 --> 00:02:33.185\nGreat job!\n\n41\n00:02:33.225 --> 00:02:36.525\nNow let us practice opinions.\n\n42\n00:02:36.545 --> 00:02:41.065\nThe phrase ik vind dat means...\n\n43\n00:02:41.085 --> 00:02:45.925\n...I find that or I think that.\n\n44\n00:02:45.985 --> 00:02:49.385\nHet is koud vandaag.\n\n45\n00:02:49.445 --> 00:02:51.585\nIt is cold today.\n\n46\n00:02:51.605 --> 00:02:55.845\nNow add: Ik vind dat...\n\n47\n00:02:55.885 --> 00:02:59.825\nIk vind dat het koud is.\n\n48\n00:03:00.005 --> 00:03:02.766\nI think that it is cold.\n\n49\n00:03:02.846 --> 00:03:07.106\nSee how is jumped to the end?\n\n50\n00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:09.446\nPattern drill time!\n\n51\n00:03:09.486 --> 00:03:12.766\nLet us repeat this structure together.\n\n52\n00:03:12.826 --> 00:03:16.706\nIk zie dat de man slaapt.\n\n53\n00:03:16.846 --> 00:03:20.486\nI see that the man sleeps.\n\n54\n00:03:20.526 --> 00:03:24.546\nWij horen dat de muziek speelt.\n\n55\n00:03:24.566 --> 00:03:27.686\nWe hear that the music plays.\n\n56\n00:03:27.746 --> 00:03:31.166\nHij weet dat het laat is.\n\n57\n00:03:31.206 --> 00:03:34.106\nHe knows that it is late.\n\n58\n00:03:34.146 --> 00:03:36.107\nHow would you say:\n\n59\n00:03:36.267 --> 00:03:40.107\nI know that she reads?\n\n60\n00:03:40.147 --> 00:03:41.767\nTry it in your head!\n\n61\n00:03:41.827 --> 00:03:43.627\nHere is the answer:\n\n62\n00:03:43.687 --> 00:03:46.987\nIk weet dat ze leest.\n\n63\n00:03:47.127 --> 00:03:50.147\nI know that she reads.\n\n64\n00:03:50.187 --> 00:03:53.167\nNow time for the Error Clinic!\n\n65\n00:03:53.227 --> 00:03:55.847\nLet us look at common mistakes.\n\n66\n00:03:55.867 --> 00:04:00.427\nMistake number one: keeping English word order.\n\n67\n00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:01.727\nDon't say:\n\n68\n00:04:01.787 --> 00:04:04.927\nIk denk dat hij is hier.\n\n69\n00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:07.227\nThat is wrong in Dutch!\n\n70\n00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:12.108\nWhy? Because is is in second position.\n\n71\n00:04:12.128 --> 00:04:15.488\nThe verb must go to the end.\n\n72\n00:04:15.528 --> 00:04:16.728\nCorrect:\n\n73\n00:04:16.788 --> 00:04:20.128\nIk denk dat hij hier is.\n\n74\n00:04:20.248 --> 00:04:23.208\nI think that he is here.\n\n75\n00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:28.268\nMistake number two: forgetting dat.\n\n76\n00:04:28.328 --> 00:04:30.508\nDon't say:\n\n77\n00:04:30.568 --> 00:04:35.708\nZe zegt ze komt morgen.\n\n78\n00:04:35.748 --> 00:04:39.908\nYou need the connector dat!\n\n79\n00:04:39.948 --> 00:04:41.748\nCorrect:\n\n80\n00:04:42.329 --> 00:04:48.869\nZe zegt dat ze morgen komt.\n\n81\n00:04:48.929 --> 00:04:53.269\nShe says that she comes tomorrow.\n\n82\n00:04:53.309 --> 00:04:56.209\nTime for our memory anchor!\n\n83\n00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:59.309\nRemember this simple rule:\n\n84\n00:04:59.489 --> 00:05:04.109\nAfter dat, push the verb back!\n\n85\n00:05:04.149 --> 00:05:08.169\nDat sends the verb to the end.\n\n86\n00:05:08.229 --> 00:05:10.749\nVandaag leerden we:\n\n87\n00:05:10.809 --> 00:05:13.369\nToday we learned:\n\n88\n00:05:13.409 --> 00:05:20.790\nDat verplaatst het werkwoord naar achteren.\n\n89\n00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.690\nDat moves the verb to the end.\n\n90\n00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.950\nGoed gedaan en tot ziens!\n\n91\n00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.290\nWell done and see you next time!\n
grammar_dat_clauses	en	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:05.840\nImagine you are chatting with Dutch friends.\n\n2\n00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.180\nYou want to give your opinion.\n\n3\n00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.720\nOr report what someone else said.\n\n4\n00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:17.240\nYou need the Dutch word dat.\n\n5\n00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:22.401\nIn English, dat means that.\n\n6\n00:00:22.421 --> 00:00:24.921\nLet us begin!\n\n7\n00:00:25.041 --> 00:00:27.481\nLet us begin!\n\n8\n00:00:27.521 --> 00:00:31.201\nWhen you use the word dat,\n\n9\n00:00:31.341 --> 00:00:35.181\nsomething special happens to the verb.\n\n10\n00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:38.641\nThe verb goes to the end!\n\n11\n00:00:38.681 --> 00:00:43.241\nIn English, we say: I think that...\n\n12\n00:00:43.301 --> 00:00:47.621\nIn Dutch, we do the same.\n\n13\n00:00:47.641 --> 00:00:50.902\nLet us start with a simple sentence.\n\n14\n00:00:50.982 --> 00:00:54.422\nHe works hard.\n\n15\n00:00:54.482 --> 00:00:57.762\nHe works hard.\n\n16\n00:00:57.802 --> 00:01:02.122\nNow let us add: I think that.\n\n17\n00:01:02.202 --> 00:01:06.422\nI think that he works.\n\n18\n00:01:06.622 --> 00:01:10.542\nI think that he works.\n\n19\n00:01:10.582 --> 00:01:14.142\nNotice where the verb werkt goes.\n\n20\n00:01:14.222 --> 00:01:18.042\nIt goes to the very end!\n\n21\n00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:21.602\nLet us try another one.\n\n22\n00:01:21.682 --> 00:01:25.663\nShe comes tomorrow.\n\n23\n00:01:25.723 --> 00:01:28.983\nShe comes tomorrow.\n\n24\n00:01:29.003 --> 00:01:33.403\nNow add: She says that.\n\n25\n00:01:33.443 --> 00:01:37.763\nShe says that she comes.\n\n26\n00:01:37.803 --> 00:01:41.643\nShe says that she comes.\n\n27\n00:01:41.683 --> 00:01:45.063\nLet us make it longer!\n\n28\n00:01:45.123 --> 00:01:49.983\nShe says that she comes tomorrow.\n\n29\n00:01:50.003 --> 00:01:54.003\nShe says that she comes tomorrow.\n\n30\n00:01:54.023 --> 00:01:57.504\nCan you guess the next sentence?\n\n31\n00:01:57.564 --> 00:02:01.564\nHow do you say: I think that...\n\n32\n00:02:01.584 --> 00:02:04.684\n...he is at home?\n\n33\n00:02:04.724 --> 00:02:09.164\nFirst, think of: He is home.\n\n34\n00:02:09.224 --> 00:02:12.444\nHe is home.\n\n35\n00:02:12.484 --> 00:02:15.264\nHe is home.\n\n36\n00:02:15.284 --> 00:02:19.484\nNow add: Ik denk dat...\n\n37\n00:02:19.504 --> 00:02:23.984\nThe verb is moves to the end!\n\n38\n00:02:24.064 --> 00:02:28.544\nI think that he is home.\n\n39\n00:02:28.684 --> 00:02:31.485\nI think that he is home.\n\n40\n00:02:31.505 --> 00:02:33.185\nGreat job!\n\n41\n00:02:33.225 --> 00:02:36.525\nNow let us practice opinions.\n\n42\n00:02:36.545 --> 00:02:41.065\nThe phrase ik vind dat means...\n\n43\n00:02:41.085 --> 00:02:45.925\n...I find that or I think that.\n\n44\n00:02:45.985 --> 00:02:49.385\nIt is cold today.\n\n45\n00:02:49.445 --> 00:02:51.585\nIt is cold today.\n\n46\n00:02:51.605 --> 00:02:55.845\nNow add: Ik vind dat...\n\n47\n00:02:55.885 --> 00:02:59.825\nI think that it is cold.\n\n48\n00:03:00.005 --> 00:03:02.766\nI think that it is cold.\n\n49\n00:03:02.846 --> 00:03:07.106\nSee how is jumped to the end?\n\n50\n00:03:07.186 --> 00:03:09.446\nPattern drill time!\n\n51\n00:03:09.486 --> 00:03:12.766\nLet us repeat this structure together.\n\n52\n00:03:12.826 --> 00:03:16.706\nI see that the man sleeps.\n\n53\n00:03:16.846 --> 00:03:20.486\nI see that the man sleeps.\n\n54\n00:03:20.526 --> 00:03:24.546\nWe hear that the music plays.\n\n55\n00:03:24.566 --> 00:03:27.686\nWe hear that the music plays.\n\n56\n00:03:27.746 --> 00:03:31.166\nHe knows that it is late.\n\n57\n00:03:31.206 --> 00:03:34.106\nHe knows that it is late.\n\n58\n00:03:34.146 --> 00:03:36.107\nHow would you say:\n\n59\n00:03:36.267 --> 00:03:40.107\nI know that she reads?\n\n60\n00:03:40.147 --> 00:03:41.767\nTry it in your head!\n\n61\n00:03:41.827 --> 00:03:43.627\nHere is the answer:\n\n62\n00:03:43.687 --> 00:03:46.987\nI know that she reads.\n\n63\n00:03:47.127 --> 00:03:50.147\nI know that she reads.\n\n64\n00:03:50.187 --> 00:03:53.167\nNow time for the Error Clinic!\n\n65\n00:03:53.227 --> 00:03:55.847\nLet us look at common mistakes.\n\n66\n00:03:55.867 --> 00:04:00.427\nMistake number one: keeping English word order.\n\n67\n00:04:00.467 --> 00:04:01.727\nDon't say:\n\n68\n00:04:01.787 --> 00:04:04.927\nI think that he is here.\n\n69\n00:04:04.967 --> 00:04:07.227\nThat is wrong in Dutch!\n\n70\n00:04:07.247 --> 00:04:12.108\nWhy? Because is is in second position.\n\n71\n00:04:12.128 --> 00:04:15.488\nThe verb must go to the end.\n\n72\n00:04:15.528 --> 00:04:16.728\nCorrect:\n\n73\n00:04:16.788 --> 00:04:20.128\nI think that he is here.\n\n74\n00:04:20.248 --> 00:04:23.208\nI think that he is here.\n\n75\n00:04:23.228 --> 00:04:28.268\nMistake number two: forgetting dat.\n\n76\n00:04:28.328 --> 00:04:30.508\nDon't say:\n\n77\n00:04:30.568 --> 00:04:35.708\nShe says she comes tomorrow.\n\n78\n00:04:35.748 --> 00:04:39.908\nYou need the connector dat!\n\n79\n00:04:39.948 --> 00:04:41.748\nCorrect:\n\n80\n00:04:42.329 --> 00:04:48.869\nShe says that she comes tomorrow.\n\n81\n00:04:48.929 --> 00:04:53.269\nShe says that she comes tomorrow.\n\n82\n00:04:53.309 --> 00:04:56.209\nTime for our memory anchor!\n\n83\n00:04:56.229 --> 00:04:59.309\nRemember this simple rule:\n\n84\n00:04:59.489 --> 00:05:04.109\nAfter dat, push the verb back!\n\n85\n00:05:04.149 --> 00:05:08.169\nDat sends the verb to the end.\n\n86\n00:05:08.229 --> 00:05:10.749\nToday we learned:\n\n87\n00:05:10.809 --> 00:05:13.369\nToday we learned:\n\n88\n00:05:13.409 --> 00:05:20.790\nDat moves the verb to the end.\n\n89\n00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:24.690\nDat moves the verb to the end.\n\n90\n00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.950\nWell done and see you next time!\n\n91\n00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.290\nWell done and see you next time!\n
grammar_demonstratives	nl	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:04.780\nImagine pointing at items in a shop.\n\n2\n00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.000\nYou need to say this or that.\n\n3\n00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.200\nIn Dutch, we have four words.\n\n4\n00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:16.981\nDEZE, DIT, DIE, and DAT.\n\n5\n00:00:17.021 --> 00:00:19.221\nHow do you choose between them?\n\n6\n00:00:19.261 --> 00:00:22.741\nIt depends on DE or HET!\n\n7\n00:00:22.781 --> 00:00:27.081\nFor DE words close, use DEZE.\n\n8\n00:00:27.121 --> 00:00:31.101\nFor HET words close, use DIT.\n\n9\n00:00:31.141 --> 00:00:35.101\nFor DE words far, use DIE.\n\n10\n00:00:35.141 --> 00:00:39.721\nFor HET words far, use DAT.\n\n11\n00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:42.641\nLaten we snel beginnen!\n\n12\n00:00:42.821 --> 00:00:45.342\nLet us start quickly!\n\n13\n00:00:45.402 --> 00:00:49.122\nFirst, let's look at DE words.\n\n14\n00:00:49.222 --> 00:00:52.482\nStoel is a DE word.\n\n15\n00:00:52.562 --> 00:00:55.722\nIk wil deze stoel.\n\n16\n00:00:55.842 --> 00:00:58.742\nI want this chair.\n\n17\n00:00:58.822 --> 00:01:02.782\nDeze stoel is erg mooi.\n\n18\n00:01:02.822 --> 00:01:06.102\nThis chair is very beautiful.\n\n19\n00:01:06.202 --> 00:01:09.322\nNow let's look at HET words.\n\n20\n00:01:09.362 --> 00:01:12.402\nBoek is a HET word.\n\n21\n00:01:12.462 --> 00:01:15.923\nIk wil dit boek.\n\n22\n00:01:16.083 --> 00:01:19.143\nI want this book.\n\n23\n00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:23.583\nDit boek is erg oud.\n\n24\n00:01:23.603 --> 00:01:26.783\nThis book is very old.\n\n25\n00:01:26.823 --> 00:01:29.443\nTime for a quick prompt!\n\n26\n00:01:29.483 --> 00:01:33.323\nHow do you say 'this house'?\n\n27\n00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:38.023\nRemember, 'huis' is a HET word.\n\n28\n00:01:38.043 --> 00:01:42.163\nThe answer is: DIT huis!\n\n29\n00:01:42.183 --> 00:01:46.604\nDit huis is heel fijn.\n\n30\n00:01:46.624 --> 00:01:49.964\nThis house is very nice.\n\n31\n00:01:50.004 --> 00:01:54.524\nGreat job! Now for far objects.\n\n32\n00:01:54.564 --> 00:02:00.004\nFor DE words far away, use DIE.\n\n33\n00:02:00.044 --> 00:02:04.264\nDie man is erg oud.\n\n34\n00:02:04.304 --> 00:02:07.464\nThat man is very old.\n\n35\n00:02:07.504 --> 00:02:11.544\nDie kat is heel lief.\n\n36\n00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:14.065\nThat cat is very sweet.\n\n37\n00:02:14.125 --> 00:02:20.965\nFor HET words far away, use DAT.\n\n38\n00:02:21.005 --> 00:02:27.885\nDat kind speelt nu buiten.\n\n39\n00:02:28.045 --> 00:02:32.585\nThat child plays outside now.\n\n40\n00:02:32.645 --> 00:02:39.725\nDat huis is heel mooi.\n\n41\n00:02:39.765 --> 00:02:43.966\nThat house is very beautiful.\n\n42\n00:02:44.006 --> 00:02:47.606\nCan you guess the next word?\n\n43\n00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:52.006\nHow do you say 'that chair'?\n\n44\n00:02:52.086 --> 00:02:56.906\nStoel is a DE word.\n\n45\n00:02:56.926 --> 00:03:01.686\nThe correct word is DIE!\n\n46\n00:03:01.766 --> 00:03:08.486\nDie stoel is gloednieuw.\n\n47\n00:03:08.546 --> 00:03:12.847\nThat chair is brand new.\n\n48\n00:03:12.887 --> 00:03:17.587\nNow let me show common mistakes.\n\n49\n00:03:17.607 --> 00:03:24.007\nMistake one: saying DEZE BOEK.\n\n50\n00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:31.047\nDeze boek is goed.\n\n51\n00:03:31.087 --> 00:03:34.447\nWhy is this incorrect?\n\n52\n00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:40.148\nBecause 'boek' is a HET word.\n\n53\n00:03:40.188 --> 00:03:45.648\nSo you must say DIT BOEK.\n\n54\n00:03:45.708 --> 00:03:52.108\nDit boek is goed.\n\n55\n00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:55.308\nThis book is good.\n\n56\n00:03:55.328 --> 00:04:00.828\nMistake two: saying DIE HUIS.\n\n57\n00:04:00.888 --> 00:04:04.928\nDie huis is groot.\n\n58\n00:04:04.948 --> 00:04:07.188\nThat is also wrong!\n\n59\n00:04:07.268 --> 00:04:12.429\nHuis uses HET, so use DAT.\n\n60\n00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:16.049\nDat huis is groot.\n\n61\n00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.849\nThat house is big.\n\n62\n00:04:18.889 --> 00:04:22.109\nHere is your simple memory tip!\n\n63\n00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:27.649\nDE words become DEZE and DIE.\n\n64\n00:04:27.709 --> 00:04:32.889\nHET words become DIT and DAT.\n\n65\n00:04:32.929 --> 00:04:36.569\nNotice how the letters match!\n\n66\n00:04:36.629 --> 00:04:42.190\nVandaag leerden we deze en dit.\n\n67\n00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:47.190\nToday we learned deze and dit.\n\n68\n00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.730\nLaten we goed blijven oefenen!\n\n69\n00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.010\nLet us keep practicing well!\n
grammar_demonstratives	en	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.340 --> 00:00:04.780\nImagine pointing at items in a shop.\n\n2\n00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:08.000\nYou need to say this or that.\n\n3\n00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.200\nIn Dutch, we have four words.\n\n4\n00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:16.981\nDEZE, DIT, DIE, and DAT.\n\n5\n00:00:17.021 --> 00:00:19.221\nHow do you choose between them?\n\n6\n00:00:19.261 --> 00:00:22.741\nIt depends on DE or HET!\n\n7\n00:00:22.781 --> 00:00:27.081\nFor DE words close, use DEZE.\n\n8\n00:00:27.121 --> 00:00:31.101\nFor HET words close, use DIT.\n\n9\n00:00:31.141 --> 00:00:35.101\nFor DE words far, use DIE.\n\n10\n00:00:35.141 --> 00:00:39.721\nFor HET words far, use DAT.\n\n11\n00:00:39.761 --> 00:00:42.641\nLet us start quickly!\n\n12\n00:00:42.821 --> 00:00:45.342\nLet us start quickly!\n\n13\n00:00:45.402 --> 00:00:49.122\nFirst, let's look at DE words.\n\n14\n00:00:49.222 --> 00:00:52.482\nStoel is a DE word.\n\n15\n00:00:52.562 --> 00:00:55.722\nI want this chair.\n\n16\n00:00:55.842 --> 00:00:58.742\nI want this chair.\n\n17\n00:00:58.822 --> 00:01:02.782\nThis chair is very beautiful.\n\n18\n00:01:02.822 --> 00:01:06.102\nThis chair is very beautiful.\n\n19\n00:01:06.202 --> 00:01:09.322\nNow let's look at HET words.\n\n20\n00:01:09.362 --> 00:01:12.402\nBoek is a HET word.\n\n21\n00:01:12.462 --> 00:01:15.923\nI want this book.\n\n22\n00:01:16.083 --> 00:01:19.143\nI want this book.\n\n23\n00:01:19.183 --> 00:01:23.583\nThis book is very old.\n\n24\n00:01:23.603 --> 00:01:26.783\nThis book is very old.\n\n25\n00:01:26.823 --> 00:01:29.443\nTime for a quick prompt!\n\n26\n00:01:29.483 --> 00:01:33.323\nHow do you say 'this house'?\n\n27\n00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:38.023\nRemember, 'huis' is a HET word.\n\n28\n00:01:38.043 --> 00:01:42.163\nThe answer is: DIT huis!\n\n29\n00:01:42.183 --> 00:01:46.604\nThis house is very nice.\n\n30\n00:01:46.624 --> 00:01:49.964\nThis house is very nice.\n\n31\n00:01:50.004 --> 00:01:54.524\nGreat job! Now for far objects.\n\n32\n00:01:54.564 --> 00:02:00.004\nFor DE words far away, use DIE.\n\n33\n00:02:00.044 --> 00:02:04.264\nThat man is very old.\n\n34\n00:02:04.304 --> 00:02:07.464\nThat man is very old.\n\n35\n00:02:07.504 --> 00:02:11.544\nThat cat is very sweet.\n\n36\n00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:14.065\nThat cat is very sweet.\n\n37\n00:02:14.125 --> 00:02:20.965\nFor HET words far away, use DAT.\n\n38\n00:02:21.005 --> 00:02:27.885\nThat child plays outside now.\n\n39\n00:02:28.045 --> 00:02:32.585\nThat child plays outside now.\n\n40\n00:02:32.645 --> 00:02:39.725\nThat house is very beautiful.\n\n41\n00:02:39.765 --> 00:02:43.966\nThat house is very beautiful.\n\n42\n00:02:44.006 --> 00:02:47.606\nCan you guess the next word?\n\n43\n00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:52.006\nHow do you say 'that chair'?\n\n44\n00:02:52.086 --> 00:02:56.906\nStoel is a DE word.\n\n45\n00:02:56.926 --> 00:03:01.686\nThe correct word is DIE!\n\n46\n00:03:01.766 --> 00:03:08.486\nThat chair is brand new.\n\n47\n00:03:08.546 --> 00:03:12.847\nThat chair is brand new.\n\n48\n00:03:12.887 --> 00:03:17.587\nNow let me show common mistakes.\n\n49\n00:03:17.607 --> 00:03:24.007\nMistake one: saying DEZE BOEK.\n\n50\n00:03:24.067 --> 00:03:31.047\nThis book is good.\n\n51\n00:03:31.087 --> 00:03:34.447\nWhy is this incorrect?\n\n52\n00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:40.148\nBecause 'boek' is a HET word.\n\n53\n00:03:40.188 --> 00:03:45.648\nSo you must say DIT BOEK.\n\n54\n00:03:45.708 --> 00:03:52.108\nThis book is good.\n\n55\n00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:55.308\nThis book is good.\n\n56\n00:03:55.328 --> 00:04:00.828\nMistake two: saying DIE HUIS.\n\n57\n00:04:00.888 --> 00:04:04.928\nThat house is big.\n\n58\n00:04:04.948 --> 00:04:07.188\nThat is also wrong!\n\n59\n00:04:07.268 --> 00:04:12.429\nHuis uses HET, so use DAT.\n\n60\n00:04:12.489 --> 00:04:16.049\nThat house is big.\n\n61\n00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:18.849\nThat house is big.\n\n62\n00:04:18.889 --> 00:04:22.109\nHere is your simple memory tip!\n\n63\n00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:27.649\nDE words become DEZE and DIE.\n\n64\n00:04:27.709 --> 00:04:32.889\nHET words become DIT and DAT.\n\n65\n00:04:32.929 --> 00:04:36.569\nNotice how the letters match!\n\n66\n00:04:36.629 --> 00:04:42.190\nToday we learned deze and dit.\n\n67\n00:04:42.230 --> 00:04:47.190\nToday we learned deze and dit.\n\n68\n00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.730\nLet us keep practicing well!\n\n69\n00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.010\nLet us keep practicing well!\n
grammar_diminutives	nl	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.500\nImagine you are in a Dutch café.\n\n2\n00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:08.160\nYou want to order a small coffee.\n\n3\n00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:12.321\nYou hear a local order a 'kopje'.\n\n4\n00:00:12.341 --> 00:00:15.301\nWhat does this word mean?\n\n5\n00:00:15.341 --> 00:00:20.161\nToday we learn about making words small.\n\n6\n00:00:20.241 --> 00:00:24.581\nIn Dutch, we call this the diminutive.\n\n7\n00:00:24.641 --> 00:00:28.802\nIt shows something is small or cute.\n\n8\n00:00:29.042 --> 00:00:32.822\nOr it shows cozy warmth!\n\n9\n00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:36.142\nLet's start with a simple rule.\n\n10\n00:00:36.162 --> 00:00:40.922\nWe add endings like '-je' to nouns.\n\n11\n00:00:40.962 --> 00:00:44.543\nHere is a very important tip.\n\n12\n00:00:44.643 --> 00:00:50.383\nEvery small word uses 'het' as 'the'.\n\n13\n00:00:50.483 --> 00:00:53.863\nLet's look at our first example.\n\n14\n00:00:53.923 --> 00:00:58.303\nHet boek ligt op de tafel.\n\n15\n00:00:58.323 --> 00:01:02.084\nThe book lies on the table.\n\n16\n00:01:02.144 --> 00:01:05.684\nLet us make 'boek' smaller.\n\n17\n00:01:05.704 --> 00:01:10.124\nWe add '-je' to make 'boekje'.\n\n18\n00:01:10.184 --> 00:01:14.464\nHet boekje ligt op de tafel.\n\n19\n00:01:14.544 --> 00:01:17.905\nThe small book is on the table.\n\n20\n00:01:17.945 --> 00:01:21.445\nNow let's try another one.\n\n21\n00:01:21.465 --> 00:01:25.045\n'Huis' means house.\n\n22\n00:01:25.105 --> 00:01:29.045\nIt becomes 'huisje'.\n\n23\n00:01:29.105 --> 00:01:34.166\nIk zie een mooi klein huisje.\n\n24\n00:01:34.246 --> 00:01:38.126\nI see a nice small house.\n\n25\n00:01:38.146 --> 00:01:41.926\n'Hond' means dog.\n\n26\n00:01:41.986 --> 00:01:45.946\nIt becomes 'hondje'.\n\n27\n00:01:45.966 --> 00:01:50.727\nDat is een lief klein hondje.\n\n28\n00:01:50.747 --> 00:01:54.607\nThat is a sweet small dog.\n\n29\n00:01:54.667 --> 00:01:57.507\nLet's pause for a moment.\n\n30\n00:01:57.527 --> 00:02:00.467\nCan you guess the next word?\n\n31\n00:02:00.527 --> 00:02:04.647\nHow do we make 'kat' small?\n\n32\n00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:07.928\nThe answer is 'katje'.\n\n33\n00:02:07.988 --> 00:02:12.808\nHet katje speelt in de tuin.\n\n34\n00:02:12.848 --> 00:02:16.228\nThe kitten plays in the garden.\n\n35\n00:02:16.288 --> 00:02:20.808\nSometimes we add '-tje' instead of '-je'.\n\n36\n00:02:20.828 --> 00:02:24.249\nThis happens with words ending in vowels.\n\n37\n00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:29.469\nOr words ending in 'l', 'r', 'n'.\n\n38\n00:02:29.509 --> 00:02:35.069\nFor example, 'tafel' becomes 'tafeltje'.\n\n39\n00:02:35.109 --> 00:02:38.649\nDe vaas staat op het tafeltje.\n\n40\n00:02:38.669 --> 00:02:42.310\nThe vase is on the small table.\n\n41\n00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:46.290\n'Stoel' becomes 'stoeltje'.\n\n42\n00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:50.230\nZit op het kleine stoeltje.\n\n43\n00:02:50.330 --> 00:02:53.290\nSit on the small chair.\n\n44\n00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:56.150\nLet's try another guess.\n\n45\n00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:00.031\nHow do you make 'vogel' small?\n\n46\n00:03:00.051 --> 00:03:03.691\nThe answer is 'vogeltje'.\n\n47\n00:03:03.751 --> 00:03:08.051\nHet vogeltje zingt een mooi lied.\n\n48\n00:03:08.071 --> 00:03:11.151\nThe small bird sings a song.\n\n49\n00:03:11.231 --> 00:03:14.872\nWhat about words ending in 'm'?\n\n50\n00:03:14.912 --> 00:03:18.692\nWe often add '-pje' to these.\n\n51\n00:03:18.732 --> 00:03:23.132\nFor example, 'boom' means tree.\n\n52\n00:03:23.192 --> 00:03:25.912\nIt becomes 'boompje'.\n\n53\n00:03:25.992 --> 00:03:29.432\nEr groeit een klein boompje hier.\n\n54\n00:03:29.512 --> 00:03:32.233\nA small tree grows here.\n\n55\n00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:35.293\n'Bloem' means flower.\n\n56\n00:03:35.353 --> 00:03:38.093\nIt becomes 'bloempje'.\n\n57\n00:03:38.173 --> 00:03:41.313\nIk geef jou een mooi bloempje.\n\n58\n00:03:41.453 --> 00:03:43.953\nI give you a small flower.\n\n59\n00:03:43.973 --> 00:03:46.833\nCan you guess for 'raam'?\n\n60\n00:03:46.853 --> 00:03:49.914\nThe answer is 'raampje'.\n\n61\n00:03:49.934 --> 00:03:53.294\nKijk door het kleine raampje.\n\n62\n00:03:53.334 --> 00:03:56.014\nLook through the small window.\n\n63\n00:03:56.054 --> 00:03:58.834\nNow let's look at some mistakes.\n\n64\n00:03:58.874 --> 00:04:01.394\nImagine someone saying this:\n\n65\n00:04:01.434 --> 00:04:04.634\nIk zie de boompje daar staan.\n\n66\n00:04:04.714 --> 00:04:07.215\nI see the small tree there.\n\n67\n00:04:07.235 --> 00:04:08.975\nWhat is wrong here?\n\n68\n00:04:08.995 --> 00:04:11.695\nRemember our golden rule.\n\n69\n00:04:11.775 --> 00:04:16.655\nEvery diminutive must use 'het', not 'de'.\n\n70\n00:04:16.695 --> 00:04:18.575\nSo we must say:\n\n71\n00:04:18.635 --> 00:04:22.135\nIk zie het boompje daar staan.\n\n72\n00:04:22.256 --> 00:04:24.596\nI see the small tree there.\n\n73\n00:04:24.656 --> 00:04:26.816\nHere is another mistake:\n\n74\n00:04:26.856 --> 00:04:29.756\nDat is een klein boomje.\n\n75\n00:04:29.796 --> 00:04:31.856\nThat is a small tree.\n\n76\n00:04:31.916 --> 00:04:33.736\nWhat went wrong here?\n\n77\n00:04:33.776 --> 00:04:38.076\nFor words ending in 'm', use '-pje'.\n\n78\n00:04:38.116 --> 00:04:40.737\nNot just '-je'.\n\n79\n00:04:40.777 --> 00:04:43.997\nSo 'boompje' is correct!\n\n80\n00:04:44.037 --> 00:04:46.437\nLet's do a quick pattern drill.\n\n81\n00:04:46.477 --> 00:04:49.397\nRepeat these short forms after me.\n\n82\n00:04:49.437 --> 00:04:52.717\nHet kleine kopje thee.\n\n83\n00:04:52.737 --> 00:04:54.477\nThe small cup of tea.\n\n84\n00:04:54.537 --> 00:04:58.278\nHet kleine groene blaadje.\n\n85\n00:04:58.298 --> 00:05:00.238\nThe small green leaf.\n\n86\n00:05:00.298 --> 00:05:03.938\nHet schattige kleine huisje.\n\n87\n00:05:03.958 --> 00:05:06.338\nThe cute small house.\n\n88\n00:05:06.378 --> 00:05:09.998\nHet zachte warme dekentje.\n\n89\n00:05:10.018 --> 00:05:13.139\nThe soft warm small blanket.\n\n90\n00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:15.479\nRemember this memory anchor:\n\n91\n00:05:15.519 --> 00:05:19.399\nDiminutives always make things cozy.\n\n92\n00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:22.139\nAnd they always take 'het'!\n\n93\n00:05:22.159 --> 00:05:26.039\nToday we learned how to use diminutives.\n\n94\n00:05:26.099 --> 00:05:30.140\nVandaag leerden we de verkleinwoorden.\n\n95\n00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.860\nToday we learned the diminutives.\n\n96\n00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.720\nKeep practicing these small, cozy words!\n\n97\n00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:38.240\nSee you next time!\n
grammar_diminutives	en	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:05.500\nImagine you are in a Dutch café.\n\n2\n00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:09.160\nYou want to order a small coffee.\n\n3\n00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:13.321\nYou hear a local order a 'kopje'.\n\n4\n00:00:13.341 --> 00:00:16.301\nWhat does this word mean?\n\n5\n00:00:16.341 --> 00:00:21.161\nToday we learn about making words small.\n\n6\n00:00:21.241 --> 00:00:25.581\nIn Dutch, we call this the diminutive.\n\n7\n00:00:25.641 --> 00:00:29.802\nIt shows something is small or cute.\n\n8\n00:00:30.042 --> 00:00:33.822\nOr it shows cozy warmth!\n\n9\n00:00:33.862 --> 00:00:37.142\nLet's start with a simple rule.\n\n10\n00:00:37.162 --> 00:00:41.922\nWe add endings like '-je' to nouns.\n\n11\n00:00:41.962 --> 00:00:45.543\nHere is a very important tip.\n\n12\n00:00:45.643 --> 00:00:51.383\nEvery small word uses 'het' as 'the'.\n\n13\n00:00:51.483 --> 00:00:54.863\nLet's look at our first example.\n\n14\n00:00:54.923 --> 00:00:59.303\nThe book lies on the table.\n\n15\n00:00:59.323 --> 00:01:03.084\nThe book lies on the table.\n\n16\n00:01:03.144 --> 00:01:06.684\nLet us make 'boek' smaller.\n\n17\n00:01:06.704 --> 00:01:11.124\nWe add '-je' to make 'boekje'.\n\n18\n00:01:11.184 --> 00:01:15.464\nThe small book is on the table.\n\n19\n00:01:15.544 --> 00:01:18.905\nThe small book is on the table.\n\n20\n00:01:18.945 --> 00:01:22.445\nNow let's try another one.\n\n21\n00:01:22.465 --> 00:01:26.045\n'Huis' means house.\n\n22\n00:01:26.105 --> 00:01:30.045\nIt becomes 'huisje'.\n\n23\n00:01:30.105 --> 00:01:35.166\nI see a nice small house.\n\n24\n00:01:35.246 --> 00:01:39.126\nI see a nice small house.\n\n25\n00:01:39.146 --> 00:01:42.926\n'Hond' means dog.\n\n26\n00:01:42.986 --> 00:01:46.946\nIt becomes 'hondje'.\n\n27\n00:01:46.966 --> 00:01:51.727\nThat is a sweet small dog.\n\n28\n00:01:51.747 --> 00:01:55.607\nThat is a sweet small dog.\n\n29\n00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:58.507\nLet's pause for a moment.\n\n30\n00:01:58.527 --> 00:02:01.467\nCan you guess the next word?\n\n31\n00:02:01.527 --> 00:02:05.647\nHow do we make 'kat' small?\n\n32\n00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:08.928\nThe answer is 'katje'.\n\n33\n00:02:08.988 --> 00:02:13.808\nThe kitten plays in the garden.\n\n34\n00:02:13.848 --> 00:02:17.228\nThe kitten plays in the garden.\n\n35\n00:02:17.288 --> 00:02:21.808\nSometimes we add '-tje' instead of '-je'.\n\n36\n00:02:21.828 --> 00:02:25.249\nThis happens with words ending in vowels.\n\n37\n00:02:25.349 --> 00:02:30.469\nOr words ending in 'l', 'r', 'n'.\n\n38\n00:02:30.509 --> 00:02:36.069\nFor example, 'tafel' becomes 'tafeltje'.\n\n39\n00:02:36.109 --> 00:02:39.649\nThe vase is on the small table.\n\n40\n00:02:39.669 --> 00:02:43.310\nThe vase is on the small table.\n\n41\n00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:47.290\n'Stoel' becomes 'stoeltje'.\n\n42\n00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:51.230\nSit on the small chair.\n\n43\n00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:54.290\nSit on the small chair.\n\n44\n00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:57.150\nLet's try another guess.\n\n45\n00:02:57.190 --> 00:03:01.031\nHow do you make 'vogel' small?\n\n46\n00:03:01.051 --> 00:03:04.691\nThe answer is 'vogeltje'.\n\n47\n00:03:04.751 --> 00:03:09.051\nThe small bird sings a song.\n\n48\n00:03:09.071 --> 00:03:12.151\nThe small bird sings a song.\n\n49\n00:03:12.231 --> 00:03:15.872\nWhat about words ending in 'm'?\n\n50\n00:03:15.912 --> 00:03:19.692\nWe often add '-pje' to these.\n\n51\n00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:24.132\nFor example, 'boom' means tree.\n\n52\n00:03:24.192 --> 00:03:26.912\nIt becomes 'boompje'.\n\n53\n00:03:26.992 --> 00:03:30.432\nA small tree grows here.\n\n54\n00:03:30.512 --> 00:03:33.233\nA small tree grows here.\n\n55\n00:03:33.253 --> 00:03:36.293\n'Bloem' means flower.\n\n56\n00:03:36.353 --> 00:03:39.093\nIt becomes 'bloempje'.\n\n57\n00:03:39.173 --> 00:03:42.313\nI give you a small flower.\n\n58\n00:03:42.453 --> 00:03:44.953\nI give you a small flower.\n\n59\n00:03:44.973 --> 00:03:47.833\nCan you guess for 'raam'?\n\n60\n00:03:47.853 --> 00:03:50.914\nThe answer is 'raampje'.\n\n61\n00:03:50.934 --> 00:03:54.294\nLook through the small window.\n\n62\n00:03:54.334 --> 00:03:57.014\nLook through the small window.\n\n63\n00:03:57.054 --> 00:03:59.834\nNow let's look at some mistakes.\n\n64\n00:03:59.874 --> 00:04:02.394\nImagine someone saying this:\n\n65\n00:04:02.434 --> 00:04:05.634\nI see the small tree there.\n\n66\n00:04:05.714 --> 00:04:08.215\nI see the small tree there.\n\n67\n00:04:08.235 --> 00:04:09.975\nWhat is wrong here?\n\n68\n00:04:09.995 --> 00:04:12.695\nRemember our golden rule.\n\n69\n00:04:12.775 --> 00:04:17.655\nEvery diminutive must use 'het', not 'de'.\n\n70\n00:04:17.695 --> 00:04:19.575\nSo we must say:\n\n71\n00:04:19.635 --> 00:04:23.135\nI see the small tree there.\n\n72\n00:04:23.256 --> 00:04:25.596\nI see the small tree there.\n\n73\n00:04:25.656 --> 00:04:27.816\nHere is another mistake:\n\n74\n00:04:27.856 --> 00:04:30.756\nThat is a small tree.\n\n75\n00:04:30.796 --> 00:04:32.856\nThat is a small tree.\n\n76\n00:04:32.916 --> 00:04:34.736\nWhat went wrong here?\n\n77\n00:04:34.776 --> 00:04:39.076\nFor words ending in 'm', use '-pje'.\n\n78\n00:04:39.116 --> 00:04:41.737\nNot just '-je'.\n\n79\n00:04:41.777 --> 00:04:44.997\nSo 'boompje' is correct!\n\n80\n00:04:45.037 --> 00:04:47.437\nLet's do a quick pattern drill.\n\n81\n00:04:47.477 --> 00:04:50.397\nRepeat these short forms after me.\n\n82\n00:04:50.437 --> 00:04:53.717\nThe small cup of tea.\n\n83\n00:04:53.737 --> 00:04:55.477\nThe small cup of tea.\n\n84\n00:04:55.537 --> 00:04:59.278\nThe small green leaf.\n\n85\n00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:01.238\nThe small green leaf.\n\n86\n00:05:01.298 --> 00:05:04.938\nThe cute small house.\n\n87\n00:05:04.958 --> 00:05:07.338\nThe cute small house.\n\n88\n00:05:07.378 --> 00:05:10.998\nThe soft warm small blanket.\n\n89\n00:05:11.018 --> 00:05:14.139\nThe soft warm small blanket.\n\n90\n00:05:14.179 --> 00:05:16.479\nRemember this memory anchor:\n\n91\n00:05:16.519 --> 00:05:20.399\nDiminutives always make things cozy.\n\n92\n00:05:20.519 --> 00:05:23.139\nAnd they always take 'het'!\n\n93\n00:05:23.159 --> 00:05:27.039\nToday we learned how to use diminutives.\n\n94\n00:05:27.099 --> 00:05:31.140\nToday we learned the diminutives.\n\n95\n00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:33.860\nToday we learned the diminutives.\n\n96\n00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:37.720\nKeep practicing these small, cozy words!\n\n97\n00:05:37.820 --> 00:05:39.240\nSee you next time!\n
grammar_directional_prepositions	nl	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.960\nImagine you are traveling in the Netherlands.\n\n2\n00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.740\nYou want to talk about your movements.\n\n3\n00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.461\nToday we learn four directional prepositions:\n\n4\n00:00:12.481 --> 00:00:18.801\nnaar, uit, van, and bij.\n\n5\n00:00:18.861 --> 00:00:26.081\nIn English: to, out of, from, at.\n\n6\n00:00:26.121 --> 00:00:28.322\nLaten we beginnen!\n\n7\n00:00:28.362 --> 00:00:30.062\nLet's get started!\n\n8\n00:00:30.102 --> 00:00:35.022\nFirst, let me introduce the preposition naar.\n\n9\n00:00:35.162 --> 00:00:39.962\nUse naar for moving towards a destination.\n\n10\n00:00:40.002 --> 00:00:43.462\nLet's hear a simple example sentence.\n\n11\n00:00:43.522 --> 00:00:46.763\nIk ga naar de winkel.\n\n12\n00:00:46.903 --> 00:00:49.063\nI am going to the store.\n\n13\n00:00:49.123 --> 00:00:52.223\nLet's add a bit more detail now.\n\n14\n00:00:52.303 --> 00:00:55.943\nHij rijdt naar de stad.\n\n15\n00:00:55.983 --> 00:00:58.383\nHe drives to the city.\n\n16\n00:00:58.423 --> 00:01:00.923\nCan you guess how to say this?\n\n17\n00:01:00.963 --> 00:01:04.484\nHow do you say: We walk home?\n\n18\n00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:10.704\nRemember: home as destination uses naar huis.\n\n19\n00:01:10.744 --> 00:01:13.784\nWij lopen naar huis.\n\n20\n00:01:13.824 --> 00:01:16.684\nWe walk home.\n\n21\n00:01:16.824 --> 00:01:20.465\nNow, let's look at the word uit.\n\n22\n00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:23.985\nUse uit for moving out of something.\n\n23\n00:01:24.045 --> 00:01:27.705\nIt also describes your country of origin.\n\n24\n00:01:27.785 --> 00:01:31.025\nIk kom uit Nederland.\n\n25\n00:01:31.185 --> 00:01:34.205\nI come from the Netherlands.\n\n26\n00:01:34.305 --> 00:01:37.706\nLet's try another sentence with uit.\n\n27\n00:01:37.766 --> 00:01:41.186\nZij komt uit de kamer.\n\n28\n00:01:41.306 --> 00:01:44.926\nShe comes out of the room.\n\n29\n00:01:44.986 --> 00:01:48.106\nNext up is the preposition van.\n\n30\n00:01:48.266 --> 00:01:52.706\nUse van for starting points or sources.\n\n31\n00:01:52.766 --> 00:01:55.907\nHij komt van zijn werk.\n\n32\n00:01:55.927 --> 00:01:59.087\nHe comes from his work.\n\n33\n00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:02.427\nLet me give you another example sentence.\n\n34\n00:02:02.447 --> 00:02:05.647\nDe trein komt van Utrecht.\n\n35\n00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:09.507\nThe train comes from Utrecht.\n\n36\n00:02:09.527 --> 00:02:12.888\nCan you guess the next word?\n\n37\n00:02:12.948 --> 00:02:18.288\nHow would you say: I return from vacation?\n\n38\n00:02:18.368 --> 00:02:22.528\nIk kom terug van vakantie.\n\n39\n00:02:22.748 --> 00:02:26.348\nI return from vacation.\n\n40\n00:02:26.388 --> 00:02:31.789\nFinally, let me introduce the preposition bij.\n\n41\n00:02:31.949 --> 00:02:36.009\nUse bij when staying at a location.\n\n42\n00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:39.849\nIt also means at someone's home.\n\n43\n00:02:39.929 --> 00:02:43.089\nIk ben bij de dokter.\n\n44\n00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:46.589\nI am at the doctor.\n\n45\n00:02:46.649 --> 00:02:49.870\nHij woont bij het park.\n\n46\n00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:53.390\nHe lives near the park.\n\n47\n00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.750\nLet me test you with a quick drill!\n\n48\n00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.050\nHow do you say: I go to work?\n\n49\n00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.890\nIk ga naar mijn werk.\n\n50\n00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.271\nI go to my work.\n\n51\n00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:11.491\nHow do you say: I am at Jan's?\n\n52\n00:03:11.571 --> 00:03:15.391\nIk ben bij Jan.\n\n53\n00:03:15.551 --> 00:03:18.651\nI am at Jan's house.\n\n54\n00:03:18.711 --> 00:03:23.732\nHow do you say: She walks from school?\n\n55\n00:03:23.812 --> 00:03:27.912\nZij loopt van school.\n\n56\n00:03:27.952 --> 00:03:30.772\nShe walks from school.\n\n57\n00:03:30.812 --> 00:03:35.672\nHow to say: He comes out of school?\n\n58\n00:03:35.752 --> 00:03:39.773\nHij komt uit school.\n\n59\n00:03:39.813 --> 00:03:42.833\nHe comes out of school.\n\n60\n00:03:42.853 --> 00:03:46.833\nTime for our common mistake clinic!\n\n61\n00:03:46.853 --> 00:03:52.493\nBeginners often confuse naar and bij.\n\n62\n00:03:52.533 --> 00:03:56.654\nLook at this incorrect Dutch sentence:\n\n63\n00:03:56.714 --> 00:04:00.834\nIk ga bij de winkel.\n\n64\n00:04:00.874 --> 00:04:04.674\nThis is incorrect in Dutch!\n\n65\n00:04:04.714 --> 00:04:08.054\nWhy is this sentence wrong?\n\n66\n00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:13.794\nBecause bij means at, not towards!\n\n67\n00:04:13.814 --> 00:04:17.815\nFor direction towards a place, use naar.\n\n68\n00:04:17.875 --> 00:04:21.135\nIk ga naar de winkel.\n\n69\n00:04:21.375 --> 00:04:24.095\nI am going to the store.\n\n70\n00:04:24.155 --> 00:04:27.595\nLet's see another common beginner error.\n\n71\n00:04:27.675 --> 00:04:31.095\nIk kom van Nederland.\n\n72\n00:04:31.135 --> 00:04:33.216\nThis is also wrong!\n\n73\n00:04:33.256 --> 00:04:37.336\nWhen talking about origin country, use uit.\n\n74\n00:04:37.416 --> 00:04:40.856\nIk kom uit Nederland.\n\n75\n00:04:41.016 --> 00:04:43.876\nI come from the Netherlands.\n\n76\n00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.876\nHere is your simple memory anchor!\n\n77\n00:04:46.916 --> 00:04:51.757\nNaar is to, uit is out of.\n\n78\n00:04:51.837 --> 00:04:56.217\nVan is from, bij is at.\n\n79\n00:04:56.277 --> 00:04:59.617\nNow let's do our final spoken recap!\n\n80\n00:04:59.657 --> 00:05:07.278\nVandaag leerden we: naar, uit, van, bij.\n\n81\n00:05:07.318 --> 00:05:14.718\nToday we learned: naar, uit, van, bij.\n\n82\n00:05:14.778 --> 00:05:18.078\nIk ga naar het park.\n\n83\n00:05:18.258 --> 00:05:20.638\nI go to the park.\n\n84\n00:05:20.718 --> 00:05:23.899\nIk kom uit het park.\n\n85\n00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:28.219\nI come out of the park.\n\n86\n00:05:28.279 --> 00:05:32.239\nIk loop van het park.\n\n87\n00:05:32.519 --> 00:05:36.979\nI walk from the park.\n\n88\n00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:41.740\nIk ben bij het park.\n\n89\n00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:47.240\nI am near the park.\n\n90\n00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.000\nGreat job practicing Dutch grammar today!\n
grammar_directional_prepositions	en	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.960\nImagine you are traveling in the Netherlands.\n\n2\n00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:07.740\nYou want to talk about your movements.\n\n3\n00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:12.461\nToday we learn four directional prepositions:\n\n4\n00:00:12.481 --> 00:00:18.801\nnaar, uit, van, and bij.\n\n5\n00:00:18.861 --> 00:00:26.081\nIn English: to, out of, from, at.\n\n6\n00:00:26.121 --> 00:00:28.322\nLet's get started!\n\n7\n00:00:28.362 --> 00:00:30.062\nLet's get started!\n\n8\n00:00:30.102 --> 00:00:35.022\nFirst, let me introduce the preposition naar.\n\n9\n00:00:35.162 --> 00:00:39.962\nUse naar for moving towards a destination.\n\n10\n00:00:40.002 --> 00:00:43.462\nLet's hear a simple example sentence.\n\n11\n00:00:43.522 --> 00:00:46.763\nI am going to the store.\n\n12\n00:00:46.903 --> 00:00:49.063\nI am going to the store.\n\n13\n00:00:49.123 --> 00:00:52.223\nLet's add a bit more detail now.\n\n14\n00:00:52.303 --> 00:00:55.943\nHe drives to the city.\n\n15\n00:00:55.983 --> 00:00:58.383\nHe drives to the city.\n\n16\n00:00:58.423 --> 00:01:00.923\nCan you guess how to say this?\n\n17\n00:01:00.963 --> 00:01:04.484\nHow do you say: We walk home?\n\n18\n00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:10.704\nRemember: home as destination uses naar huis.\n\n19\n00:01:10.744 --> 00:01:13.784\nWe walk home.\n\n20\n00:01:13.824 --> 00:01:16.684\nWe walk home.\n\n21\n00:01:16.824 --> 00:01:20.465\nNow, let's look at the word uit.\n\n22\n00:01:20.625 --> 00:01:23.985\nUse uit for moving out of something.\n\n23\n00:01:24.045 --> 00:01:27.705\nIt also describes your country of origin.\n\n24\n00:01:27.785 --> 00:01:31.025\nI come from the Netherlands.\n\n25\n00:01:31.185 --> 00:01:34.205\nI come from the Netherlands.\n\n26\n00:01:34.305 --> 00:01:37.706\nLet's try another sentence with uit.\n\n27\n00:01:37.766 --> 00:01:41.186\nShe comes out of the room.\n\n28\n00:01:41.306 --> 00:01:44.926\nShe comes out of the room.\n\n29\n00:01:44.986 --> 00:01:48.106\nNext up is the preposition van.\n\n30\n00:01:48.266 --> 00:01:52.706\nUse van for starting points or sources.\n\n31\n00:01:52.766 --> 00:01:55.907\nHe comes from his work.\n\n32\n00:01:55.927 --> 00:01:59.087\nHe comes from his work.\n\n33\n00:01:59.167 --> 00:02:02.427\nLet me give you another example sentence.\n\n34\n00:02:02.447 --> 00:02:05.647\nThe train comes from Utrecht.\n\n35\n00:02:05.667 --> 00:02:09.507\nThe train comes from Utrecht.\n\n36\n00:02:09.527 --> 00:02:12.888\nCan you guess the next word?\n\n37\n00:02:12.948 --> 00:02:18.288\nHow would you say: I return from vacation?\n\n38\n00:02:18.368 --> 00:02:22.528\nI return from vacation.\n\n39\n00:02:22.748 --> 00:02:26.348\nI return from vacation.\n\n40\n00:02:26.388 --> 00:02:31.789\nFinally, let me introduce the preposition bij.\n\n41\n00:02:31.949 --> 00:02:36.009\nUse bij when staying at a location.\n\n42\n00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:39.849\nIt also means at someone's home.\n\n43\n00:02:39.929 --> 00:02:43.089\nI am at the doctor.\n\n44\n00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:46.589\nI am at the doctor.\n\n45\n00:02:46.649 --> 00:02:49.870\nHe lives near the park.\n\n46\n00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:53.390\nHe lives near the park.\n\n47\n00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.750\nLet me test you with a quick drill!\n\n48\n00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.050\nHow do you say: I go to work?\n\n49\n00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.890\nI go to my work.\n\n50\n00:03:04.130 --> 00:03:06.271\nI go to my work.\n\n51\n00:03:06.311 --> 00:03:11.491\nHow do you say: I am at Jan's?\n\n52\n00:03:11.571 --> 00:03:15.391\nI am at Jan's house.\n\n53\n00:03:15.551 --> 00:03:18.651\nI am at Jan's house.\n\n54\n00:03:18.711 --> 00:03:23.732\nHow do you say: She walks from school?\n\n55\n00:03:23.812 --> 00:03:27.912\nShe walks from school.\n\n56\n00:03:27.952 --> 00:03:30.772\nShe walks from school.\n\n57\n00:03:30.812 --> 00:03:35.672\nHow to say: He comes out of school?\n\n58\n00:03:35.752 --> 00:03:39.773\nHe comes out of school.\n\n59\n00:03:39.813 --> 00:03:42.833\nHe comes out of school.\n\n60\n00:03:42.853 --> 00:03:46.833\nTime for our common mistake clinic!\n\n61\n00:03:46.853 --> 00:03:52.493\nBeginners often confuse naar and bij.\n\n62\n00:03:52.533 --> 00:03:56.654\nLook at this incorrect Dutch sentence:\n\n63\n00:03:56.714 --> 00:04:00.834\nI go at the store.\n\n64\n00:04:00.874 --> 00:04:04.674\nThis is incorrect in Dutch!\n\n65\n00:04:04.714 --> 00:04:08.054\nWhy is this sentence wrong?\n\n66\n00:04:08.114 --> 00:04:13.794\nBecause bij means at, not towards!\n\n67\n00:04:13.814 --> 00:04:17.815\nFor direction towards a place, use naar.\n\n68\n00:04:17.875 --> 00:04:21.135\nI am going to the store.\n\n69\n00:04:21.375 --> 00:04:24.095\nI am going to the store.\n\n70\n00:04:24.155 --> 00:04:27.595\nLet's see another common beginner error.\n\n71\n00:04:27.675 --> 00:04:31.095\nI come from Netherlands.\n\n72\n00:04:31.135 --> 00:04:33.216\nThis is also wrong!\n\n73\n00:04:33.256 --> 00:04:37.336\nWhen talking about origin country, use uit.\n\n74\n00:04:37.416 --> 00:04:40.856\nI come from the Netherlands.\n\n75\n00:04:41.016 --> 00:04:43.876\nI come from the Netherlands.\n\n76\n00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:46.876\nHere is your simple memory anchor!\n\n77\n00:04:46.916 --> 00:04:51.757\nNaar is to, uit is out of.\n\n78\n00:04:51.837 --> 00:04:56.217\nVan is from, bij is at.\n\n79\n00:04:56.277 --> 00:04:59.617\nNow let's do our final spoken recap!\n\n80\n00:04:59.657 --> 00:05:07.278\nToday we learned: naar, uit, van, bij.\n\n81\n00:05:07.318 --> 00:05:14.718\nToday we learned: naar, uit, van, bij.\n\n82\n00:05:14.778 --> 00:05:18.078\nI go to the park.\n\n83\n00:05:18.258 --> 00:05:20.638\nI go to the park.\n\n84\n00:05:20.718 --> 00:05:23.899\nI come out of the park.\n\n85\n00:05:24.199 --> 00:05:28.219\nI come out of the park.\n\n86\n00:05:28.279 --> 00:05:32.239\nI walk from the park.\n\n87\n00:05:32.519 --> 00:05:36.979\nI walk from the park.\n\n88\n00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:41.740\nI am near the park.\n\n89\n00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:47.240\nI am near the park.\n\n90\n00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:50.000\nGreat job practicing Dutch grammar today!\n
grammar_er	nl	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.880\nImagine describing a room in Dutch.\n\n2\n00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.880\nHow do you say 'there is'?\n\n3\n00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.140\nYou need the magic word 'er'!\n\n4\n00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:13.521\nIn English, you say 'there is'.\n\n5\n00:00:13.561 --> 00:00:16.861\nIn Dutch, we use 'er'.\n\n6\n00:00:16.921 --> 00:00:18.561\nLaten we beginnen!\n\n7\n00:00:18.641 --> 00:00:19.961\nLet's begin!\n\n8\n00:00:19.981 --> 00:00:24.721\nTo say 'there is', use 'er is'.\n\n9\n00:00:24.781 --> 00:00:27.081\nEr is een man.\n\n10\n00:00:27.121 --> 00:00:29.061\nThere is a man.\n\n11\n00:00:29.081 --> 00:00:33.782\nTo say 'there are', use 'er zijn'.\n\n12\n00:00:33.862 --> 00:00:36.562\nEr zijn twee mannen.\n\n13\n00:00:36.602 --> 00:00:38.742\nThere are two men.\n\n14\n00:00:38.782 --> 00:00:41.782\nDutch also uses posture verbs.\n\n15\n00:00:41.822 --> 00:00:45.462\nVerbs like 'staat' and 'ligt'.\n\n16\n00:00:45.502 --> 00:00:48.302\n'Staat' is for standing objects.\n\n17\n00:00:48.382 --> 00:00:51.342\n'Ligt' is for lying objects.\n\n18\n00:00:51.422 --> 00:00:54.122\nLet's practice vertical things first!\n\n19\n00:00:54.202 --> 00:00:56.603\nEr staat een boom.\n\n20\n00:00:56.643 --> 00:00:58.883\nThere is a tree standing.\n\n21\n00:00:58.943 --> 00:01:02.023\nEr staat een kopje.\n\n22\n00:01:02.043 --> 00:01:04.823\nThere is a cup standing.\n\n23\n00:01:04.843 --> 00:01:09.143\nNow, let's practice flat objects!\n\n24\n00:01:09.203 --> 00:01:11.563\nEr ligt een boek.\n\n25\n00:01:11.603 --> 00:01:14.183\nThere is a book lying.\n\n26\n00:01:14.243 --> 00:01:16.563\nEr ligt een pen.\n\n27\n00:01:16.583 --> 00:01:19.084\nThere is a pen lying.\n\n28\n00:01:19.104 --> 00:01:21.264\nTime for a quick question!\n\n29\n00:01:21.284 --> 00:01:24.384\nHow to say 'There is a car'?\n\n30\n00:01:24.424 --> 00:01:26.804\nThink about the verb posture!\n\n31\n00:01:26.864 --> 00:01:29.764\nA car stands on wheels.\n\n32\n00:01:29.804 --> 00:01:32.384\nSo we use 'er staat'.\n\n33\n00:01:32.424 --> 00:01:35.044\nEr staat een auto.\n\n34\n00:01:35.064 --> 00:01:37.604\nThere is a car standing.\n\n35\n00:01:37.644 --> 00:01:41.325\nGreat job! Another question now.\n\n36\n00:01:41.385 --> 00:01:44.305\nHow to say 'There is money'?\n\n37\n00:01:44.365 --> 00:01:47.285\nMoney lies flat on a table!\n\n38\n00:01:47.325 --> 00:01:49.905\nSo we use 'er ligt'.\n\n39\n00:01:49.965 --> 00:01:52.645\nEr ligt geld op tafel.\n\n40\n00:01:52.685 --> 00:01:55.425\nThere is money on the table.\n\n41\n00:01:55.485 --> 00:01:58.185\nLet's do a quick pattern drill!\n\n42\n00:01:58.225 --> 00:02:01.105\nRepeat after me every time.\n\n43\n00:02:01.185 --> 00:02:04.806\nEr is een kat.\n\n44\n00:02:04.826 --> 00:02:08.086\nThere is a cat.\n\n45\n00:02:08.146 --> 00:02:12.226\nEr zijn drie katten.\n\n46\n00:02:12.246 --> 00:02:16.046\nThere are three cats.\n\n47\n00:02:16.106 --> 00:02:20.026\nEr staat een stoel.\n\n48\n00:02:20.066 --> 00:02:24.026\nThere is a chair standing.\n\n49\n00:02:24.086 --> 00:02:28.007\nEr ligt een krant.\n\n50\n00:02:28.027 --> 00:02:32.087\nThere is a newspaper lying.\n\n51\n00:02:32.107 --> 00:02:36.067\nGreat rhythm! You are doing well.\n\n52\n00:02:36.127 --> 00:02:39.487\nLet me show common mistakes.\n\n53\n00:02:39.507 --> 00:02:43.607\nMistake one: omitting 'er'.\n\n54\n00:02:43.627 --> 00:02:47.648\nBeginners say: 'Is een boek'.\n\n55\n00:02:47.668 --> 00:02:50.048\nThat is wrong in Dutch!\n\n56\n00:02:50.068 --> 00:02:53.688\nYou must always include 'er'.\n\n57\n00:02:53.728 --> 00:02:55.448\nCorrect version:\n\n58\n00:02:55.528 --> 00:02:58.168\nEr is een boek.\n\n59\n00:02:58.188 --> 00:03:00.508\nThere is a book.\n\n60\n00:03:00.548 --> 00:03:04.628\nMistake two: wrong posture verb.\n\n61\n00:03:04.668 --> 00:03:08.288\nDon't say: 'Er is een fles'.\n\n62\n00:03:08.348 --> 00:03:10.949\nA bottle stands upright!\n\n63\n00:03:10.969 --> 00:03:12.989\nCorrect version:\n\n64\n00:03:13.069 --> 00:03:15.689\nEr staat een fles.\n\n65\n00:03:15.729 --> 00:03:18.189\nThere is a bottle standing.\n\n66\n00:03:18.249 --> 00:03:21.349\nHere is your memory anchor tip!\n\n67\n00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:25.089\nIf upright, use 'er staat'.\n\n68\n00:03:25.149 --> 00:03:28.909\nIf flat, use 'er ligt'.\n\n69\n00:03:28.949 --> 00:03:33.010\nFor general existence, use 'er is'.\n\n70\n00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:35.710\nLet me recap today's lesson.\n\n71\n00:03:35.790 --> 00:03:39.670\nVandaag leerden we het woord er.\n\n72\n00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.610\nToday we learned the word 'er'.\n
grammar_er	en	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.880\nImagine describing a room in Dutch.\n\n2\n00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:07.880\nHow do you say 'there is'?\n\n3\n00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.140\nYou need the magic word 'er'!\n\n4\n00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.521\nIn English, you say 'there is'.\n\n5\n00:00:14.561 --> 00:00:17.861\nIn Dutch, we use 'er'.\n\n6\n00:00:17.921 --> 00:00:19.561\nLet's begin!\n\n7\n00:00:19.641 --> 00:00:20.961\nLet's begin!\n\n8\n00:00:20.981 --> 00:00:25.721\nTo say 'there is', use 'er is'.\n\n9\n00:00:25.781 --> 00:00:28.081\nThere is a man.\n\n10\n00:00:28.121 --> 00:00:30.061\nThere is a man.\n\n11\n00:00:30.081 --> 00:00:34.782\nTo say 'there are', use 'er zijn'.\n\n12\n00:00:34.862 --> 00:00:37.562\nThere are two men.\n\n13\n00:00:37.602 --> 00:00:39.742\nThere are two men.\n\n14\n00:00:39.782 --> 00:00:42.782\nDutch also uses posture verbs.\n\n15\n00:00:42.822 --> 00:00:46.462\nVerbs like 'staat' and 'ligt'.\n\n16\n00:00:46.502 --> 00:00:49.302\n'Staat' is for standing objects.\n\n17\n00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:52.342\n'Ligt' is for lying objects.\n\n18\n00:00:52.422 --> 00:00:55.122\nLet's practice vertical things first!\n\n19\n00:00:55.202 --> 00:00:57.603\nThere is a tree standing.\n\n20\n00:00:57.643 --> 00:00:59.883\nThere is a tree standing.\n\n21\n00:00:59.943 --> 00:01:03.023\nThere is a cup standing.\n\n22\n00:01:03.043 --> 00:01:05.823\nThere is a cup standing.\n\n23\n00:01:05.843 --> 00:01:10.143\nNow, let's practice flat objects!\n\n24\n00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:12.563\nThere is a book lying.\n\n25\n00:01:12.603 --> 00:01:15.183\nThere is a book lying.\n\n26\n00:01:15.243 --> 00:01:17.563\nThere is a pen lying.\n\n27\n00:01:17.583 --> 00:01:20.084\nThere is a pen lying.\n\n28\n00:01:20.104 --> 00:01:22.264\nTime for a quick question!\n\n29\n00:01:22.284 --> 00:01:25.384\nHow to say 'There is a car'?\n\n30\n00:01:25.424 --> 00:01:27.804\nThink about the verb posture!\n\n31\n00:01:27.864 --> 00:01:30.764\nA car stands on wheels.\n\n32\n00:01:30.804 --> 00:01:33.384\nSo we use 'er staat'.\n\n33\n00:01:33.424 --> 00:01:36.044\nThere is a car standing.\n\n34\n00:01:36.064 --> 00:01:38.604\nThere is a car standing.\n\n35\n00:01:38.644 --> 00:01:42.325\nGreat job! Another question now.\n\n36\n00:01:42.385 --> 00:01:45.305\nHow to say 'There is money'?\n\n37\n00:01:45.365 --> 00:01:48.285\nMoney lies flat on a table!\n\n38\n00:01:48.325 --> 00:01:50.905\nSo we use 'er ligt'.\n\n39\n00:01:50.965 --> 00:01:53.645\nThere is money on the table.\n\n40\n00:01:53.685 --> 00:01:56.425\nThere is money on the table.\n\n41\n00:01:56.485 --> 00:01:59.185\nLet's do a quick pattern drill!\n\n42\n00:01:59.225 --> 00:02:02.105\nRepeat after me every time.\n\n43\n00:02:02.185 --> 00:02:05.806\nThere is a cat.\n\n44\n00:02:05.826 --> 00:02:09.086\nThere is a cat.\n\n45\n00:02:09.146 --> 00:02:13.226\nThere are three cats.\n\n46\n00:02:13.246 --> 00:02:17.046\nThere are three cats.\n\n47\n00:02:17.106 --> 00:02:21.026\nThere is a chair standing.\n\n48\n00:02:21.066 --> 00:02:25.026\nThere is a chair standing.\n\n49\n00:02:25.086 --> 00:02:29.007\nThere is a newspaper lying.\n\n50\n00:02:29.027 --> 00:02:33.087\nThere is a newspaper lying.\n\n51\n00:02:33.107 --> 00:02:37.067\nGreat rhythm! You are doing well.\n\n52\n00:02:37.127 --> 00:02:40.487\nLet me show common mistakes.\n\n53\n00:02:40.507 --> 00:02:44.607\nMistake one: omitting 'er'.\n\n54\n00:02:44.627 --> 00:02:48.648\nBeginners say: 'Is een boek'.\n\n55\n00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:51.048\nThat is wrong in Dutch!\n\n56\n00:02:51.068 --> 00:02:54.688\nYou must always include 'er'.\n\n57\n00:02:54.728 --> 00:02:56.448\nCorrect version:\n\n58\n00:02:56.528 --> 00:02:59.168\nThere is a book.\n\n59\n00:02:59.188 --> 00:03:01.508\nThere is a book.\n\n60\n00:03:01.548 --> 00:03:05.628\nMistake two: wrong posture verb.\n\n61\n00:03:05.668 --> 00:03:09.288\nDon't say: 'Er is een fles'.\n\n62\n00:03:09.348 --> 00:03:11.949\nA bottle stands upright!\n\n63\n00:03:11.969 --> 00:03:13.989\nCorrect version:\n\n64\n00:03:14.069 --> 00:03:16.689\nThere is a bottle standing.\n\n65\n00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:19.189\nThere is a bottle standing.\n\n66\n00:03:19.249 --> 00:03:22.349\nHere is your memory anchor tip!\n\n67\n00:03:22.409 --> 00:03:26.089\nIf upright, use 'er staat'.\n\n68\n00:03:26.149 --> 00:03:29.909\nIf flat, use 'er ligt'.\n\n69\n00:03:29.949 --> 00:03:34.010\nFor general existence, use 'er is'.\n\n70\n00:03:34.070 --> 00:03:36.710\nLet me recap today's lesson.\n\n71\n00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:40.670\nToday we learned the word 'er'.\n\n72\n00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:43.610\nToday we learned the word 'er'.\n
grammar_future_gaan	nl	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:05.100\nImagine planning your weekend with Dutch friends.\n\n2\n00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:07.860\nYou want to talk about your plans.\n\n3\n00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.680\nYou need to express the future tense.\n\n4\n00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:16.280\nIn English, you say: I am going to.\n\n5\n00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:21.161\nIn Dutch, we use the verb gaan.\n\n6\n00:00:21.241 --> 00:00:24.601\nLaten we beginnen!\n\n7\n00:00:24.761 --> 00:00:26.241\nLet us begin!\n\n8\n00:00:26.301 --> 00:00:29.961\nThe rule is very simple and logical.\n\n9\n00:00:30.001 --> 00:00:34.781\nCombine conjugated gaan with an infinitive verb.\n\n10\n00:00:34.821 --> 00:00:37.761\nPut the infinitive at the end.\n\n11\n00:00:37.921 --> 00:00:40.801\nLet's see our first example sentence.\n\n12\n00:00:40.861 --> 00:00:44.501\nIk ga morgen werken.\n\n13\n00:00:44.661 --> 00:00:46.921\nI am going to work tomorrow.\n\n14\n00:00:46.961 --> 00:00:50.682\nNotice that ga comes second here.\n\n15\n00:00:50.742 --> 00:00:54.302\nWerken goes to the very end.\n\n16\n00:00:54.342 --> 00:00:58.342\nWij gaan samen eten.\n\n17\n00:00:58.362 --> 00:01:00.622\nWe are going to eat together.\n\n18\n00:01:00.682 --> 00:01:03.802\nIt works just like English going to.\n\n19\n00:01:03.942 --> 00:01:08.122\nLet me show you more simple examples.\n\n20\n00:01:08.202 --> 00:01:14.522\nHij gaat een boek lezen.\n\n21\n00:01:14.582 --> 00:01:18.042\nHe is going to read a book.\n\n22\n00:01:18.222 --> 00:01:21.403\nLook at the position of lezen.\n\n23\n00:01:21.463 --> 00:01:24.583\nIt sits right at the end.\n\n24\n00:01:24.623 --> 00:01:27.883\nNow, let me ask you a question.\n\n25\n00:01:27.943 --> 00:01:32.283\nHow would you say: I am going?\n\n26\n00:01:32.363 --> 00:01:36.263\nIk ga slapen.\n\n27\n00:01:36.523 --> 00:01:39.283\nI am going to sleep.\n\n28\n00:01:39.323 --> 00:01:44.363\nGreat job! Let's build longer sentences.\n\n29\n00:01:44.423 --> 00:01:49.363\nZij gaat koffie drinken.\n\n30\n00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:53.024\nShe is going to drink coffee.\n\n31\n00:01:53.084 --> 00:01:58.984\nWij gaan naar school fietsen.\n\n32\n00:01:59.124 --> 00:02:02.344\nWe are going to cycle to school.\n\n33\n00:02:02.384 --> 00:02:05.084\nCan you guess the next sentence?\n\n34\n00:02:05.144 --> 00:02:08.364\nHow do you say: They are going?\n\n35\n00:02:08.464 --> 00:02:12.044\nZij gaan muziek luisteren.\n\n36\n00:02:12.084 --> 00:02:15.104\nThey are going to listen to music.\n\n37\n00:02:15.204 --> 00:02:18.064\nLet's practice the pattern together now.\n\n38\n00:02:18.124 --> 00:02:23.184\nConjugate gaan, then add the infinitive.\n\n39\n00:02:23.245 --> 00:02:26.525\nIk ga vandaag koken.\n\n40\n00:02:26.665 --> 00:02:29.405\nI am going to cook today.\n\n41\n00:02:29.485 --> 00:02:33.105\nJij gaat straks bellen.\n\n42\n00:02:33.205 --> 00:02:36.045\nYou are going to call later.\n\n43\n00:02:36.125 --> 00:02:39.465\nHij gaat geld sparen.\n\n44\n00:02:39.505 --> 00:02:42.265\nHe is going to save money.\n\n45\n00:02:42.425 --> 00:02:45.865\nWe gaan een film kijken.\n\n46\n00:02:45.925 --> 00:02:48.805\nWe are going to watch a movie.\n\n47\n00:02:48.885 --> 00:02:52.225\nJullie gaan veel leren.\n\n48\n00:02:52.365 --> 00:02:55.326\nYou all are going to learn much.\n\n49\n00:02:55.406 --> 00:02:59.946\nExcellent! You are mastering the pattern.\n\n50\n00:03:00.006 --> 00:03:03.486\nNow try this one yourself first.\n\n51\n00:03:03.526 --> 00:03:07.886\nHow do you say: I will stay?\n\n52\n00:03:07.946 --> 00:03:12.366\nThink for a brief moment now.\n\n53\n00:03:12.426 --> 00:03:16.766\nHere is the correct Dutch sentence.\n\n54\n00:03:16.826 --> 00:03:20.846\nIk ga thuis blijven.\n\n55\n00:03:21.026 --> 00:03:25.006\nI am going to stay home.\n\n56\n00:03:25.046 --> 00:03:29.267\nNow, time for our error clinic!\n\n57\n00:03:29.327 --> 00:03:34.047\nLet me highlight two common beginner mistakes.\n\n58\n00:03:34.107 --> 00:03:39.907\nFirst mistake: putting verbs together early.\n\n59\n00:03:39.967 --> 00:03:44.287\nListen to this incorrect Dutch sentence.\n\n60\n00:03:44.347 --> 00:03:48.107\nIk ga werken morgen.\n\n61\n00:03:48.147 --> 00:03:52.147\nThat sentence is incorrect in Dutch!\n\n62\n00:03:52.207 --> 00:03:55.247\nWhy is that sentence wrong?\n\n63\n00:03:55.307 --> 00:03:59.648\nWerken must go to the end!\n\n64\n00:03:59.708 --> 00:04:03.648\nHere is the correct sentence again.\n\n65\n00:04:03.728 --> 00:04:08.008\nIk ga morgen werken.\n\n66\n00:04:08.188 --> 00:04:12.528\nI am going to work tomorrow.\n\n67\n00:04:12.588 --> 00:04:16.788\nSecond mistake: forgetting the full infinitive.\n\n68\n00:04:16.888 --> 00:04:21.088\nListen to another incorrect sentence.\n\n69\n00:04:21.148 --> 00:04:24.908\nWij gaan eten kook.\n\n70\n00:04:24.948 --> 00:04:29.989\nThat is wrong because kook is conjugated.\n\n71\n00:04:30.029 --> 00:04:35.329\nYou must always use the infinitive koken!\n\n72\n00:04:35.429 --> 00:04:38.209\nLet's fix that sentence now.\n\n73\n00:04:38.269 --> 00:04:42.329\nWij gaan eten koken.\n\n74\n00:04:42.369 --> 00:04:45.629\nWe are going to cook food.\n\n75\n00:04:45.689 --> 00:04:49.569\nHere is your handy memory anchor tip.\n\n76\n00:04:49.729 --> 00:04:54.729\nGaan is second, action verb is last!\n\n77\n00:04:54.749 --> 00:04:58.609\nKeep the infinitive at the very end!\n\n78\n00:04:58.669 --> 00:05:02.130\nLet's do a fast final review.\n\n79\n00:05:02.170 --> 00:05:06.210\nVandaag leerden we de toekomst.\n\n80\n00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:10.390\nToday we learned the future tense.\n\n81\n00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:15.810\nUse conjugated gaan plus full infinitive.\n\n82\n00:05:15.830 --> 00:05:16.490\nTot de volgende keer!\n\n83\n00:05:16.510 --> 00:05:17.070\nUntil next time!\n
