:49.087\nIn order to stay healthy.\n\n33\n00:01:49.147 --> 00:01:52.367\nIn order to stay healthy.\n\n34\n00:01:52.447 --> 00:01:54.987\nNow let me ask you something.\n\n35\n00:01:55.047 --> 00:01:58.287\nHow do you say: to sleep?\n\n36\n00:01:58.327 --> 00:02:00.747\nThink about the pattern.\n\n37\n00:02:00.767 --> 00:02:05.008\nThe answer is: om te slapen.\n\n38\n00:02:05.048 --> 00:02:07.488\nIt is time to sleep.\n\n39\n00:02:07.568 --> 00:02:11.908\nIt is time to sleep.\n\n40\n00:02:12.028 --> 00:02:16.588\nNow, how about: to study?\n\n41\n00:02:16.608 --> 00:02:22.189\nThe Dutch verb is studeren.\n\n42\n00:02:22.249 --> 00:02:27.169\nHow would you say: to study?\n\n43\n00:02:27.189 --> 00:02:33.669\nThat is right: om te studeren.\n\n44\n00:02:33.769 --> 00:02:40.730\nI study in order to learn.\n\n45\n00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:45.890\nI study in order to learn.\n\n46\n00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:49.590\nPattern drill time!\n\n47\n00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:54.751\nLet's repeat with different actions.\n\n48\n00:02:54.791 --> 00:02:59.871\nFirst action: buying bread.\n\n49\n00:02:59.971 --> 00:03:06.891\nI go to buy bread.\n\n50\n00:03:07.011 --> 00:03:12.052\nI go to buy bread.\n\n51\n00:03:12.132 --> 00:03:17.052\nSecond action: making tea.\n\n52\n00:03:17.132 --> 00:03:23.972\nI am here to make tea.\n\n53\n00:03:24.132 --> 00:03:29.153\nI am here to make tea.\n\n54\n00:03:29.193 --> 00:03:33.813\nThird action: helping you.\n\n55\n00:03:33.873 --> 00:03:39.993\nI come to help you.\n\n56\n00:03:40.133 --> 00:03:43.193\nI come to help you.\n\n57\n00:03:43.213 --> 00:03:46.294\nGreat job on these sentences!\n\n58\n00:03:46.414 --> 00:03:50.754\nNow, let's visit the Error Clinic!\n\n59\n00:03:50.814 --> 00:03:54.094\nHere is a common beginner mistake.\n\n60\n00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:57.574\nSome people forget the word TE.\n\n61\n00:03:57.594 --> 00:04:00.815\nThey say this incorrect sentence:\n\n62\n00:04:00.875 --> 00:04:03.615\nI am here to learn.\n\n63\n00:04:03.755 --> 00:04:05.955\nI am here to learn.\n\n64\n00:04:05.995 --> 00:04:07.895\nWhat is wrong here?\n\n65\n00:04:07.935 --> 00:04:11.995\nThe word TE is missing before leren!\n\n66\n00:04:12.015 --> 00:04:14.235\nThe correct sentence is:\n\n67\n00:04:14.315 --> 00:04:17.416\nI am here to learn.\n\n68\n00:04:17.536 --> 00:04:19.796\nI am here to learn.\n\n69\n00:04:19.896 --> 00:04:23.416\nAnother mistake is wrong word order.\n\n70\n00:04:23.456 --> 00:04:27.276\nBeginners put TE in wrong places.\n\n71\n00:04:27.296 --> 00:04:28.716\nThey say:\n\n72\n00:04:28.796 --> 00:04:30.916\nI go to eat an apple.\n\n73\n00:04:30.936 --> 00:04:32.416\nI go to eat an apple.\n\n74\n00:04:32.436 --> 00:04:34.237\nThat is wrong!\n\n75\n00:04:34.257 --> 00:04:38.917\nPut extra words inside om... te!\n\n76\n00:04:38.977 --> 00:04:41.437\nHere is the correct sentence:\n\n77\n00:04:41.517 --> 00:04:45.317\nI am going to eat an apple.\n\n78\n00:04:45.437 --> 00:04:48.277\nI am going to eat an apple.\n\n79\n00:04:48.337 --> 00:04:51.138\nRemember this simple rule:\n\n80\n00:04:51.218 --> 00:04:54.598\nOM starts the reason structure.\n\n81\n00:04:54.638 --> 00:04:57.878\nTE goes right before the verb.\n\n82\n00:04:57.938 --> 00:05:01.078\nAnd the verb ends the phrase!\n\n83\n00:05:01.138 --> 00:05:06.859\nMemory tip: OM... TE split the reason!\n\n84\n00:05:06.879 --> 00:05:09.859\nTime for our final summary!\n\n85\n00:05:09.919 --> 00:05:12.739\nToday we learned:\n\n86\n00:05:12.799 --> 00:05:17.239\nUse om te with a verb.\n\n87\n00:05:17.279 --> 00:05:20.979\nUse om te with a verb.\n\n88\n00:05:21.039 --> 00:05:24.600\nThe verb comes at the end.\n\n89\n00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:27.640\nThe verb comes at the end.\n\n90\n00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:29.940\nFantastic work today!\n\n91\n00:05:30.020 --> 00:05:31.080\nSee you next time!\n
grammar_past_tense_zijn	nl	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.420\nImagine telling a friend about your trip!\n\n2\n00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:06.900\nYou want to say: I went home.\n\n3\n00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:09.880\nIn English, you use simple past.\n\n4\n00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:13.500\nIn Dutch, we use present perfect.\n\n5\n00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.821\nUsually, Dutch uses the verb hebben.\n\n6\n00:00:16.861 --> 00:00:20.541\nBut for directional movement, use zijn!\n\n7\n00:00:20.581 --> 00:00:23.201\nLet's start with a simple sentence.\n\n8\n00:00:23.281 --> 00:00:25.521\nLaten we beginnen!\n\n9\n00:00:25.601 --> 00:00:26.981\nLet us begin!\n\n10\n00:00:27.021 --> 00:00:29.801\nFirst, look at the verb gaan.\n\n11\n00:00:29.841 --> 00:00:33.201\nIn the past, it becomes gegaan.\n\n12\n00:00:33.261 --> 00:00:36.121\nIk ben naar huis gegaan.\n\n13\n00:00:36.181 --> 00:00:37.681\nI went home.\n\n14\n00:00:37.721 --> 00:00:41.262\nNotice we use ben, not heb!\n\n15\n00:00:41.322 --> 00:00:44.342\nWhy? Because you moved somewhere!\n\n16\n00:00:44.382 --> 00:00:46.642\nNow let's try another person.\n\n17\n00:00:46.702 --> 00:00:49.562\nHij is naar school gegaan.\n\n18\n00:00:49.602 --> 00:00:51.122\nHe went to school.\n\n19\n00:00:51.142 --> 00:00:52.962\nNow, can you guess next?\n\n20\n00:00:53.002 --> 00:00:56.142\nHow do you say: We went home?\n\n21\n00:00:56.182 --> 00:01:01.522\nThe answer uses zijn for wij.\n\n22\n00:01:01.582 --> 00:01:06.522\nWij zijn naar huis gegaan.\n\n23\n00:01:06.582 --> 00:01:08.202\nWe went home.\n\n24\n00:01:08.262 --> 00:01:10.423\nGreat job!\n\n25\n00:01:10.583 --> 00:01:15.943\nNext verb: coming somewhere, or komen.\n\n26\n00:01:16.003 --> 00:01:20.363\nThe past participle is gekomen.\n\n27\n00:01:20.443 --> 00:01:24.303\nZij is laat gekomen.\n\n28\n00:01:24.363 --> 00:01:27.063\nShe came late.\n\n29\n00:01:27.143 --> 00:01:31.023\nJan is thuis gekomen.\n\n30\n00:01:31.083 --> 00:01:33.903\nJan came home.\n\n31\n00:01:33.943 --> 00:01:38.204\nWhat about the verb zijn itself?\n\n32\n00:01:38.244 --> 00:01:42.284\nThe past participle is geweest.\n\n33\n00:01:42.344 --> 00:01:46.244\nAnd it always takes zijn!\n\n34\n00:01:46.304 --> 00:01:50.484\nIk ben in Amsterdam geweest.\n\n35\n00:01:50.644 --> 00:01:54.104\nI have been in Amsterdam.\n\n36\n00:01:54.144 --> 00:01:58.204\nWij zijn in Spanje geweest.\n\n37\n00:01:58.264 --> 00:02:01.624\nWe have been in Spain.\n\n38\n00:02:01.744 --> 00:02:04.665\nLet's pause and think!\n\n39\n00:02:04.765 --> 00:02:08.305\nHow would you say: You were here?\n\n40\n00:02:08.525 --> 00:02:13.145\nUse je and geweest.\n\n41\n00:02:13.205 --> 00:02:15.705\nHere is the answer:\n\n42\n00:02:15.845 --> 00:02:20.725\nJe bent hier geweest.\n\n43\n00:02:20.785 --> 00:02:23.745\nYou have been here.\n\n44\n00:02:23.765 --> 00:02:28.185\nPerfect! Let's do a pattern drill.\n\n45\n00:02:28.205 --> 00:02:32.806\nPattern one: moving to a place.\n\n46\n00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:40.166\nIk ben naar de winkel gegaan.\n\n47\n00:02:40.246 --> 00:02:42.946\nI went to the store.\n\n48\n00:02:42.966 --> 00:02:47.686\nPattern two: arriving somewhere.\n\n49\n00:02:47.746 --> 00:02:54.806\nHij is in Utrecht gekomen.\n\n50\n00:02:54.866 --> 00:02:58.107\nHe arrived in Utrecht.\n\n51\n00:02:58.147 --> 00:03:02.887\nPattern three: being somewhere.\n\n52\n00:03:02.947 --> 00:03:11.107\nZij is in de tuin geweest.\n\n53\n00:03:11.127 --> 00:03:14.247\nShe has been in the garden.\n\n54\n00:03:14.267 --> 00:03:18.987\nPattern four: traveling somewhere.\n\n55\n00:03:19.027 --> 00:03:26.468\nWij zijn naar Bosnië gegaan.\n\n56\n00:03:26.488 --> 00:03:29.568\nWe went to Bosnia.\n\n57\n00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:32.288\nNow time for our error clinic!\n\n58\n00:03:32.328 --> 00:03:37.668\nCommon mistake one: using hebben with gaan.\n\n59\n00:03:37.708 --> 00:03:41.568\nWrong: Ik heb naar huis gegaan.\n\n60\n00:03:41.608 --> 00:03:43.948\nWhy is this wrong?\n\n61\n00:03:44.008 --> 00:03:47.688\nMovement to a goal needs zijn!\n\n62\n00:03:47.728 --> 00:03:49.788\nCorrect version:\n\n63\n00:03:49.848 --> 00:03:53.069\nIk ben naar huis gegaan.\n\n64\n00:03:53.209 --> 00:03:54.989\nI went home.\n\n65\n00:03:55.009 --> 00:04:00.749\nCommon mistake two: using hebben with geweest.\n\n66\n00:04:00.809 --> 00:04:04.229\nWrong: Ik heb daar geweest.\n\n67\n00:04:04.269 --> 00:04:06.249\nCorrect version:\n\n68\n00:04:06.309 --> 00:04:09.229\nIk ben daar geweest.\n\n69\n00:04:09.409 --> 00:04:11.069\nI have been there.\n\n70\n00:04:11.129 --> 00:04:13.849\nHere is your memory anchor:\n\n71\n00:04:13.889 --> 00:04:18.849\nMovement to a destination always uses zijn!\n\n72\n00:04:18.929 --> 00:04:20.910\nSpoken recap:\n\n73\n00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.810\nToday we learned:\n\n74\n00:04:22.950 --> 00:04:28.730\nUse zijn with gaan, komen, and geweest.\n\n75\n00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:31.530\nBedankt en tot ziens!\n\n76\n00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:33.570\nThank you and see you soon!\n
grammar_past_tense_zijn	en	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.420\nImagine telling a friend about your trip!\n\n2\n00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.900\nYou want to say: I went home.\n\n3\n00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:10.880\nIn English, you use simple past.\n\n4\n00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:14.500\nIn Dutch, we use present perfect.\n\n5\n00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.821\nUsually, Dutch uses the verb hebben.\n\n6\n00:00:17.861 --> 00:00:21.541\nBut for directional movement, use zijn!\n\n7\n00:00:21.581 --> 00:00:24.201\nLet's start with a simple sentence.\n\n8\n00:00:24.281 --> 00:00:26.521\nLet us begin!\n\n9\n00:00:26.601 --> 00:00:27.981\nLet us begin!\n\n10\n00:00:28.021 --> 00:00:30.801\nFirst, look at the verb gaan.\n\n11\n00:00:30.841 --> 00:00:34.201\nIn the past, it becomes gegaan.\n\n12\n00:00:34.261 --> 00:00:37.121\nI went home.\n\n13\n00:00:37.181 --> 00:00:38.681\nI went home.\n\n14\n00:00:38.721 --> 00:00:42.262\nNotice we use ben, not heb!\n\n15\n00:00:42.322 --> 00:00:45.342\nWhy? Because you moved somewhere!\n\n16\n00:00:45.382 --> 00:00:47.642\nNow let's try another person.\n\n17\n00:00:47.702 --> 00:00:50.562\nHe went to school.\n\n18\n00:00:50.602 --> 00:00:52.122\nHe went to school.\n\n19\n00:00:52.142 --> 00:00:53.962\nNow, can you guess next?\n\n20\n00:00:54.002 --> 00:00:57.142\nHow do you say: We went home?\n\n21\n00:00:57.182 --> 00:01:02.522\nThe answer uses zijn for wij.\n\n22\n00:01:02.582 --> 00:01:07.522\nWe went home.\n\n23\n00:01:07.582 --> 00:01:09.202\nWe went home.\n\n24\n00:01:09.262 --> 00:01:11.423\nGreat job!\n\n25\n00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:16.943\nNext verb: coming somewhere, or komen.\n\n26\n00:01:17.003 --> 00:01:21.363\nThe past participle is gekomen.\n\n27\n00:01:21.443 --> 00:01:25.303\nShe came late.\n\n28\n00:01:25.363 --> 00:01:28.063\nShe came late.\n\n29\n00:01:28.143 --> 00:01:32.023\nJan came home.\n\n30\n00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:34.903\nJan came home.\n\n31\n00:01:34.943 --> 00:01:39.204\nWhat about the verb zijn itself?\n\n32\n00:01:39.244 --> 00:01:43.284\nThe past participle is geweest.\n\n33\n00:01:43.344 --> 00:01:47.244\nAnd it always takes zijn!\n\n34\n00:01:47.304 --> 00:01:51.484\nI have been in Amsterdam.\n\n35\n00:01:51.644 --> 00:01:55.104\nI have been in Amsterdam.\n\n36\n00:01:55.144 --> 00:01:59.204\nWe have been in Spain.\n\n37\n00:01:59.264 --> 00:02:02.624\nWe have been in Spain.\n\n38\n00:02:02.744 --> 00:02:05.665\nLet's pause and think!\n\n39\n00:02:05.765 --> 00:02:09.305\nHow would you say: You were here?\n\n40\n00:02:09.525 --> 00:02:14.145\nUse je and geweest.\n\n41\n00:02:14.205 --> 00:02:16.705\nHere is the answer:\n\n42\n00:02:16.845 --> 00:02:21.725\nYou have been here.\n\n43\n00:02:21.785 --> 00:02:24.745\nYou have been here.\n\n44\n00:02:24.765 --> 00:02:29.185\nPerfect! Let's do a pattern drill.\n\n45\n00:02:29.205 --> 00:02:33.806\nPattern one: moving to a place.\n\n46\n00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:41.166\nI went to the store.\n\n47\n00:02:41.246 --> 00:02:43.946\nI went to the store.\n\n48\n00:02:43.966 --> 00:02:48.686\nPattern two: arriving somewhere.\n\n49\n00:02:48.746 --> 00:02:55.806\nHe arrived in Utrecht.\n\n50\n00:02:55.866 --> 00:02:59.107\nHe arrived in Utrecht.\n\n51\n00:02:59.147 --> 00:03:03.887\nPattern three: being somewhere.\n\n52\n00:03:03.947 --> 00:03:12.107\nShe has been in the garden.\n\n53\n00:03:12.127 --> 00:03:15.247\nShe has been in the garden.\n\n54\n00:03:15.267 --> 00:03:19.987\nPattern four: traveling somewhere.\n\n55\n00:03:20.027 --> 00:03:27.468\nWe went to Bosnia.\n\n56\n00:03:27.488 --> 00:03:30.568\nWe went to Bosnia.\n\n57\n00:03:30.608 --> 00:03:33.288\nNow time for our error clinic!\n\n58\n00:03:33.328 --> 00:03:38.668\nCommon mistake one: using hebben with gaan.\n\n59\n00:03:38.708 --> 00:03:42.568\nWrong: Ik heb naar huis gegaan.\n\n60\n00:03:42.608 --> 00:03:44.948\nWhy is this wrong?\n\n61\n00:03:45.008 --> 00:03:48.688\nMovement to a goal needs zijn!\n\n62\n00:03:48.728 --> 00:03:50.788\nCorrect version:\n\n63\n00:03:50.848 --> 00:03:54.069\nI went home.\n\n64\n00:03:54.209 --> 00:03:55.989\nI went home.\n\n65\n00:03:56.009 --> 00:04:01.749\nCommon mistake two: using hebben with geweest.\n\n66\n00:04:01.809 --> 00:04:05.229\nWrong: Ik heb daar geweest.\n\n67\n00:04:05.269 --> 00:04:07.249\nCorrect version:\n\n68\n00:04:07.309 --> 00:04:10.229\nI have been there.\n\n69\n00:04:10.409 --> 00:04:12.069\nI have been there.\n\n70\n00:04:12.129 --> 00:04:14.849\nHere is your memory anchor:\n\n71\n00:04:14.889 --> 00:04:19.849\nMovement to a destination always uses zijn!\n\n72\n00:04:19.929 --> 00:04:21.910\nSpoken recap:\n\n73\n00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.810\nToday we learned:\n\n74\n00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:29.730\nUse zijn with gaan, komen, and geweest.\n\n75\n00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:32.530\nThank you and see you soon!\n\n76\n00:04:32.590 --> 00:04:34.570\nThank you and see you soon!\n
grammar_perfect_tense	en	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:04.620\nImagine talking to a Dutch friend.\n\n2\n00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:07.240\nThey ask what you did yesterday.\n\n3\n00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:10.240\nYou need the perfect tense!\n\n4\n00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.481\nIn English, you say: I worked.\n\n5\n00:00:13.541 --> 00:00:17.081\nIn Dutch, you use two verbs.\n\n6\n00:00:17.121 --> 00:00:21.801\nFirst, an auxiliary like hebben or zijn.\n\n7\n00:00:21.881 --> 00:00:26.161\nSecond, a past participle with ge-.\n\n8\n00:00:26.241 --> 00:00:29.141\nLet's look at our first sentence!\n\n9\n00:00:29.201 --> 00:00:31.982\nLet's start!\n\n10\n00:00:32.122 --> 00:00:33.882\nLet's start!\n\n11\n00:00:33.962 --> 00:00:37.962\nFirst verb: werken, to work.\n\n12\n00:00:38.022 --> 00:00:40.942\nI have worked.\n\n13\n00:00:41.202 --> 00:00:43.202\nI have worked.\n\n14\n00:00:43.262 --> 00:00:47.842\nNotice 'gewerkt' has ge- at the start.\n\n15\n00:00:47.882 --> 00:00:51.283\nNow let me add a word: yesterday.\n\n16\n00:00:51.343 --> 00:00:55.583\nIn Dutch, yesterday is gisteren.\n\n17\n00:00:55.663 --> 00:00:59.143\nI worked yesterday.\n\n18\n00:00:59.343 --> 00:01:01.703\nI worked yesterday.\n\n19\n00:01:01.723 --> 00:01:05.403\nThe ge-verb goes to the end!\n\n20\n00:01:05.523 --> 00:01:09.204\nLet's try with another subject: she.\n\n21\n00:01:09.304 --> 00:01:12.164\nShe worked yesterday.\n\n22\n00:01:12.244 --> 00:01:15.824\nShe worked yesterday.\n\n23\n00:01:15.884 --> 00:01:19.284\nCan you guess the next sentence?\n\n24\n00:01:19.344 --> 00:01:23.944\nHow do you say: We worked?\n\n25\n00:01:23.984 --> 00:01:27.025\nThink about it for a second!\n\n26\n00:01:27.065 --> 00:01:30.305\nHere is the answer:\n\n27\n00:01:30.485 --> 00:01:35.765\nWe have worked.\n\n28\n00:01:35.785 --> 00:01:39.185\nWe have worked.\n\n29\n00:01:39.225 --> 00:01:43.485\nGreat job! Now another verb.\n\n30\n00:01:43.525 --> 00:01:49.626\nThe verb koken means to cook.\n\n31\n00:01:49.706 --> 00:01:54.886\nI have cooked.\n\n32\n00:01:55.106 --> 00:01:58.106\nI have cooked.\n\n33\n00:01:58.186 --> 00:02:02.626\nLet me add a word: soup!\n\n34\n00:02:02.686 --> 00:02:09.027\nHe has cooked soup.\n\n35\n00:02:09.087 --> 00:02:12.527\nHe has cooked soup.\n\n36\n00:02:12.567 --> 00:02:15.087\nNow it is your turn again.\n\n37\n00:02:15.127 --> 00:02:19.587\nHow would you say: You cooked?\n\n38\n00:02:19.607 --> 00:02:22.728\nTake a moment to guess!\n\n39\n00:02:22.788 --> 00:02:26.688\nHere is the correct sentence:\n\n40\n00:02:26.748 --> 00:02:32.028\nYou have cooked.\n\n41\n00:02:32.148 --> 00:02:36.868\nYou have cooked.\n\n42\n00:02:36.968 --> 00:02:41.209\nExcellent! What about movement verbs?\n\n43\n00:02:41.589 --> 00:02:45.869\nVerbs of movement use ZIJN!\n\n44\n00:02:45.909 --> 00:02:50.309\nFor example, the verb gaan.\n\n45\n00:02:50.389 --> 00:02:54.889\nI have gone.\n\n46\n00:02:55.069 --> 00:02:58.009\nI have gone.\n\n47\n00:02:58.109 --> 00:03:02.850\nAnother example is the verb komen.\n\n48\n00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:07.970\nHe has come.\n\n49\n00:03:08.010 --> 00:03:11.450\nHe has come.\n\n50\n00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.010\nLet's add a destination!\n\n51\n00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:22.271\nWe went home.\n\n52\n00:03:22.331 --> 00:03:25.471\nWe went home.\n\n53\n00:03:25.511 --> 00:03:28.311\nTime for a quick pattern drill!\n\n54\n00:03:28.371 --> 00:03:31.971\nRepeat after each sentence.\n\n55\n00:03:32.031 --> 00:03:36.751\nI have listened.\n\n56\n00:03:36.951 --> 00:03:40.092\nI have listened.\n\n57\n00:03:40.152 --> 00:03:44.712\nI have played.\n\n58\n00:03:44.892 --> 00:03:48.052\nI have played.\n\n59\n00:03:48.112 --> 00:03:52.572\nI have learned.\n\n60\n00:03:52.772 --> 00:03:55.652\nI have learned.\n\n61\n00:03:55.712 --> 00:03:58.913\nI have made.\n\n62\n00:03:59.193 --> 00:04:02.993\nI have made.\n\n63\n00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:08.353\nNow, let's enter the error clinic!\n\n64\n00:04:08.393 --> 00:04:14.793\nMistake number one: forgetting ge-.\n\n65\n00:04:14.833 --> 00:04:19.994\nBeginner mistake: Ik heb werkt.\n\n66\n00:04:20.034 --> 00:04:23.354\nWhat went wrong here?\n\n67\n00:04:23.434 --> 00:04:28.254\nYou forgot the prefix ge-!\n\n68\n00:04:28.274 --> 00:04:30.794\nThe correct version is:\n\n69\n00:04:30.874 --> 00:04:34.835\nI have worked.\n\n70\n00:04:35.095 --> 00:04:38.775\nI have worked.\n\n71\n00:04:38.815 --> 00:04:45.015\nMistake number two: wrong verb position.\n\n72\n00:04:45.075 --> 00:04:50.155\nBeginner mistake: Ik gewerkt heb.\n\n73\n00:04:50.195 --> 00:04:53.596\nWhat is wrong with this?\n\n74\n00:04:53.616 --> 00:04:58.516\nThe ge-verb must go last!\n\n75\n00:04:58.596 --> 00:05:01.156\nLet's fix it together:\n\n76\n00:05:01.216 --> 00:05:05.916\nI worked yesterday.\n\n77\n00:05:06.136 --> 00:05:09.696\nI worked yesterday.\n\n78\n00:05:09.736 --> 00:05:15.897\nMistake number three: wrong auxiliary verb.\n\n79\n00:05:15.937 --> 00:05:21.057\nBeginner mistake: Ik heb gegaan.\n\n80\n00:05:21.197 --> 00:05:27.337\nGaan is movement, so use zijn!\n\n81\n00:05:27.397 --> 00:05:30.417\nHere is the correct sentence:\n\n82\n00:05:30.497 --> 00:05:34.358\nI have gone.\n\n83\n00:05:34.618 --> 00:05:38.018\nI have gone.\n\n84\n00:05:38.078 --> 00:05:40.658\nHere is your memory anchor:\n\n85\n00:05:40.718 --> 00:05:45.378\nAuxiliary second, ge-verb at the end!\n\n86\n00:05:45.438 --> 00:05:50.479\nHebben for actions, zijn for movement!\n\n87\n00:05:50.499 --> 00:05:53.699\nLet me give a brief recap.\n\n88\n00:05:53.759 --> 00:06:00.559\nToday we learned the perfect tense.\n\n89\n00:06:00.579 --> 00:06:05.039\nToday we learned the perfect tense.\n\n90\n00:06:05.079 --> 00:06:12.380\nCombine hebben or zijn with ge-verb!\n\n91\n00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:16.240\nPractice this structure every day.\n\n92\n00:06:16.280 --> 00:06:17.700\nSee you next lesson!\n
grammar_prepositions_place	nl	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.460\nImagine you are looking for your keys.\n\n2\n00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:07.520\nAre they on the table?\n\n3\n00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.001\nOr under the couch?\n\n4\n00:00:11.041 --> 00:00:16.221\nTo say where things are, use prepositions!\n\n5\n00:00:16.281 --> 00:00:21.421\nIn English, we say in or on.\n\n6\n00:00:21.461 --> 00:00:25.782\nDutch uses prepositions of place too.\n\n7\n00:00:25.882 --> 00:00:28.522\nLaten we beginnen!\n\n8\n00:00:28.622 --> 00:00:31.202\nLet us begin!\n\n9\n00:00:31.302 --> 00:00:35.082\nFirst, let us look at IN.\n\n10\n00:00:35.122 --> 00:00:39.803\nDe sleutel zit in de tas.\n\n11\n00:00:39.843 --> 00:00:43.983\nThe key is in the bag.\n\n12\n00:00:44.023 --> 00:00:47.823\nNext, let us look at OP.\n\n13\n00:00:47.843 --> 00:00:52.523\nDe sleutel ligt op tafel.\n\n14\n00:00:52.543 --> 00:00:56.464\nThe key is on the table.\n\n15\n00:00:56.504 --> 00:01:00.304\nOP means on top of something.\n\n16\n00:01:00.384 --> 00:01:04.464\nIN means inside of something.\n\n17\n00:01:04.484 --> 00:01:07.604\nNow let us try ONDER.\n\n18\n00:01:07.664 --> 00:01:11.245\nDe kat zit onder de stoel.\n\n19\n00:01:11.265 --> 00:01:14.805\nThe cat sits under the chair.\n\n20\n00:01:14.885 --> 00:01:20.805\nONDER means under or below.\n\n21\n00:01:20.845 --> 00:01:25.385\nNow, what about BOVEN?\n\n22\n00:01:25.425 --> 00:01:31.626\nDe lamp hangt boven de tafel.\n\n23\n00:01:31.646 --> 00:01:36.066\nThe lamp hangs above the table.\n\n24\n00:01:36.106 --> 00:01:41.466\nBOVEN means above or over.\n\n25\n00:01:41.486 --> 00:01:44.207\nTime for a quick question!\n\n26\n00:01:44.247 --> 00:01:47.327\nHow do you say under?\n\n27\n00:01:47.387 --> 00:01:52.727\nIs it ONDER or BOVEN?\n\n28\n00:01:52.747 --> 00:01:57.407\nThe answer is ONDER!\n\n29\n00:01:57.427 --> 00:02:02.428\nNow let us learn VOOR.\n\n30\n00:02:02.488 --> 00:02:08.208\nDe hond staat voor het huis.\n\n31\n00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:13.188\nThe dog stands in front of the house.\n\n32\n00:02:13.228 --> 00:02:17.689\nVOOR means in front of.\n\n33\n00:02:17.749 --> 00:02:23.329\nOpposite of VOOR is ACHTER.\n\n34\n00:02:23.349 --> 00:02:28.809\nDe hond staat achter het huis.\n\n35\n00:02:28.829 --> 00:02:33.750\nThe dog stands behind the house.\n\n36\n00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:38.410\nACHTER means behind.\n\n37\n00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:41.650\nNext, let us learn NAAST.\n\n38\n00:02:41.710 --> 00:02:44.130\nIk sta naast mijn vriend.\n\n39\n00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.111\nI stand next to my friend.\n\n40\n00:02:47.151 --> 00:02:50.211\nNAAST means next to.\n\n41\n00:02:50.291 --> 00:02:53.651\nFinally, let us look at TUSSEN.\n\n42\n00:02:53.711 --> 00:02:56.611\nHet boek ligt tussen twee boeken.\n\n43\n00:02:56.631 --> 00:02:59.971\nThe book lies between two books.\n\n44\n00:03:00.011 --> 00:03:02.712\nTUSSEN means between.\n\n45\n00:03:02.752 --> 00:03:04.292\nCan you guess this one?\n\n46\n00:03:04.352 --> 00:03:06.712\nHow do you say next to?\n\n47\n00:03:06.732 --> 00:03:09.372\nThe answer is NAAST!\n\n48\n00:03:09.432 --> 00:03:12.032\nLet us drill these patterns together!\n\n49\n00:03:12.072 --> 00:03:14.532\nDe man zit op de stoel.\n\n50\n00:03:14.552 --> 00:03:16.933\nThe man sits on the chair.\n\n51\n00:03:16.973 --> 00:03:19.493\nDe man zit in de auto.\n\n52\n00:03:19.533 --> 00:03:21.933\nThe man sits in the car.\n\n53\n00:03:21.973 --> 00:03:24.473\nDe man staat voor de deur.\n\n54\n00:03:24.493 --> 00:03:27.153\nThe man stands at the door.\n\n55\n00:03:27.193 --> 00:03:31.493\nDe man staat naast de tafel.\n\n56\n00:03:31.513 --> 00:03:35.774\nThe man stands next to the table.\n\n57\n00:03:35.814 --> 00:03:40.334\nDe man staat achter de boom.\n\n58\n00:03:40.354 --> 00:03:44.654\nThe man stands behind the tree.\n\n59\n00:03:44.694 --> 00:03:47.954\nNow for our Error Clinic!\n\n60\n00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:51.175\nHere is a common mistake.\n\n61\n00:03:51.215 --> 00:03:53.555\nDo not say:\n\n62\n00:03:53.595 --> 00:03:57.455\nDe kat is op het huis.\n\n63\n00:03:57.495 --> 00:04:01.095\nThe cat is on the house.\n\n64\n00:04:01.155 --> 00:04:04.696\nIf it is inside, say IN!\n\n65\n00:04:04.756 --> 00:04:06.336\nCorrect:\n\n66\n00:04:06.376 --> 00:04:10.036\nDe kat is in het huis.\n\n67\n00:04:10.056 --> 00:04:14.116\nThe cat is in the house.\n\n68\n00:04:14.136 --> 00:04:16.116\nAnother common mistake:\n\n69\n00:04:16.156 --> 00:04:19.716\nConfusing VOOR and ACHTER.\n\n70\n00:04:19.777 --> 00:04:23.917\nVOOR is front, ACHTER is back.\n\n71\n00:04:23.937 --> 00:04:26.537\nHere is your memory anchor.\n\n72\n00:04:26.597 --> 00:04:30.157\nThink: VOOR starts like front.\n\n73\n00:04:30.257 --> 00:04:34.237\nAnd ACHTER means after or behind.\n\n74\n00:04:34.277 --> 00:04:37.518\nTime for our quick recap!\n\n75\n00:04:37.578 --> 00:04:42.858\nVandaag leerden we voorzetsels van plaats.\n\n76\n00:04:42.878 --> 00:04:47.478\nToday we learned prepositions of place.\n\n77\n00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:59.839\nIn, op, onder, boven, voor, achter, naast, tussen.\n\n78\n00:04:59.919 --> 00:05:11.000\nIn, on, under, above, front, behind, next, between.\n\n79\n00:05:11.020 --> 00:05:14.820\nGreat job! Keep practicing every day!\n
grammar_prepositions_place	en	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:05.460\nImagine you are looking for your keys.\n\n2\n00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.520\nAre they on the table?\n\n3\n00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.001\nOr under the couch?\n\n4\n00:00:12.041 --> 00:00:17.221\nTo say where things are, use prepositions!\n\n5\n00:00:17.281 --> 00:00:22.421\nIn English, we say in or on.\n\n6\n00:00:22.461 --> 00:00:26.782\nDutch uses prepositions of place too.\n\n7\n00:00:26.882 --> 00:00:29.522\nLet us begin!\n\n8\n00:00:29.622 --> 00:00:32.202\nLet us begin!\n\n9\n00:00:32.302 --> 00:00:36.082\nFirst, let us look at IN.\n\n10\n00:00:36.122 --> 00:00:40.803\nThe key is in the bag.\n\n11\n00:00:40.843 --> 00:00:44.983\nThe key is in the bag.\n\n12\n00:00:45.023 --> 00:00:48.823\nNext, let us look at OP.\n\n13\n00:00:48.843 --> 00:00:53.523\nThe key is on the table.\n\n14\n00:00:53.543 --> 00:00:57.464\nThe key is on the table.\n\n15\n00:00:57.504 --> 00:01:01.304\nOP means on top of something.\n\n16\n00:01:01.384 --> 00:01:05.464\nIN means inside of something.\n\n17\n00:01:05.484 --> 00:01:08.604\nNow let us try ONDER.\n\n18\n00:01:08.664 --> 00:01:12.245\nThe cat sits under the chair.\n\n19\n00:01:12.265 --> 00:01:15.805\nThe cat sits under the chair.\n\n20\n00:01:15.885 --> 00:01:21.805\nONDER means under or below.\n\n21\n00:01:21.845 --> 00:01:26.385\nNow, what about BOVEN?\n\n22\n00:01:26.425 --> 00:01:32.626\nThe lamp hangs above the table.\n\n23\n00:01:32.646 --> 00:01:37.066\nThe lamp hangs above the table.\n\n24\n00:01:37.106 --> 00:01:42.466\nBOVEN means above or over.\n\n25\n00:01:42.486 --> 00:01:45.207\nTime for a quick question!\n\n26\n00:01:45.247 --> 00:01:48.327\nHow do you say under?\n\n27\n00:01:48.387 --> 00:01:53.727\nIs it ONDER or BOVEN?\n\n28\n00:01:53.747 --> 00:01:58.407\nThe answer is ONDER!\n\n29\n00:01:58.427 --> 00:02:03.428\nNow let us learn VOOR.\n\n30\n00:02:03.488 --> 00:02:09.208\nThe dog stands in front of the house.\n\n31\n00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:14.188\nThe dog stands in front of the house.\n\n32\n00:02:14.228 --> 00:02:18.689\nVOOR means in front of.\n\n33\n00:02:18.749 --> 00:02:24.329\nOpposite of VOOR is ACHTER.\n\n34\n00:02:24.349 --> 00:02:29.809\nThe dog stands behind the house.\n\n35\n00:02:29.829 --> 00:02:34.750\nThe dog stands behind the house.\n\n36\n00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:39.410\nACHTER means behind.\n\n37\n00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.650\nNext, let us learn NAAST.\n\n38\n00:02:42.710 --> 00:02:45.130\nI stand next to my friend.\n\n39\n00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:48.111\nI stand next to my friend.\n\n40\n00:02:48.151 --> 00:02:51.211\nNAAST means next to.\n\n41\n00:02:51.291 --> 00:02:54.651\nFinally, let us look at TUSSEN.\n\n42\n00:02:54.711 --> 00:02:57.611\nThe book lies between two books.\n\n43\n00:02:57.631 --> 00:03:00.971\nThe book lies between two books.\n\n44\n00:03:01.011 --> 00:03:03.712\nTUSSEN means between.\n\n45\n00:03:03.752 --> 00:03:05.292\nCan you guess this one?\n\n46\n00:03:05.352 --> 00:03:07.712\nHow do you say next to?\n\n47\n00:03:07.732 --> 00:03:10.372\nThe answer is NAAST!\n\n48\n00:03:10.432 --> 00:03:13.032\nLet us drill these patterns together!\n\n49\n00:03:13.072 --> 00:03:15.532\nThe man sits on the chair.\n\n50\n00:03:15.552 --> 00:03:17.933\nThe man sits on the chair.\n\n51\n00:03:17.973 --> 00:03:20.493\nThe man sits in the car.\n\n52\n00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.933\nThe man sits in the car.\n\n53\n00:03:22.973 --> 00:03:25.473\nThe man stands at the door.\n\n54\n00:03:25.493 --> 00:03:28.153\nThe man stands at the door.\n\n55\n00:03:28.193 --> 00:03:32.493\nThe man stands next to the table.\n\n56\n00:03:32.513 --> 00:03:36.774\nThe man stands next to the table.\n\n57\n00:03:36.814 --> 00:03:41.334\nThe man stands behind the tree.\n\n58\n00:03:41.354 --> 00:03:45.654\nThe man stands behind the tree.\n\n59\n00:03:45.694 --> 00:03:48.954\nNow for our Error Clinic!\n\n60\n00:03:48.994 --> 00:03:52.175\nHere is a common mistake.\n\n61\n00:03:52.215 --> 00:03:54.555\nDo not say:\n\n62\n00:03:54.595 --> 00:03:58.455\nThe cat is on the house.\n\n63\n00:03:58.495 --> 00:04:02.095\nThe cat is on the house.\n\n64\n00:04:02.155 --> 00:04:05.696\nIf it is inside, say IN!\n\n65\n00:04:05.756 --> 00:04:07.336\nCorrect:\n\n66\n00:04:07.376 --> 00:04:11.036\nThe cat is in the house.\n\n67\n00:04:11.056 --> 00:04:15.116\nThe cat is in the house.\n\n68\n00:04:15.136 --> 00:04:17.116\nAnother common mistake:\n\n69\n00:04:17.156 --> 00:04:20.716\nConfusing VOOR and ACHTER.\n\n70\n00:04:20.777 --> 00:04:24.917\nVOOR is front, ACHTER is back.\n\n71\n00:04:24.937 --> 00:04:27.537\nHere is your memory anchor.\n\n72\n00:04:27.597 --> 00:04:31.157\nThink: VOOR starts like front.\n\n73\n00:04:31.257 --> 00:04:35.237\nAnd ACHTER means after or behind.\n\n74\n00:04:35.277 --> 00:04:38.518\nTime for our quick recap!\n\n75\n00:04:38.578 --> 00:04:43.858\nToday we learned prepositions of place.\n\n76\n00:04:43.878 --> 00:04:48.478\nToday we learned prepositions of place.\n\n77\n00:04:48.578 --> 00:05:00.839\nIn, on, under, above, front, behind, next, between.\n\n78\n00:05:00.919 --> 00:05:12.000\nIn, on, under, above, front, behind, next, between.\n\n79\n00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.820\nGreat job! Keep practicing every day!\n
grammar_prepositions_time	nl	WEBVTT\n\n1\n00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:03.780\nImagine meeting a friend at eight o'clock.\n\n2\n00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.340\nYou need to use the right Dutch word!\n\n3\n00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:13.181\nToday we learn prepositions of time in Dutch.\n\n4\n00:00:13.241 --> 00:00:16.681\nLaten we beginnen!\n\n5\n00:00:16.801 --> 00:00:19.721\nLet us begin!\n\n6\n00:00:19.801 --> 00:00:24.561\nFirst, we use OM for clock times.\n\n7\n00:00:24.641 --> 00:00:28.161\nIn English, you say AT eight o'clock.\n\n8\n00:00:28.221 --> 00:00:33.442\nIn Dutch, you use OM for times.\n\n9\n00:00:33.502 --> 00:00:38.082\nIk kom om acht uur.\n\n10\n00:00:38.282 --> 00:00:42.262\nI come at eight o'clock.\n\n11\n00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:46.802\nSecond, we use OP for days.\n\n12\n00:00:46.862 --> 00:00:50.363\nIn English, you say ON Monday.\n\n13\n00:00:50.443 --> 00:00:55.063\nIn Dutch, you also say OP.\n\n14\n00:00:55.083 --> 00:00:59.923\nWij spreken af op maandag.\n\n15\n00:00:59.963 --> 00:01:03.783\nWe meet on Monday.\n\n16\n00:01:03.843 --> 00:01:08.244\nThird, we use IN for months.\n\n17\n00:01:08.344 --> 00:01:09.864\nAlso for seasons and years.\n\n18\n00:01:09.884 --> 00:01:12.764\nHet feest is in juli.\n\n19\n00:01:12.784 --> 00:01:15.244\nThe party is in July.\n\n20\n00:01:15.364 --> 00:01:18.144\nLet me give you another example now.\n\n21\n00:01:18.184 --> 00:01:23.664\nDe trein vertrekt om drie uur.\n\n22\n00:01:23.684 --> 00:01:27.185\nThe train leaves at three o'clock.\n\n23\n00:01:27.225 --> 00:01:29.905\nNow let's try days of the week.\n\n24\n00:01:29.965 --> 00:01:33.305\nIk werk niet op zondag.\n\n25\n00:01:33.465 --> 00:01:36.445\nI do not work on Sunday.\n\n26\n00:01:36.505 --> 00:01:40.225\nNow let's talk about seasons with IN.\n\n27\n00:01:40.285 --> 00:01:44.025\nHet sneeuwt vaak in de winter.\n\n28\n00:01:44.085 --> 00:01:47.066\nIt often snows in the winter.\n\n29\n00:01:47.086 --> 00:01:49.106\nCan you guess the next word?\n\n30\n00:01:49.186 --> 00:01:52.586\nHow do you say AT nine o'clock?\n\n31\n00:01:52.686 --> 00:01:55.806\nThe answer uses the word OM.\n\n32\n00:01:55.846 --> 00:02:00.086\nDe les begint om negen uur.\n\n33\n00:02:00.106 --> 00:02:03.026\nThe lesson starts at nine o'clock.\n\n34\n00:02:03.166 --> 00:02:07.027\nNow, what about time ranges?\n\n35\n00:02:07.047 --> 00:02:12.147\nWe use VAN and TOT for ranges.\n\n36\n00:02:12.227 --> 00:02:18.187\nThis means FROM and UNTIL in English.\n\n37\n00:02:18.267 --> 00:02:24.208\nIk werk van negen tot vijf.\n\n38\n00:02:24.368 --> 00:02:28.368\nI work from nine till five.\n\n39\n00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:34.388\nIk leer van mei tot juni.\n\n40\n00:02:34.548 --> 00:02:38.768\nI learn from May to June.\n\n41\n00:02:38.788 --> 00:02:43.869\nNext, we have the preposition OVER.\n\n42\n00:02:43.929 --> 00:02:48.809\nOVER means IN for future time.\n\n43\n00:02:48.849 --> 00:02:53.489\nFor example, in ten minutes.\n\n44\n00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:58.589\nIk kom over tien minuten.\n\n45\n00:02:58.689 --> 00:03:02.650\nI come in ten minutes.\n\n46\n00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:08.090\nDe bus komt over vijf minuten.\n\n47\n00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:12.570\nThe bus arrives in five minutes.\n\n48\n00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:15.970\nTime for a quick pause and prompt!\n\n49\n00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:19.211\nHow would you say ON Friday?\n\n50\n00:03:19.331 --> 00:03:24.171\nRemember, for days we use OP.\n\n51\n00:03:24.251 --> 00:03:27.731\nIk zie je op vrijdag.\n\n52\n00:03:27.851 --> 00:03:30.431\nI see you on Friday.\n\n53\n00:03:30.451 --> 00:03:33.891\nPattern drill time! Let's practice now.\n\n54\n00:03:33.951 --> 00:03:36.691\nRepeat these structures with me.\n\n55\n00:03:36.771 --> 00:03:40.532\nIk eet om een uur.\n\n56\n00:03:40.672 --> 00:03:43.372\nI eat at one o'clock.\n\n57\n00:03:43.432 --> 00:03:46.992\nHij komt op dinsdag.\n\n58\n00:03:47.032 --> 00:03:49.612\nHe comes on Tuesday.\n\n59\n00:03:49.652 --> 00:03:53.232\nWij reizen in de zomer.\n\n60\n00:03:53.292 --> 00:03:55.872\nWe travel in the summer.\n\n61\n00:03:56.012 --> 00:04:00.513\nVan twee tot drie rust ik.\n\n62\n00:04:00.553 --> 00:04:03.733\nFrom two to three I rest.\n\n63\n00:04:03.853 --> 00:04:07.233\nZe bellen over een uur.\n\n64\n00:04:07.293 --> 00:04:09.473\nThey call in an hour.\n\n65\n00:04:09.533 --> 00:04:14.093\nNow let's visit the error clinic.\n\n66\n00:04:14.153 --> 00:04:17.694\nBeginner mistake number one:\n\n67\n00:04:17.774 --> 00:04:22.774\nSaying IN for time on clocks.\n\n68\n00:04:22.814 --> 00:04:27.174\nIncorrect: IN negen uur.\n\n69\n00:04:27.214 --> 00:04:29.474\nWhy is this wrong?\n\n70\n00:04:29.494 --> 00:04:34.895\nClock times always take OM, not IN!\n\n71\n00:04:34.975 --> 00:04:38.855\nIk kom om negen uur.\n\n72\n00:04:38.955 --> 00:04:42.215\nI come at nine o'clock.\n\n73\n00:04:42.255 --> 00:04:45.715\nBeginner mistake number two:\n\n74\n00:04:45.895 --> 00:04:50.135\nUsing IN for days of the week.\n\n75\n00:04:50.195 --> 00:04:54.576\nIncorrect: IN maandag.\n\n76\n00:04:54.616 --> 00:04:57.576\nDays of the week take OP!\n\n77\n00:04:57.656 --> 00:05:01.316\nIk werk op maandag.\n\n78\n00:05:01.436 --> 00:05:03.836\nI work on Monday.\n\n79\n00:05:03.996 --> 00:05:07.236\nBeginner mistake number three:\n\n80\n00:05:07.376 --> 00:05:11.536\nUsing IN for future minutes.\n\n81\n00:05:11.616 --> 00:05:15.837\nIncorrect: IN tien minuten.\n\n82\n00:05:15.957 --> 00:05:20.037\nUse OVER for future time intervals!\n\n83\n00:05:20.097 --> 00:05:23.917\nHet begint over tien minuten.\n\n84\n00:05:24.017 --> 00:05:26.957\nIt starts in ten minutes.\n\n85\n00:05:26.997 --> 00:05:29.337\nHere is your memory anchor.\n\n86\n00:05:29.417 --> 00:05:33.258\nOM for clocks, OP for days.\n\n87\n00:05:33.338 --> 00:05:37.798\nIN for months, OVER for later.\n\n88\n00:05:37.978 --> 00:05:41.698\nVAN and TOT for start and end!\n\n89\n00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:43.518\nGreat job today!\n\n90\n00:05:43.558 --> 00:05:46.458\nLet's do a quick spoken recap.\n\n91\n00:05:46.518 --> 00:05:50.658\nVandaag leerden we de voorzetsels.\n\n92\n00:05:50.698 --> 00:05:54.039\nToday we learned the prepositions.\n\n93\n00:05:54.139 --> 00:05:57.439\nOm voor tijd van de klok.\n\n94\n00:05:57.539 --> 00:05:59.939\nOm for clock times.\n\n95\n00:06:00.019 --> 00:06:03.259\nOp voor dagen van de week.\n\n96\n00:06:03.359 --> 00:06:05.759\nOp for days of the week.\n\n97\n00:06:05.839 --> 00:06:09.199\nIn voor maanden en seizoenen.\n\n98\n00:06:09.299 --> 00:06:11.900\nIn for months and seasons.\n\n99\n00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:14.860\nEn over voor de toekomst.\n\n100\n00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.560\nAnd over for the future.\n\n101\n00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.140\nSee you in the next lesson!\n
