cw_city_places	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #20: Places in the City | A1 Beginners	Plaatsen in de stad	Places in the city	In this lesson we cover: Plaatsen in de stad (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• de supermarkt\n• de bibliotheek\n• het ziekenhuis\n• het postkantoor\n• het station\n• het park\n• de apotheek\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• de supermarkt — the supermarket\n• de bibliotheek — the library\n• het ziekenhuis — the hospital\n• het postkantoor — the post office\n• het station — the station\n• het park — the park\n• de apotheek — the pharmacy\n• de school — the school\n• de bank — the bank\n• het restaurant — the restaurant\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Articles with Places (de vs het): In Dutch, nouns take either 'de' or 'het'. City places can be either, so it is best to learn the article with each word.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- de supermarkt\n\nMini Examples\n- de supermarkt\n- het ziekenhuis\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	yq-epb943DE	430	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Speaker1: Welcome to our Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Welkom bij de les! Speaker1: Today we learn city places. Speaker1: We leren over de stad. Speaker1: Are you ready? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: First, we have the supermarket. Speaker1: Ik ga naar de supermarkt. Speaker1: I go to the supermarket. Speaker1: Ik koop brood in de supermarkt. Speaker1: I buy bread in the supermarket. Speaker1: Next is the library. Speaker1: Ik ben in de bibliotheek. Speaker1: I am in the library. Speaker1: Ik lees een boek in de bibliotheek. Speaker1: I read a book in the library. Speaker1: Now we have the hospital. Speaker1: Het ziekenhuis is erg groot. Speaker1: The hospital is very big. Speaker1: De dokter werkt in het ziekenhuis. Speaker1: The doctor works in the hospital. Speaker1: Our next word is the post office. Speaker1: Waar is het postkantoor? Speaker1: Where is the post office? Speaker1: Ik stuur een brief op het postkantoor. Speaker1: I send a letter at the post office. Speaker1: Next, let us learn the station. Speaker1: De trein staat op het station. Speaker1: The train is at the station. Speaker1: Ik wacht op het station. Speaker1: I wait at the station. Speaker1: Now, we visit the park. Speaker1: Wij wandelen in het park. Speaker1: We walk in the park. Speaker1: Het park is erg mooi. Speaker1: The park is very beautiful. Speaker1: Next is the pharmacy. Speaker1: Ik koop medicijnen bij de apotheek. Speaker1: I buy medicines at the pharmacy. Speaker1: De apotheek is open. Speaker1: The pharmacy is open. Speaker1: Let us learn about the school. Speaker1: De kinderen zijn op school. Speaker1: The children are at school. Speaker1: Ik leer op school. Speaker1: I learn at school. Speaker1: Next place is the bank. Speaker1: Het geld is bij de bank. Speaker1: The money is at the bank. Speaker1: Hij werkt bij een bank. Speaker1: He works at a bank. Speaker1: Now we have the restaurant. Speaker1: Wij eten in het restaurant. Speaker1: We eat in the restaurant. Speaker1: Het restaurant is gezellig. Speaker1: The restaurant is cozy. Speaker1: Let us look at the museum. Speaker1: Het museum is erg oud. Speaker1: The museum is very old. Speaker1: Ik kijk naar kunst in het museum. Speaker1: I look at art in the museum. Speaker1: Next place is the shop. Speaker1: Die winkel is nu open. Speaker1: That shop is open now. Speaker1: Zij koopt kleding in de winkel. Speaker1: She buys clothes in the shop. Speaker1: Next we have the market. Speaker1: De markt is erg druk. Speaker1: The market is very busy. Speaker1: Ik koop verse vis op de markt. Speaker1: I buy fresh fish at the market. Speaker1: Now let us learn the cinema. Speaker1: We kijken een film in de bioscoop. Speaker1: We watch a movie in the cinema. Speaker1: De bioscoop is erg donker. Speaker1: The cinema is very dark. Speaker1: Our next place is the hotel. Speaker1: Het hotel staat in het centrum. Speaker1: The hotel stands in the center. Speaker1: Ik slaap in een mooi hotel. Speaker1: I sleep in a nice hotel. Speaker1: Next is the cafe. Speaker1: Ik drink koffie in het café. Speaker1: I drink coffee in the cafe. Speaker1: Het café is erg warm. Speaker1: The cafe is very warm. Speaker1: Great job! Let us practice short questions. Speaker1: Waar is de supermarkt? Speaker1: Where is the supermarket? Speaker1: De supermarkt is in de stad. Speaker1: The supermarket is in the city. Speaker1: Waar is het station? Speaker1: Where is the station? Speaker1: Het station is dichtbij. Speaker1: The station is nearby. Speaker1: Excellent work today! Speaker1: Let us do a quick recap. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: supermarkt, bibliotheek, ziekenhuis. Speaker1: Today we learned: supermarket, library, hospital. Speaker1: Ook leerden we: postkantoor, station, park, apotheek. Speaker1: Also we learned: post office, station, park, pharmacy. Speaker1: Keep practicing these words every day. Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer! Speaker1: See you next time!	27	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
grammar_modal_verbs	A1A2:grammar	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Grammar #10: Modal Verbs (Kunnen, Mogen, Moeten…) | A1 Beginners	Modale Werkwoorden	Dutch Modal Verbs	In this lesson we cover: Modale Werkwoorden (A1 - Grammar)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik wil koffie drinken.\n• Ik kan goed zwemmen.\n• Ik moet nu werken.\n• Mag ik hier zitten?\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• kunnen — can / to be able to\n• mogen — may / to be allowed to\n• moeten — must / to have to\n• willen — want / to desire\n• zwemmen — to swim\n• drinken — to drink\n• werken — to work\n• zitten — to sit\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Modal Verb Sentence Structure: In Dutch sentences with a modal verb (kunnen, mogen, moeten, willen), the modal verb goes in second position and is conjugated according to the subject. The main action verb moves to the very end of the sentence in its infinitive form.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik wil een appel eten.\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik wil een appel eten.\n- Hij moet nu gaan.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	EU6i6H5eij8	\N	Speaker1: Imagine ordering coffee in Amsterdam. Speaker1: How do you say 'I want'? Speaker1: You use a modal verb! Speaker1: Today we learn four modal verbs. Speaker1: They are: kunnen, mogen, moeten, willen. Speaker1: Can, may, must, and want. Speaker1: In English, we say 'I can swim'. Speaker1: Dutch works in a similar way. Speaker1: But there is one special rule. Speaker1: The second verb goes to the end! Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let us begin! Speaker1: First verb: kunnen. It means can. Speaker1: Ik kan goed zwemmen. Speaker1: I can swim well. Speaker1: Notice 'zwemmen' is at the end. Speaker1: Second verb: willen. It means want. Speaker1: Ik wil koffie drinken. Speaker1: I want to drink coffee. Speaker1: 'Drinken' is at the very end! Speaker1: Let's look at the verb 'moeten'. Speaker1: 'Moeten' means must or have to. Speaker1: Ik moet nu werken. Speaker1: I must work now. Speaker1: Next verb: 'mogen'. It means may. Speaker1: 'Mogen' is used for permission. Speaker1: Mag ik hier zitten? Speaker1: May I sit here? Speaker1: Great! Let's try 'kunnen' again. Speaker1: Hij kan Nederlands spreken. Speaker1: He can speak Dutch. Speaker1: Can you guess the next sentence? Speaker1: How do you say: 'We want to sleep'? Speaker1: Think for a moment. Speaker1: The answer is: Speaker1: Wij willen nu slapen. Speaker1: We want to sleep now. Speaker1: Notice 'slapen' goes to the end! Speaker1: Now let's try 'moeten' with 'you'. Speaker1: How do you say: 'You must go'? Speaker1: Here is the sentence: Speaker1: Je moet nu gaan. Speaker1: You must go now. Speaker1: Excellent work! Time for pattern drill. Speaker1: Drill 1: want to eat. Speaker1: Ik wil een appel eten. Speaker1: I want to eat an apple. Speaker1: Drill 2: can pay. Speaker1: Kan ik hier pinnen? Speaker1: Can I pay by card here? Speaker1: Drill 3: must study. Speaker1: Zij moeten vandaag studeren. Speaker1: They must study today. Speaker1: Drill 4: may enter. Speaker1: Je mag hier binnenkomen. Speaker1: You may enter here. Speaker1: Can you guess this one? Speaker1: How to say 'I can help'? Speaker1: Here is the answer: Speaker1: Ik kan jou helpen. Speaker1: I can help you. Speaker1: Now, time for the error clinic! Speaker1: Beginners often make these mistakes. Speaker1: Mistake 1: wrong verb order. Speaker1: Here is a wrong sentence: Speaker1: Ik wil drinken koffie. Speaker1: I want drink coffee. Speaker1: What went wrong here? Speaker1: 'Drinken' must go to the end! Speaker1: Here is the correct sentence: Speaker1: Ik wil koffie drinken. Speaker1: I want to drink coffee. Speaker1: Mistake 2: conjugating the second verb. Speaker1: Here is another wrong sentence: Speaker1: Hij kan spreekt Nederlands. Speaker1: He can speaks Dutch. Speaker1: Don't conjugate the second verb! Speaker1: Always keep the second verb whole. Speaker1: Here is the correct version: Speaker1: Hij kan Nederlands spreken. Speaker1: He can speak Dutch. Speaker1: Memory anchor time! Speaker1: Modal verb second, action verb last! Speaker1: Keep the action verb whole! Speaker1: Let's do a quick spoken recap. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: Speaker1: Kunnen is can, mogen is may. Speaker1: Moeten is must, willen is want. Speaker1: And action verbs go last! Speaker1: Heel goed gedaan! Speaker1: Very well done! Speaker1: See you next time!	9	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_colors	A1A2:start_here	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #2: Basic Colors | A1 Beginners	Basiskleuren	Basic Colors in Dutch	In this lesson we cover: Basiskleuren (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• De appel is rood.\n• De lucht is blauw.\n• Het gras is groen.\n• De zon is geel.\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• rood — red\n• blauw — blue\n• groen — green\n• geel — yellow\n• zwart — black\n• wit — white\n• oranje — orange\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Predicate Adjectives with Colors: When a color adjective comes after the verb 'zijn' (to be), it does not take an extra '-e' ending.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- De appel is rood.\n\nMini Examples\n- De appel is rood.\n- De zon is geel.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	_0rz6ZgEfLk	\N	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson! Speaker1: Today we will learn basic colors. Speaker1: Colors make our world beautiful. Speaker1: We will cover seven common colors. Speaker1: Are you ready to start learning? Speaker1: Listen carefully to each sentence. Speaker1: Repeat after me to practice speaking. Speaker1: Let us begin right now! Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: First, we have red. Speaker1: De appel is rood. Speaker1: The apple is red. Speaker1: Rood is een mooie kleur. Speaker1: Red is a nice color. Speaker1: Next, we learn blue. Speaker1: De lucht is blauw. Speaker1: The sky is blue. Speaker1: Mijn auto is blauw. Speaker1: My car is blue. Speaker1: Now, let us learn green. Speaker1: Het gras is groen. Speaker1: The grass is green. Speaker1: Een blad is groen. Speaker1: A leaf is green. Speaker1: Next is the word yellow. Speaker1: De zon is geel. Speaker1: The sun is yellow. Speaker1: Een banaan is geel. Speaker1: A banana is yellow. Speaker1: Now we look at black. Speaker1: De nacht is zwart. Speaker1: The night is black. Speaker1: De kat is zwart. Speaker1: The cat is black. Speaker1: Next, we have white. Speaker1: De sneeuw is wit. Speaker1: The snow is white. Speaker1: Melk is wit. Speaker1: Milk is white. Speaker1: Finally, we learn orange. Speaker1: Een sinaasappel is oranje. Speaker1: An orange is orange. Speaker1: De wortel is oranje. Speaker1: The carrot is orange. Speaker1: Great job practicing these colors! Speaker1: Let us review our list. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: Speaker1: Today we learned: Speaker1: Rood, blauw, en groen. Speaker1: Red, blue, and green. Speaker1: Geel, zwart, en wit. Speaker1: Yellow, black, and white. Speaker1: En natuurlijk oranje! Speaker1: And of course orange! Speaker1: Practice these colors every day! Speaker1: Tot ziens! Speaker1: See you next time!	6	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_conjunctions_basic	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #32: Basic Conjunctions | A1 Beginners	Basisschakelwoorden	Basic Conjunctions: en, maar, of, want, dus	In this lesson we cover: Basisschakelwoorden (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• koffie en thee\n• moe, maar hij werkt\n• vandaag of morgen\n• want ik heb honger\n• dus ik eet\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• en — and\n• maar — but\n• of — or\n• want — because\n• dus — so / therefore\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Basic Coordinating Conjunctions: In Dutch, conjunctions like 'en', 'maar', 'of', 'want', and 'dus' connect words or full clauses. 'Want' introduces a reason without changing the subject-verb word order in the main clause.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik eet, want ik heb honger.\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik eet, want ik heb honger.\n- Hij is moe, maar hij werkt.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	dRPfacPG3pM	\N	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today, we are learning five essential Dutch conjunctions. Speaker1: Conjunctions are small words that connect ideas, sentences, or items. Speaker1: They make your speech flow smoothly and naturally. Speaker1: We will learn five very common words today. Speaker1: These words are EN, MAAR, OF, WANT, and DUS. Speaker1: You will hear each word used in clear, simple sentences. Speaker1: Listen carefully to how they connect words together. Speaker1: Are you ready to begin? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Our first word is EN, which means AND in English. Speaker1: EN is used to join two things, people, or actions. Speaker1: Let's listen to three simple sentences with EN. Speaker1: Ik drink koffie en thee. Speaker1: I drink coffee and tea. Speaker1: Jan en Piet lezen een boek. Speaker1: Jan and Piet read a book. Speaker1: Wij eten brood en kaas. Speaker1: We eat bread and cheese. Speaker1: Great job! Remember: EN means AND. Speaker1: Our second word is MAAR, which means BUT. Speaker1: MAAR shows a contrast or a difference between two ideas. Speaker1: Here are three simple example sentences with MAAR. Speaker1: Ik wil rijst, maar niet vandaag. Speaker1: I want rice, but not today. Speaker1: Hij is moe, maar hij werkt. Speaker1: He is tired, but he works. Speaker1: Het is koud, maar heel mooi. Speaker1: It is cold, but very beautiful. Speaker1: Excellent! Remember: MAAR means BUT. Speaker1: Our third word is OF, which means OR. Speaker1: OF is used when giving a choice between options. Speaker1: Let's listen to three example sentences with OF. Speaker1: Wil je koffie of thee? Speaker1: Do you want coffee or tea? Speaker1: Komen we vandaag of morgen? Speaker1: Are we coming today or tomorrow? Speaker1: Is het een kat of hond? Speaker1: Is it a cat or a dog? Speaker1: Very good! Remember: OF means OR. Speaker1: Our fourth word is WANT, which means BECAUSE. Speaker1: WANT gives a reason for something. Speaker1: In Dutch, the word order stays normal after WANT. Speaker1: Let's listen to three simple sentences with WANT. Speaker1: Ik eet, want ik heb honger. Speaker1: I eat, because I am hungry. Speaker1: Hij slaapt, want hij is moe. Speaker1: He sleeps, because he is tired. Speaker1: Wij blijven thuis, want het regent. Speaker1: We stay home, because it rains. Speaker1: Wonderful! Remember: WANT means BECAUSE. Speaker1: Our fifth word is DUS, which means SO or THEREFORE. Speaker1: DUS shows a result or conclusion. Speaker1: Let's listen to three simple sentences with DUS. Speaker1: Ik heb honger, dus ik eet. Speaker1: I am hungry, so I eat. Speaker1: Het is laat, dus ik slaap. Speaker1: It is late, so I sleep. Speaker1: Hij studeert, dus hij weet veel. Speaker1: He studies, so he knows a lot. Speaker1: Fantastic! Remember: DUS means SO. Speaker1: Now let's practice with a few extra review sentences. Speaker1: Ik spreek Nederlands en Engels. Speaker1: I speak Dutch and English. Speaker1: Het is klein, maar erg fijn. Speaker1: It is small, but very nice. Speaker1: Ga je lopend of met bus? Speaker1: Are you going on foot or by bus? Speaker1: Ze lacht, want ze is blij. Speaker1: She laughs, because she is happy. Speaker1: Je leert snel, dus het helpt. Speaker1: You learn quickly, so it helps. Speaker1: You are doing an incredible job today! Speaker1: Let's recap all five words we learned. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: en, maar, of, want, dus. Speaker1: Today we learned: en, maar, of, want, dus. Speaker1: Keep practicing these basic conjunctions every day. Speaker1: Thank you for joining me, and see you next time!	9	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_animals	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #22: Common Animals | A1 Beginners	De Dieren	Common Animals	In this lesson we cover: De Dieren (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik heb een hond.\n• De kat slaapt op de bank.\n• De vis zwemt in water.\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• de hond — the dog\n• de kat — the cat\n• het konijn — the rabbit\n• de vogel — the bird\n• de vis — the fish\n• het paard — the horse\n• de koe — the cow\n• het varken — the pig\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Articles with Nouns (De vs. Het): Every Dutch noun uses either 'de' or 'het' as its definite article. In this lesson, hond, kat, vogel, vis, and koe use 'de', while konijn, paard, and varken use 'het'.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- de hond (the dog)\n\nMini Examples\n- de hond (the dog)\n- het konijn (the rabbit)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	oJ1b3qcj8lc	484	Speaker1: Hello everyone, welcome to today's lesson! Speaker1: Today we will learn about common animals. Speaker1: Animals are very fun to talk about. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's get started! Speaker1: First, let's learn about household pets. Speaker1: Our first animal is the dog. Speaker1: In Dutch, a dog is DE HOND. Speaker1: De hond is heel lief. Speaker1: The dog is very sweet. Speaker1: Ik heb een hond. Speaker1: I have a dog. Speaker1: De hond blaft erg hard. Speaker1: The dog barks very loudly. Speaker1: Great job! Remember, HOND means dog. Speaker1: Next, we have the cat. Speaker1: In Dutch, a cat is DE KAT. Speaker1: De kat slaapt op de bank. Speaker1: The cat sleeps on the couch. Speaker1: Ik vind de kat leuk. Speaker1: I like the cat. Speaker1: De kat drinkt warme melk. Speaker1: The cat drinks warm milk. Speaker1: Excellent! KAT means cat. Speaker1: Now, let's look at the rabbit. Speaker1: In Dutch, a rabbit is HET KONIJN. Speaker1: Het konijn eet een wortel. Speaker1: The rabbit eats a carrot. Speaker1: Het konijn is erg klein. Speaker1: The rabbit is very small. Speaker1: Ik zie een wit konijn. Speaker1: I see a white rabbit. Speaker1: Very good! KONIJN means rabbit. Speaker1: Now let's learn about birds and fish. Speaker1: Our fourth animal is the bird. Speaker1: In Dutch, a bird is DE VOGEL. Speaker1: De vogel vliegt heel hoog. Speaker1: The bird flies very high. Speaker1: De vogel zingt een lied. Speaker1: The bird sings a song. Speaker1: Ik hoor een kleine vogel. Speaker1: I hear a small bird. Speaker1: Well done! VOGEL means bird. Speaker1: Next, let's talk about fish. Speaker1: In Dutch, a fish is DE VIS. Speaker1: De vis zwemt in water. Speaker1: The fish swims in water. Speaker1: De vis is erg mooi. Speaker1: The fish is very beautiful. Speaker1: Ik zie een rode vis. Speaker1: I see a red fish. Speaker1: Perfect! VIS means fish. Speaker1: Now, let's move to farm animals. Speaker1: Our sixth word is horse. Speaker1: In Dutch, a horse is HET PAARD. Speaker1: Het paard loopt in het gras. Speaker1: The horse walks in the grass. Speaker1: Het paard is heel groot. Speaker1: The horse is very big. Speaker1: Ik rijd op het paard. Speaker1: I ride on the horse. Speaker1: Wonderful! PAARD means horse. Speaker1: Next, we have the cow. Speaker1: In Dutch, a cow is DE KOE. Speaker1: De koe geeft verse melk. Speaker1: The cow gives fresh milk. Speaker1: De koe staat in het gras. Speaker1: The cow stands in the grass. Speaker1: Ik zie een zwarte koe. Speaker1: I see a black cow. Speaker1: Great! KOE means cow. Speaker1: Our eighth animal is the pig. Speaker1: In Dutch, a pig is HET VARKEN. Speaker1: Het varken is roze en dik. Speaker1: The pig is pink and fat. Speaker1: Het varken slaapt in het stro. Speaker1: The pig sleeps in the straw. Speaker1: Ik zie een schoon varken. Speaker1: I see a clean pig. Speaker1: Fantastic! VARKEN means pig. Speaker1: Let's recap all the words we learned today. Speaker1: Listen carefully to the summary. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we deze acht woorden. Speaker1: Today we learned these eight words. Speaker1: We hebben de hond en de kat. Speaker1: We have the dog and the cat. Speaker1: We hebben het konijn en de vogel. Speaker1: We have the rabbit and the bird. Speaker1: We hebben de vis en het paard. Speaker1: We have the fish and the horse. Speaker1: We hebben de koe en het varken. Speaker1: We have the cow and the pig. Speaker1: Dank je wel en tot ziens! Speaker1: Thank you and goodbye!	16	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_degree_adverbs	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #29: Degree Adverbs | A1 Beginners	Graadwoorden in het Nederlands	Degree Adverbs in Dutch	In this lesson we cover: Graadwoorden in het Nederlands (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• heel koud\n• erg warm\n• echt lekker\n• zo lief\n• vrij goed\n• best mooi\n• nogal druk\n• tamelijk hoog\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• heel — very\n• erg — very / really\n• echt — really / truly\n• zo — so\n• vrij — quite / fairly\n• best — pretty / quite\n• nogal — rather / quite\n• tamelijk — fairly / rather\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Degree Adverbs (Graadwoorden): Degree adverbs modify adjectives or other adverbs to express intensity. 'Heel' and 'erg' increase intensity strongly, 'echt' adds emphasis, while 'vrij', 'best', 'nogal', and 'tamelijk' represent moderate intensity.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Het water is heel koud.\n\nMini Examples\n- Het water is heel koud.\n- Het is vrij goedkoop.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	lflKsODQBDU	303	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn degree adverbs. Speaker1: Degree adverbs show intensity. Speaker1: They make words stronger or weaker. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let us begin! Speaker1: Our first word is heel. Speaker1: Heel means very in English. Speaker1: Het water is heel koud. Speaker1: The water is very cold. Speaker1: Zij is heel vriendelijk. Speaker1: She is very friendly. Speaker1: Our second word is erg. Speaker1: Erg also means very. Speaker1: Het is erg warm vandaag. Speaker1: It is very warm today. Speaker1: Ik ben erg moe. Speaker1: I am very tired. Speaker1: Next we have echt. Speaker1: Echt means really or truly. Speaker1: Dit is echt lekker. Speaker1: This is really delicious. Speaker1: Hij is echt slim. Speaker1: He is really smart. Speaker1: Now we look at zo. Speaker1: Zo means so in English. Speaker1: Je bent zo lief. Speaker1: You are so sweet. Speaker1: Het is zo laat. Speaker1: It is so late. Speaker1: Next is the word vrij. Speaker1: Vrij means quite or fairly. Speaker1: De film is vrij goed. Speaker1: The movie is quite good. Speaker1: Het huis is vrij groot. Speaker1: The house is fairly big. Speaker1: Our sixth word is best. Speaker1: Best means pretty or quite. Speaker1: Het is best koud buiten. Speaker1: It is pretty cold outside. Speaker1: Ik vind dit best mooi. Speaker1: I think this is pretty beautiful. Speaker1: Next we study nogal. Speaker1: Nogal means rather or quite. Speaker1: De man is nogal druk. Speaker1: The man is rather busy. Speaker1: Dit boek is nogal saai. Speaker1: This book is rather boring. Speaker1: Our last word is tamelijk. Speaker1: Tamelijk means fairly or rather. Speaker1: De prijs is tamelijk hoog. Speaker1: The price is fairly high. Speaker1: Het is tamelijk ver lopen. Speaker1: It is fairly far to walk. Speaker1: Now let us practice together. Speaker1: Listen and repeat the sentences. Speaker1: De soep is heel warm. Speaker1: The soup is very warm. Speaker1: Ik vind het erg leuk. Speaker1: I find it very fun. Speaker1: Het is echt waar. Speaker1: It is really true. Speaker1: Waarom ben je zo moe? Speaker1: Why are you so tired? Speaker1: Het is vrij goedkoop. Speaker1: It is fairly cheap. Speaker1: De test is best moeilijk. Speaker1: The test is pretty difficult. Speaker1: Hij praat nogal snel. Speaker1: He speaks rather fast. Speaker1: De kamer is tamelijk klein. Speaker1: The room is fairly small. Speaker1: Great job today! Speaker1: Let us recap our words. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we heel en erg. Speaker1: Today we learned heel and erg. Speaker1: We leerden echt en zo. Speaker1: We learned echt and zo. Speaker1: En ook vrij en best. Speaker1: And also vrij and best. Speaker1: En tenslotte nogal en tamelijk. Speaker1: And finally nogal and tamelijk. Speaker1: Practice these words every day. Speaker1: See you next time! Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer! Speaker1: Until next time!	26	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
at_the_market	A1A2:dialogue	A1A2	At the market - Dialogue | Dutch in 5 minutes | A1-A2 Beginners	Op de markt	At the market	In this lesson we cover: Op de markt (A1-A2 - Dialogue)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Wat kosten deze appels?\n• Mag ik een kilo...?\n• Hoeveel kost het?\n• Kan ik hier pinnen?\n• Doe maar twee stuks.\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• de kraam — the market stall\n• het fruit — the fruit\n• de groenten — the vegetables\n• rijp — ripe\n• de aanbieding — the special offer\n• pinnen — to pay by card\n• het zakje — the small bag\n• belegen — matured (cheese)\n• de bon — the receipt\n• het gewicht — the weight\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Asking for prices with 'Wat kost...' and 'Hoeveel kost...': In Dutch markets, both 'Wat kost...' (What does ... cost?) and 'Hoeveel kost...' (How much does ... cost?) are common, followed by singular or plural verbs depending on the item.\n- Quantities and Measurements: Units like 'kilo', 'gram', and 'stuks' do not take a plural form when preceded by numbers.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Wat kosten deze appels?\n- twee kilo aardappelen\n\nMini Examples\n- Wat kosten deze appels?\n- Hoeveel kost die zak aardappelen?\n- twee kilo aardappelen\n- vijfhonderd gram kaas\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	QYxkEfkmtgo	378	Speaker1: Goedemorgen! Welkom op de markt. Speaker2: Goedemorgen. Wat een mooie groenten heeft u vandaag. Speaker1: Dank u wel! Alles is heel vers. Zoekt u iets speciaals? Speaker2: Ja, ik wil vanavond soep maken. Speaker1: Ah, lekker! Wat voor soep gaat u maken? Speaker2: Tomatensoep. Dus ik heb tomaten nodig. Speaker1: Deze rode tomaten zijn perfect voor soep. Ze zijn heel rijp. Speaker2: Hoeveel kosten deze tomaten? Speaker1: Ze zijn twee euro per kilo. Speaker2: Dat is niet duur. Doe maar een kilo, alstublieft. Speaker1: Prima. Ik weeg ze voor u. Speaker2: