cw_adjectives_basic	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #11: Basic Adjectives | A1 Beginners	Basiseigenschappen	Basic Adjectives	In this lesson we cover: Basiseigenschappen (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Het huis is heel groot.\n• De kat is erg klein.\n• De bloem is erg mooi.\n• De auto is heel snel.\n• Dit boek is erg goed.\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• groot — big / tall\n• klein — small / little\n• mooi — beautiful / pretty\n• lelijk — ugly\n• goed — good / well\n• slecht — bad\n• snel — fast / quick\n• langzaam — slow\n• nieuw — new\n• oud — old\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Basic Adjectives Predicate Position: When an adjective follows a verb like 'zijn' (to be) in Dutch, it remains in its basic form and does not get an extra -e ending.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Het huis is groot.\n\nMini Examples\n- Het huis is groot.\n- De kat is klein.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	RWQw8A_6nWk	\N	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch vocabulary lesson! Speaker1: Today we are learning basic adjectives in Dutch. Speaker1: Adjectives help us describe things and people around us. Speaker1: I will explain everything clearly in English. Speaker1: Then I will say short example sentences in Dutch. Speaker1: Please listen carefully to the pronunciation. Speaker1: Repeat each Dutch sentence out loud after me. Speaker1: We will cover ten key words today. Speaker1: Words like big, small, fast, and slow. Speaker1: This will really improve your Dutch speaking skills. Speaker1: Are you ready to begin? Speaker1: Let's dive right in! Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: That means: Let's begin! Speaker1: Let's explore our first pair of words. Speaker1: Our first word is GROOT. Speaker1: GROOT means big or tall in English. Speaker1: Let's listen to a simple sentence with GROOT. Speaker1: Het huis is heel groot. Speaker1: That translates to: The house is very big. Speaker1: Notice how GROOT comes at the end of the sentence. Speaker1: Let's practice another sentence with GROOT. Speaker1: De man is erg groot. Speaker1: That means: The man is very tall. Speaker1: So remember, GROOT means big or tall. Speaker1: Our second word is the opposite: KLEIN. Speaker1: KLEIN means small or little. Speaker1: Here is our first example sentence. Speaker1: De kat is erg klein. Speaker1: That means: The cat is very small. Speaker1: Let's try another short sentence together. Speaker1: Het kind is nog klein. Speaker1: That translates to: The child is still small. Speaker1: Great job! KLEIN means small. Speaker1: Now let's move to our third word: MOOI. Speaker1: MOOI means beautiful, nice, or pretty. Speaker1: Let's listen to an example sentence. Speaker1: De bloem is erg mooi. Speaker1: That means: The flower is very beautiful. Speaker1: Here is another common sentence. Speaker1: Het weer is vandaag mooi. Speaker1: That translates to: The weather is beautiful today. Speaker1: Remember: MOOI means beautiful or nice. Speaker1: Our fourth word is LELIJK. Speaker1: LELIJK is the opposite of MOOI, and it means ugly. Speaker1: Let's hear it in a short sentence. Speaker1: De oude stoel is lelijk. Speaker1: That means: The old chair is ugly. Speaker1: Let's practice one more sentence. Speaker1: Dit schilderij is heel lelijk. Speaker1: That translates to: This painting is very ugly. Speaker1: So LELIJK means ugly. Speaker1: Our fifth word is GOED. Speaker1: GOED means good or well. Speaker1: Let's use GOED in a sentence. Speaker1: Dit boek is erg goed. Speaker1: That means: This book is very good. Speaker1: Here is an encouraging phrase you will hear often. Speaker1: Je doet het heel goed! Speaker1: That means: You are doing very well! Speaker1: Remember: GOED means good. Speaker1: Our sixth word is SLECHT. Speaker1: SLECHT means bad. Speaker1: Let's look at an example sentence. Speaker1: Het eten is erg slecht. Speaker1: That means: The food is very bad. Speaker1: Let's try another simple sentence. Speaker1: Het weer is vandaag slecht. Speaker1: That translates to: The weather is bad today. Speaker1: So SLECHT means bad. Speaker1: Our seventh word is SNEL. Speaker1: SNEL means fast or quick. Speaker1: Let's practice a short sentence with SNEL. Speaker1: De auto is heel snel. Speaker1: That means: The car is very fast. Speaker1: Here is another example. Speaker1: De hond rent erg snel. Speaker1: That translates to: The dog runs very fast. Speaker1: Excellent! SNEL means fast. Speaker1: Our eighth word is LANGZAAM. Speaker1: LANGZAAM means slow. Speaker1: Let's hear it in a sentence. Speaker1: De schildpad is erg langzaam. Speaker1: That means: The turtle is very slow. Speaker1: Let's try one more example sentence. Speaker1: Hij loopt heel erg langzaam. Speaker1: That translates to: He walks very slowly. Speaker1: So LANGZAAM means slow. Speaker1: Our ninth word is NIEUW. Speaker1: NIEUW means new. Speaker1: Let's listen to this example. Speaker1: Mijn auto is helemaal nieuw. Speaker1: That means: My car is completely new. Speaker1: Let's try another short sentence. Speaker1: Ik koop een nieuw boek. Speaker1: That translates to: I buy a new book. Speaker1: Great job! NIEUW means new. Speaker1: Our tenth word today is OUD. Speaker1: OUD means old. Speaker1: Let's look at our example sentence. Speaker1: Opa is al erg oud. Speaker1: That means: Grandfather is already very old. Speaker1: Let's practice one final sentence. Speaker1: De stad is heel oud. Speaker1: That translates to: The city is very old. Speaker1: So OUD means old. Speaker1: You did a wonderful job today! Speaker1: Let's do a quick spoken review of all ten words. Speaker1: We herhalen de woorden nu. Speaker1: That means: We repeat the words now. Speaker1: Het huis is erg groot. Speaker1: The house is very big. Speaker1: De kat is heel klein. Speaker1: The cat is very small. Speaker1: De bloem is erg m