2: Om mijn arm, toch? Speaker1: Ja, stroop uw mouw maar op. Speaker2: Welke arm wilt u? Speaker1: Uw linkerarm is prima. Speaker2: Ik doe mijn mouw omhoog. Speaker1: De band gaat nu een beetje strak zitten. Speaker2: Ja, ik voel het. Speaker1: Even stil blijven zitten. Speaker2: Ik zeg niets. Speaker1: Prima. Uw bloeddruk is honderdtwintig over tachtig. Speaker2: Is dat goed? Speaker1: Dat is een perfecte bloeddruk. Speaker2: Heel fijn! Speaker1: U mag weer op de stoel komen zitten. Speaker2: Dank u, ik trek mijn jas weer aan. Speaker1: U heeft gewoon een griepje. Speaker2: Een gewone griep? Speaker1: Ja, een virale infectie. Speaker2: Heb ik antibiotica nodig? Speaker1: Nee, antibiotica helpen niet tegen een virus. Speaker2: Wat kan ik dan het beste doen? Speaker1: U moet vooral veel rust nemen. Speaker2: Moet ik thuisblijven van mijn werk? Speaker1: Ja, ik adviseer u om twee dagen thuis te blijven. Speaker2: Oké, dat zal ik doen. Speaker1: Drinkt u genoeg water en thee? Speaker2: Ik drink wel veel warme thee met honing. Speaker1: Dat is heel goed voor uw keel. Speaker2: Kan ik iets tegen de pijn nemen? Speaker1: U mag paracetamol gebruiken. Speaker2: Hoeveel paracetamol mag ik nemen? Speaker1: Maximaal vier keer per dag twee tabletten van vijfhonderd milligram. Speaker2: Dus acht tabletten per dag maximaal? Speaker1: Ja, dat is de maximale dosering. Speaker2: Om de hoeveel uur mag dat? Speaker1: Elke zes uur twee tabletten. Speaker2: Duidelijk. En als de koorts hoger wordt? Speaker1: Als de koorts boven de negen en dertig vijf komt, moet u me bellen. Speaker2: En als het na vier dagen niet beter gaat? Speaker1: Dan moet u ook even terugkomen op het spreekuur. Speaker2: Oké, ik begrijp het. Speaker1: Heeft u verder nog vragen? Speaker2: Heb ik een recept nodig voor de paracetamol? Speaker1: Nee, die kunt u zo bij de drogist of apotheek kopen. Speaker2: Mooi, dan haal ik die zo meteen. Speaker1: Heel goed. Blijf lekker warm thuis. Speaker2: Dat ga ik zeker doen. Speaker1: Bescherm uzelf goed tegen de kou buiten. Speaker2: Ja, ik doe mijn sjaal en muts om. Speaker1: Beterschap! Speaker2: Dank u wel voor de goede zorg, dokter. Speaker1: Graag gedaan. Tot ziens! Speaker2: Tot ziens en een fijne dag!	8	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_health_words	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #26: Health and Illness Words | A1 Beginners	Gezondheid en Ziekte	Health and Illness	In this lesson we cover: Gezondheid en Ziekte (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik ben ziek\n• Ik heb pijn\n• Ik ga naar de dokter\n• Beterschap!\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• ziek — sick\n• pijn — pain\n• hoofd — head\n• buik — belly / stomach\n• keel — throat\n• dokter — doctor\n• medicijn — medicine\n• rust — rest\n• beter — better\n• koorts — fever\n• apotheek — pharmacy\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Expressing Pain in Dutch: To describe pain in a body part, you can say 'Ik heb pijn in mijn...' (I have pain in my...) or '[Body part] doet pijn' ([Body part] hurts).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik heb pijn in mijn hoofd.\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik heb pijn in mijn hoofd.\n- Mijn buik doet pijn.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	HYDkdFIxC4k	213	Speaker1: Hello everyone, welcome back to Dutch with Gemini! Speaker1: Today we are learning essential health words in Dutch. Speaker1: These words will help you explain how you feel or ask for help. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Our first word is ZIEK, which means sick. Speaker1: Ik ben vandaag een beetje ziek. Speaker1: Mijn broer is ook ziek. Speaker1: Next, we have PIJN, which means pain. Speaker1: Ik heb hier veel pijn. Speaker1: Waar doet het precies pijn? Speaker1: Now let's look at body parts. HOOFD means head. Speaker1: Mijn hoofd doet een beetje pijn. Speaker1: Ik heb pijn in mijn hoofd. Speaker1: The word for belly or stomach is BUIK. Speaker1: Het kind heeft pijn in de buik. Speaker1: Mijn buik is heel erg gevoelig. Speaker1: The word for throat is KEEL. Speaker1: Ik heb last van mijn keel. Speaker1: Drink warme thee voor je keel. Speaker1: When you are sick, you see a DOKTER, which means doctor. Speaker1: Ik ga morgen naar de dokter. Speaker1: De dokter helpt de zieke mensen. Speaker1: The word for medicine is MEDICIJN. Speaker1: Neem dit medicijn met water in. Speaker1: Dit medicijn werkt heel snel. Speaker1: To heal, you need RUST, which means rest. Speaker1: Je hebt nu veel rust nodig. Speaker1: Neem een dagje rust. Speaker1: When you recover, you feel BETER, which means better. Speaker1: Ik voel mij al veel beter. Speaker1: Beterschap en word snel beter! Speaker1: Another useful word is KOORTS, which means fever. Speaker1: Hij heeft vandaag hoge koorts. Speaker1: Heb je ook koorts? Speaker1: You buy your medicine at the APOTHEEK, or pharmacy. Speaker1: Ik koop medicijnen bij de apotheek. Speaker1: Waar is de dichtstbijzijnde apotheek? Speaker1: Finally, don't forget WATER, which means water. Speaker1: Drink veel water als je ziek bent. Speaker1: Een glas koud water helpt altijd. Speaker1: You did a fantastic job today! Speaker1: Let's review all our health vocabulary. Speaker1: Nu kennen we belangrijke woorden. Speaker1: We kennen ziek, pijn en dokter. Speaker1: We kennen hoofd, buik en keel. Speaker1: En ook medicijn, rust en beter. Speaker1: Keep practicing these sentences every day! Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer!	25	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_house_objects	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #19: Household Objects | A1 Beginners	Objecten in Huis	Household Objects	In this lesson we cover: Objecten in Huis (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• sleutel\n• telefoon\n• deur\n• raam\n• lamp\n• tafel\n• stoel\n• spiegel\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• sleutel — key\n• telefoon — phone\n• deur — door\n• raam — window\n• lamp — lamp\n• tafel — table\n• stoel — chair\n• spiegel — mirror\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- \n\nPattern Breakdown\n- \n\nMini Examples\n- \n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	qCHmYWJRAGc	273	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Speaker1: Welcome to our Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn household objects. Speaker1: Welkom bij deze les! Speaker1: Are you ready? Speaker1: Laten we nu beginnen! Speaker1: Our first word is sleutel. Speaker1: Sleutel means key in English. Speaker1: Let's listen to two example sentences. Speaker1: Dit is een oude sleutel. Speaker1: De sleutel ligt op tafel. Speaker1: Great job! Speaker1: Remember: sleutel means key. Speaker1: Our next word is telefoon. Speaker1: Telefoon means phone in English. Speaker1: Here are two examples. Speaker1: Dit is mijn nieuwe telefoon. Speaker1: De telefoon ringt erg luid. Speaker1: Excellent! Speaker1: Remember: telefoon means phone. Speaker1: Next, we have the word deur. Speaker1: Deur means door in English. Speaker1: Let's practice with two sentences. Speaker1: De deur is nu open. Speaker1: Ik sluit de zware deur. Speaker1: Wonderful practice! Speaker1: Remember: deur means door. Speaker1: Now let me teach you raam. Speaker1: Raam means window in English. Speaker1: Look at these simple sentences. Speaker1: Het raam is heel schoon. Speaker1: Ik kijk door het raam. Speaker1: Very well done! Speaker1: Remember: raam means window. Speaker1: Our fifth word is lamp. Speaker1: Lamp means lamp in English. Speaker1: Here are clear examples. Speaker1: De lamp staat in de kamer. Speaker1: Ik doe de lamp aan. Speaker1: Great effort! Speaker1: Remember: lamp means lamp. Speaker1: Next up is the word tafel. Speaker1: Tafel means table in English. Speaker1: Let me give you two examples. Speaker1: De tafel is erg groot. Speaker1: Wij eten aan de tafel. Speaker1: Awesome job! Speaker1: Remember: tafel means table. Speaker1: Let's learn the word stoel. Speaker1: Stoel means chair in English. Speaker1: Listen to these Dutch sentences. Speaker1: Dit is een fijne stoel. Speaker1: Ik zit op de stoel. Speaker1: Fantastic work! Speaker1: Remember: stoel means chair. Speaker1: Our final word today is spiegel. Speaker1: Spiegel means mirror in English. Speaker1: Let me show you two sentences. Speaker1: De spiegel hangt aan de muur. Speaker1: Ik kijk in de spiegel. Speaker1: Perfect! Speaker1: Remember: spiegel means mirror. Speaker1: You did an amazing job today! Speaker1: Let's do a quick spoken review. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we deze woorden: Speaker1: Sleutel, telefoon, deur, raam, lamp, tafel, stoel, spiegel. Speaker1: Practice these words at home! Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer!	19	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_days_of_week	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #3: Days of the Week | A1 Beginners	De Dagen van de Week	Days of the Week	In this lesson we cover: Dagen van de week (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• maandag tot en met zondag\n• Vandaag is het\n• Morgen is het\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• maandag — Monday\n• dinsdag — Tuesday\n• woensdag — Wednesday\n• donderdag — Thursday\n• vrijdag — Friday\n• zaterdag — Saturday\n• zondag — Sunday\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Days of the week capitalization: In Dutch, days of the week are written with lowercase letters unless they start a sentence.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- vandaag is het maandag.\n\nMini Examples\n- vandaag is het maandag.\n- Maandag is de eerste dag.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	HkS-0gLHI30	\N	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today, we are going to learn the seven days of the week in Dutch. Speaker1: Knowing the days of the week is essential for making plans and discussing your schedule. Speaker1: We will learn each day step by step with short and clear example sentences. Speaker1: Pay close attention to how each word is pronounced. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: That means: Let's begin! Speaker1: Our first day is MAANDAG, which means Monday. Speaker1: Let's listen to three simple sentences with MAANDAG. Speaker1: Vandaag is het maandag. Speaker1: Today is Monday. Speaker1: Ik werk op maandag. Speaker1: I work on Monday. Speaker1: Maandag is een fijne dag. Speaker1: Monday is a fine day. Speaker1: Remember: MAANDAG means Monday. Speaker1: Our second day is DINSDAG, which means Tuesday. Speaker1: Here are three simple example sentences with DINSDAG. Speaker1: Morgen is het dinsdag. Speaker1: Tomorrow is Tuesday. Speaker1: Ik lees op dinsdag. Speaker1: I read on Tuesday. Speaker1: Dinsdag is een mooie dag. Speaker1: Tuesday is a beautiful day. Speaker1: Remember: DINSDAG means Tuesday. Speaker1: Our third day is WOENSDAG, which means Wednesday. Speaker1: Let's practice three sentences with WOENSDAG. Speaker1: Het is vandaag woensdag. Speaker1: It is Wednesday today. Speaker1: Ik kook op woensdag. Speaker1: I cook on Wednesday. Speaker1: Woensdag is in het midden. Speaker1: Wednesday is in the middle. Speaker1: Remember: WOENSDAG means Wednesday. Speaker1: Our fourth day is DONDERDAG, which means Thursday. Speaker1: Here are three simple sentences with DONDERDAG. Speaker1: Ik zwem op donderdag. Speaker1: I swim on Thursday. Speaker1: Het is bijna donderdag. Speaker1: It is almost Thursday. Speaker1: Donderdag is een drukke dag. Speaker1: Thursday is a busy day. Speaker1: Remember: DONDERDAG means Thursday. Speaker1: Our fifth day is VRIJDAG, which means Friday. Speaker1: Let's listen to three sentences with VRIJDAG. Speaker1: Vrijdag is een leuke dag. Speaker1: Friday is a fun day. Speaker1: Ik dans op vrijdag. Speaker1: I dance on Friday. Speaker1: Het is vandaag vrijdag. Speaker1: Today is Friday. Speaker1: Remember: VRIJDAG means Friday. Speaker1: Our sixth day is ZATERDAG, which means Saturday. Speaker1: Here are three example sentences with ZATERDAG. Speaker1: Zaterdag is in het weekend. Speaker1: Saturday is in the weekend. Speaker1: Ik rust op zaterdag. Speaker1: I rest on Saturday. Speaker1: Morgen is het zaterdag. Speaker1: Tomorrow is Saturday. Speaker1: Remember: ZATERDAG means Saturday. Speaker1: Our seventh day is ZONDAG, which means Sunday. Speaker1: Let's practice three sentences with ZONDAG. Speaker1: Zondag is een rustige dag. Speaker1: Sunday is a quiet day. Speaker1: Ik wandel op zondag. Speaker1: I walk on Sunday. Speaker1: Vandaag is het zondag. Speaker1: Today is Sunday. Speaker1: Remember: ZONDAG means Sunday. Speaker1: Great job! Now let's do a quick spoken recap of all seven days. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: maandag, dinsdag, woensdag, donderdag, vrijdag, zaterdag, zondag. Speaker1: Keep practicing these days every day! See you in the next lesson!	4	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_linking_words	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #31: Linking Words | A1 Beginners	Handige Verbindingswoorden	Useful Linking Words	In this lesson we cover: Handige Verbindingswoorden (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• eerst drink ik\n• dan eet ik\n• daarna speel ik\n• tot slot\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• eerst — first\n• dan — then\n• daarna — after that\n• tot slot — finally / in conclusion\n• ook — also / too\n• wel — indeed / affirmative contrast\n• toch — still / anyway\n• eigenlijk — actually / in fact\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Word Order with Linking Words: When a sentence starts with a linking adverb like 'eerst', 'dan', or 'daarna', inversion occurs: the verb comes directly after the linking word, followed by the subject.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Eerst drink ik koffie.\n\nMini Examples\n- Eerst drink ik koffie.\n- Dan ga ik werken.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	BjXjeD-LuME	398	Speaker1: Hello everyone, welcome back to your Dutch lesson! Speaker1: Today, we are learning common linking words. Speaker1: Linking words connect your ideas and make your Dutch sound natural. Speaker1: We will practice eight essential words step by step. Speaker1: Are you ready? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Our first word is 'eerst'. Speaker1: 'Eerst' means 'first' in English. Speaker1: Listen to this example sentence: Speaker1: Eerst drink ik warm water. Speaker1: First I drink warm water. Speaker1: You use 'eerst' at the start of a sequence. Speaker1: Let's hear another example: Speaker1: Eerst lees ik een boek. Speaker1: First I read a book. Speaker1: Remember: 'eerst' means 'first'. Speaker1: Next, we have the word 'dan'. Speaker1: 'Dan' means 'then'. Speaker1: Here is our first example: Speaker1: Dan eet ik een appel. Speaker1: Then I eat an apple. Speaker1: 'Dan' helps move to the next action. Speaker1: Let's try another sentence: Speaker1: Dan ga ik naar school. Speaker1: Then I go to school. Speaker1: Great work! 'Dan' means 'then'. Speaker1: Our third word is 'daarna'. Speaker1: 'Daarna' means 'after that'. Speaker1: Listen carefully to this sentence: Speaker1: Daarna speel ik een spel. Speaker1: After that I play a game. Speaker1: It shows what happens after an action. Speaker1: Here is one more example: Speaker1: Daarna kook ik het eten. Speaker1: After that I cook the dinner. Speaker1: So, 'daarna' means 'after that'. Speaker1: Now let's look at 'tot slot'. Speaker1: 'Tot slot' means 'finally' or 'in conclusion'. Speaker1: Here is an example: Speaker1: Tot slot drink ik thee. Speaker1: Finally I drink tea. Speaker1: Use 'tot slot' at the end of a series. Speaker1: Let's practice with another sentence: Speaker1: Tot slot ga ik slapen. Speaker1: Finally I go to sleep. Speaker1: Remember: 'tot slot' means 'finally'. Speaker1: Moving on, our fifth word is 'ook'. Speaker1: 'Ook' means 'also' or 'too'. Speaker1: Listen to this simple example: Speaker1: Ik wil ook een koekje. Speaker1: I also want a cookie. Speaker1: It adds extra information. Speaker1: Here is another sentence: Speaker1: Hij komt ook naar school. Speaker1: He is also coming to school. Speaker1: So, 'ook' means 'also'. Speaker1: Our sixth word is 'wel'. Speaker1: 'Wel' is used to express agreement or positive emphasis. Speaker1: Let's hear an example sentence: Speaker1: Ik spreek wel Nederlands. Speaker1: I do speak Dutch. Speaker1: It gives a warm or affirmative tone. Speaker1: Here is another example: Speaker1: Het is wel een mooi huis. Speaker1: It is indeed a nice house. Speaker1: Remember: 'wel' adds positive emphasis. Speaker1: The seventh word is 'toch'. Speaker1: 'Toch' means 'still', 'anyway', or 'after all'. Speaker1: Listen to this sentence: Speaker1: Ik ga toch naar buiten. Speaker1: I am going outside anyway. Speaker1: It often expresses an action despite something else. Speaker1: Let me give you another example: Speaker1: Het is toch heel warm. Speaker1: It is still very warm. Speaker1: So, 'toch' means 'still' or 'anyway'. Speaker1: Our eighth word is 'eigenlijk'. Speaker1: 'Eigenlijk' means 'actually' or 'in fact'. Speaker1: Here is an example: Speaker1: Ik ben eigenlijk heel moe. Speaker1: I am actually very tired. Speaker1: It helps share a genuine feeling or fact. Speaker1: Let's listen to another sentence: Speaker1: Dat is eigenlijk erg goed. Speaker1: That is actually very good. Speaker1: Remember: 'eigenlijk' means 'actually'. Speaker1: You did a fantastic job today! Speaker1: Let's do a quick spoken recap of all eight words. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we deze handige woorden. Speaker1: Today we learned these useful words. Speaker1: Eerst, dan, daarna en tot slot. Speaker1: First, then, after that, and finally. Speaker1: Ook, wel, toch en eigenlijk. Speaker1: Also, indeed, anyway, and actually. Speaker1: Keep practicing these linking words in your daily Dutch conversations. Speaker1: See you next time! Goodbye!	29	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_months	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #4: The Months | A1 Beginners	De Maanden van het Jaar		In this lesson we cover: De Maanden van het Jaar (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• januari\n• februari\n• de eerste maand\n• het is lente\n• het is zomer\n• het is herfst\n• de laatste maand\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• januari — January\n• februari — February\n• maart — March\n• april — April\n• mei — May\n• juni — June\n• juli — July\n• augustus — August\n• september — September\n• oktober — October\n• november — November\n• december — December\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Capitalization of Months: In Dutch, the names of the months are written in lowercase letters, unlike in English, unless they appear at the beginning of a sentence.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Het is koud in januari.\n\nMini Examples\n- Het is koud in januari.\n- December is de laatste maand.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	5G-W_lkL6WY	\N	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn the months. Speaker1: We learn twelve months in Dutch. Speaker1: Are you ready? Speaker1: Laten we nu beginnen! Speaker1: "Let's begin now!" Speaker1: Our first month is January. Speaker1: In Dutch, January is januari. Speaker1: Het is koud in januari. Speaker1: "It is cold in January." Speaker1: [laughs] Januari is de eerste maand. Speaker1: "January is the first month." Speaker1: Great job! Speaker1: Our second month is February. Speaker1: In Dutch, February is februari. Speaker1: [curious] Ik ben jarig in februari. Speaker1: "My birthday is in February." Speaker1: Februari is een korte maand. Speaker1: "February is a short month." Speaker1: Next, we have March. Speaker1: In Dutch, March is maart. Speaker1: Het is lente in maart. Speaker1: "It is spring in March." Speaker1: Ik werk veel in m