cw_numbers_large	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #14: Large Numbers | A1 Beginners	Grote getallen in het Nederlands	Large Numbers in Dutch	In this lesson we cover: Grote getallen in het Nederlands (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• honderd euro\n• duizend mensen\n• een miljoen inwoners\n• twee euro vijftig\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• honderd — hundred\n• tweehonderd — two hundred\n• duizend — thousand\n• vijfduizend — five thousand\n• miljoen — million\n• euro — euro\n• cent — cent\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Large Numbers in Dutch: In Dutch, hundred (honderd) and thousand (duizend) are combined directly with unit digits without spaces (e.g., tweehonderd, vijfduizend). Million (miljoen) is written as a separate word (e.g., een miljoen).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- honderd (100)\n\nMini Examples\n- honderd (100)\n- tweehonderd (200)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	D0yJRBWe1zo	265	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson! Speaker1: Today we will learn large numbers in Dutch. Speaker1: We will learn words like hundred, thousand, and million. Speaker1: We will also talk about prices and years. Speaker1: Are you ready to begin? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's start! Speaker1: Our first key word is HONDERD. Speaker1: Honderd means hundred. Speaker1: Het boek kost honderd euro. Speaker1: The book costs one hundred euros. Speaker1: Ik zie honderd mensen hier. Speaker1: I see one hundred people here. Speaker1: Next is TWEEHONDERD. Speaker1: Tweehonderd means two hundred. Speaker1: Hij heeft tweehonderd boeken thuis. Speaker1: He has two hundred books at home. Speaker1: Dit hotel heeft tweehonderd kamers. Speaker1: This hotel has two hundred rooms. Speaker1: Now let's look at DRIEHONDERD. Speaker1: Driehonderd means three hundred. Speaker1: De fiets kost driehonderd euro. Speaker1: The bicycle costs three hundred euros. Speaker1: Great! Now let's move to thousands. Speaker1: The word for thousand is DUIZEND. Speaker1: Er wonen duizend mensen hier. Speaker1: One thousand people live here. Speaker1: De auto kost duizend euro. Speaker1: The car costs one thousand euros. Speaker1: Next is VIJFDUIZEND. Speaker1: Vijfduizend means five thousand. Speaker1: Hij verdient vijfduizend euro. Speaker1: He earns five thousand euros. Speaker1: Now for even bigger numbers! Speaker1: The word for million is MILJOEN. Speaker1: Amsterdam heeft een miljoen inwoners. Speaker1: Amsterdam has one million inhabitants. Speaker1: Now let's practice prices. Speaker1: In Dutch, we use EURO and CENT. Speaker1: De koffie kost twee euro vijftig. Speaker1: The coffee costs two euros fifty. Speaker1: De broek kost vijftig euro. Speaker1: The pants cost fifty euros. Speaker1: Een appel kost tachtig cent. Speaker1: An apple costs eighty cents. Speaker1: Now let's learn how to express years. Speaker1: For years in the 1900s, we say nineteen hundred followed by the number. Speaker1: Ik ben geboren in negentienennegentig. Speaker1: I was born in nineteen ninety-nine. Speaker1: For years in the 2000s, we say tweeduizend followed by the number. Speaker1: Het is nu tweeduizendvierentwintig. Speaker1: It is now two thousand twenty-four. Speaker1: Mijn vriend woont hier sinds tweeduizendtien. Speaker1: My friend lives here since two thousand ten. Speaker1: Fantastic practice today! Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: honderd, duizend, en miljoen. Speaker1: Today we learned: hundred, thousand, and million. Speaker1: Dank je wel en tot ziens! Speaker1: Thank you and see you soon!	30	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_opposites	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #28: Common Opposites | A1 Beginners	Veelvoorkomende Tegenstellingen	Common Opposites in Dutch	In this lesson we cover: Veelvoorkomende Tegenstellingen (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• groot en klein\n• warm en koud\n• snel en langzaam\n• oud en jong\n• duur en goedkoop\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• groot — big / large\n• klein — small / little\n• warm — warm / hot\n• koud — cold\n• snel — fast / quick\n• langzaam — slow\n• oud — old\n• jong — young\n• duur — expensive\n• goedkoop — cheap\n• licht — light / bright\n• donker — dark\n• hoog — high / tall\n• laag — low\n• open — open\n• dicht — closed\n• blij — happy\n• verdrietig — sad\n• mooi — beautiful / pretty\n• lelijk — ugly\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Adjectives in Predicate Position: In Dutch, when an adjective comes after the verb 'zijn' (to be) to describe a noun, it remains in its basic uninflected form without adding an '-e'.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Het huis is groot. (The house is big.)\n\nMini Examples\n- Het huis is groot. (The house is big.)\n- De muis is klein. (The mouse is small.)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	1ykYJwbaZ7o	455	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Welcome to today's Dutch vocabulary lesson. Speaker1: Today we are going to learn common opposite words in Dutch. Speaker1: Learning opposites helps us describe things easily in everyday conversations. Speaker1: Tegenstellingen in het Nederlands. Speaker1: Opposites in Dutch. Speaker1: Are you ready to practice together? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Our first pair of words is GROOT and KLEIN. Speaker1: Groot means big or large. Speaker1: Het huis is heel groot. Speaker1: The house is very big. Speaker1: Klein means small or little. Speaker1: De muis is erg klein. Speaker1: The mous