LELIJK. Speaker1: Mooi means beautiful or pretty. Speaker1: Die bloem is erg mooi. Speaker1: That flower is very beautiful. Speaker1: Lelijk means ugly. Speaker1: Het oude schilderij is lelijk. Speaker1: The old painting is ugly. Speaker1: Great job today! Let's do a quick spoken recap. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: Speaker1: Today we learned: Speaker1: groot, klein, warm, koud, Speaker1: big, small, warm, cold, Speaker1: snel, langzaam, oud, jong, Speaker1: fast, slow, old, young, Speaker1: duur, goedkoop, licht, donker, Speaker1: expensive, cheap, light, dark, Speaker1: hoog, laag, open, dicht, Speaker1: high, low, open, closed, Speaker1: blij, verdrietig, mooi, en lelijk. Speaker1: happy, sad, beautiful, and ugly. Speaker1: Fantastic work! Keep practicing these words every day. Speaker1: See you in the next lesson!	15	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_ordinal_numbers	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #13: Ordinal Numbers | A1 Beginners	Rangtelwoorden: De Eerste tot de Tiende	Ordinal Numbers: First to Tenth	In this lesson we cover: Rangtelwoorden: De Eerste tot de Tiende (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• eerste dag\n• tweede verdieping\n• derde kop\n• vierde boek\n• vijfde vraag\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• eerste — first\n• tweede — second\n• derde — third\n• vierde — fourth\n• vijfde — fifth\n• zesde — sixth\n• zevende — seventh\n• achtste — eighth\n• negende — ninth\n• tiende — tenth\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Ordinal Numbers Formation: In Dutch, ordinal numbers are formed by adding -de or -ste to the cardinal number. Numbers 1 (eerste) and 3 (derde) are irregular exceptions.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- twee -> tweede\n\nMini Examples\n- twee -> tweede\n- vier -> vierde\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	p0O0pQdnxoU	395	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn ordinal numbers from first to tenth. Speaker1: Ordinal numbers show order and position. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let us begin! Speaker1: Our first ordinal word is 'eerste', which means first. Speaker1: Dit is mijn eerste dag. Speaker1: This is my first day. Speaker1: Next is 'tweede', which means second. Speaker1: Hij woont op de tweede verdieping. Speaker1: He lives on the second floor. Speaker1: Third in Dutch is 'derde'. Speaker1: Het is mijn derde kop koffie. Speaker1: It is my third cup of coffee. Speaker1: Fourth in Dutch is 'vierde'. Speaker1: Zij leest het vierde boek. Speaker1: She reads the fourth book. Speaker1: Fifth in Dutch is 'vijfde'. Speaker1: Dit is de vijfde vraag. Speaker1: This is the fifth question. Speaker1: Sixth in Dutch is 'zesde'. Speaker1: We zitten in de zesde klas. Speaker1: We are in the sixth class. Speaker1: Seventh in Dutch is 'zevende'. Speaker1: Zondag is de zevende dag. Speaker1: Sunday is the seventh day. Speaker1: Eighth in Dutch is 'achtste'. Speaker1: Augustus is de achtste maand. Speaker1: August is the eighth month. Speaker1: Ninth in Dutch is 'negende'. Speaker1: Dat is de negende auto. Speaker1: That is the ninth car. Speaker1: Tenth in Dutch is 'tiende'. Speaker1: Dit is de tiende pagina. Speaker1: This is the tenth page. Speaker1: Now let's review simple sentences together. Speaker1: De eerste man koopt een appel. Speaker1: The first man buys an apple. Speaker1: De tweede man koopt een peer. Speaker1: The second man buys a pear. Speaker1: De derde man drinkt water. Speaker1: The third man drinks water. Speaker1: Great job today! Speaker1: Let's summarize the ordinal numbers we learned. Speaker1: Vandaag herhalen we deze woorden. Speaker1: Today we repeat these words. Speaker1: Eerste, tweede, derde, vierde, vijfde. Speaker1: First, second, third, fourth, fifth. Speaker1: Zesde, zevende, achtste, negende, tiende. Speaker1: Sixth, seventh, eighth, ninth, tenth. Speaker1: Goed gedaan en tot de volgende keer! Speaker1: Well done and see you next time!	20	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_polite_basics	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #8: Polite Basics | A1 Beginners	Beleefde Woorden	Polite Basics	In this lesson we cover: Beleefde Woorden (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• alsjeblieft\n• dank je wel\n• pardon\n• het spijt me\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• ja — yes\n• nee — no\n• oké — okay\n• alsjeblieft — please / here you go (informal)\n• alstublieft — please / here you go (formal)\n• dank je — thank you (informal)\n• dank u — thank you (formal)\n• graag — gladly / with pleasure\n• pardon — excuse me\n• sorry — sorry\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Informal vs. Formal Politeness: Use 'alsjeblieft' and 'dank je' with friends, family, and young people. Use 'alstublieft' and 'dank u' when addressing elders, strangers, or in professional settings.\n- Using 'Graag' in Responses: 'Graag' means 'gladly' or 'with pleasure'. When offered something like food or drink, answering 'Ja, graag!' is the standard polite way to say 'Yes, please!'\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Een koffie, alsjeblieft. (informal)\n- Koffie? - Ja, graag!\n\nMini Examples\n- Een koffie, alsjeblieft. (informal)\n- Een moment, alstublieft. (formal)\n- Koffie? - Ja, graag!\n- Ik help je graag.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	4fPgYBPSmYc	271	Speaker1: Hello everyone, welcome to today's Dutch lesson! Speaker1: Today we are going to learn essential polite response words in Dutch. Speaker1: These short words will help you respond politely in everyday conversations. Speaker1: We will cover words like yes, no, please, thank you, and excuse me. Speaker1: Listen carefully to the pronunciation and practice along with me. Speaker1: Laten we samen beginnen! Speaker1: Our first word is JA, which means YES. Speaker1: Ja, ik wil koffie drinken. Speaker1: Ja, dat is heel goed. Speaker1: Our second word is NEE, which means NO. Speaker1: Nee, ik drink geen thee. Speaker1: Nee, dat wil ik niet. Speaker1: Next, we have OKÉ, which means OKAY. Speaker1: Oké, dat is helemaal prima. Speaker1: Oké, wij gaan nu beginnen. Speaker1: Now let's learn GOED, which means GOOD or WELL. Speaker1: Het gaat heel goed vandaag. Speaker1: Dat is een goed idee. Speaker1: Next is ALSJEBLIEFT, which means PLEASE or HERE YOU GO when speaking informally. Speaker1: Een kopje koffie, alsjeblieft. Speaker1: Hier is jouw water, alsjeblieft. Speaker1: To answer, we say DANK JE, which means THANK YOU. Speaker1: Dank je voor jouw hulp. Speaker1: Dank je wel voor alles. Speaker1: In formal situations, we use ALSTUBLIEFT for PLEASE or HERE YOU GO. Speaker1: Een moment geduld, alstublieft. Speaker1: Alstublieft, uw sleutel is hier. Speaker1: The formal way to say THANK YOU is DANK U. Speaker1: Dank u wel, meneer. Speaker1: Dank u voor de informatie. Speaker1: Next, we have GRAAG, which means GLADLY or WITH PLEASURE. Speaker1: Ik help jou heel graag. Speaker1: Ja, heel graag een koffie. Speaker1: To get someone's attention politely, use PARDON, which means EXCUSE ME. Speaker1: Pardon, waar is het station? Speaker1: Pardon, ik zoek de winkel. Speaker1: To apologize, use SORRY, which means SORRY. Speaker1: Sorry, ik ben te laat. Speaker1: Sorry, dat is mijn fout. Speaker1: A slightly more sincere apology is HET SPIJT ME, which means I AM SORRY. Speaker1: Het spijt me heel erg. Speaker1: Het spijt me, ik weet het niet. Speaker1: Now let's look at basic greetings. First, HALLO, which means HELLO. Speaker1: Hallo, hoe gaat het vandaag? Speaker1: Hallo, fijn je te zien. Speaker1: Next is DAG, which can mean HELLO or BYE. Speaker1: Dag, ik ga nu weg. Speaker1: Dag, ik wens jou een fijne dag! Speaker1: And finally, TOT ZIENS, which means GOODBYE. Speaker1: Tot ziens en fijne dag! Speaker1: Tot ziens, tot de volgende keer! Speaker1: Great job today! Let's do a quick spoken recap of the main words. Speaker1: Vandaag leren we: ja, nee, oké. Speaker1: En ook: alsjeblieft, graag, pardon, tot ziens. Speaker1: Keep practicing these essential polite response words every day! Speaker1: See you in the next Dutch lesson!	31	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_prepositions	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #18: Prepositions | A1 Beginners	Voorzetsels		In this lesson we cover: Voorzetsels (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• in de tuin\n• op tafel\n• aan de muur\n• bij het station\n• van de markt\n• naar huis\n• voor jou\n• met melk\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• in — in / inside\n• op — on / at\n• aan — on / at\n• bij — at / near / with\n• van — from / of\n• naar — to / towards\n• voor — for / in front of\n• met — with\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Dutch Prepositions: Prepositions link nouns or pronouns to other words in a sentence, indicating time, position, or direction.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik woon in Amsterdam. (I live in Amsterdam.)\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik woon in Amsterdam. (I live in Amsterdam.)\n- Het boek ligt op tafel. (The book lies on the table.)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	B4puYlvafTE	\N	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson! Speaker1: I am your teacher. Speaker1: Today we learn common prepositions. Speaker1: Prepositions connect words in a sentence. Speaker1: They show location, time, and direction. Speaker1: [curious] Are you ready to learn Dutch? Speaker1: We will learn eight basic prepositions. Speaker1: Listen closely to the example sentences. Speaker1: Repeat after me to practice pronunciation. Speaker1: Let's begin our Dutch lesson! Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: That means: Let's begin! Speaker1: Succes met de les! Speaker1: Good luck with the lesson! Speaker1: First, we look at IN. Speaker1: IN means in or inside. Speaker1: Here is the first sentence. Speaker1: Ik woon in Amsterdam. Speaker1: I live in Amsterdam. Speaker1: Here is the second sentence. Speaker1: De kat zit in de doos. Speaker1: The cat sits in the box. Speaker1: Here is the third sentence. Speaker1: Wij zijn in de tuin. Speaker1: We are in the garden. Speaker1: Remember: IN means in. Speaker1: Next, we look at OP. Speaker1: OP usually means on. Speaker1: Let's listen to sentence one. Speaker1: Het boek ligt op tafel. Speaker1: The book lies on the table. Speaker1: Now sentence two. Speaker1: Jan zit op de stoel. Speaker1: Jan sits on the chair. Speaker1: Now sentence three. Speaker1: De vogel zit op het dak. Speaker1: The bird sits on the roof. Speaker1: Remember: OP means on. Speaker1: Our third preposition is AAN. Speaker1: AAN means at or on. Speaker1: Let's listen to example one. Speaker1: [curious] De lamp hangt aan de muur. Speaker1: The lamp hangs on the wall. Speaker1: Here is example two. Speaker1: Wij zitten aan de tafel. Speaker1: We sit at the table. Speaker1: Here is example three. Speaker1: Hij staat aan de deur. Speaker1: He stands at the door. Speaker1: Remember: AAN means at or on. Speaker1: The fourth word is BIJ. Speaker1: BIJ means near, at, or with. Speaker1: Let's hear sentence one. Speaker1: Ik ben bij het station. Speaker1: I am at the station. Speaker1: Let's hear sentence two. Speaker1: Zij woont bij haar moeder. Speaker1: She lives with her mother. Speaker1: Let's hear sentence three. Speaker1: De hond slaapt bij de kast. Speaker1: The dog sleeps near the closet. Speaker1: Remember: BIJ means at or near. Speaker1: Our fifth preposition is VAN. Speaker1: VAN means from or of. Speaker1: [sighs] Let's look at sentence one. Speaker1: Ik kom van de markt. Speaker1: I come from the market. Speaker1: Sentence two is simple. Speaker1: Dit is het boek van Tom. Speaker1: This is Tom's book. Speaker1: Sentence three comes now. Speaker1: Wij houden van de zon. Speaker1: We love the sun. Speaker1: Remember: VAN means from or of. Speaker1: The sixth word is NAAR. Speaker1: NAAR means to or towards. Speaker1: Let me show sentence one. Speaker1: Ik ga naar huis. Speaker1: I go home. Speaker1: Now sentence number two. Speaker1: Zij fietst naar de school. Speaker1: She cycles to school. Speaker1: Now sentence number three. Speaker1: [amazed] Wij reizen naar de stad. Speaker1: We travel to the city. Speaker1: Remember: NAAR means to. Speaker1: Our seventh preposition is VOOR. Speaker1: VOOR means for or in front of. Speaker1: Here is sentence one. Speaker1: Dit cadeau is voor jou. Speaker1: This gift is for you. Speaker1: Here is sentence two. Speaker1: De auto staat voor het huis. Speaker1: The car is in front of the house. Speaker1: Here is sentence three. Speaker1: Ik wacht voor de winkel. Speaker1: I wait in front of the shop. Speaker1: Remember: VOOR means for. Speaker1: Our final preposition is MET. Speaker1: MET means with. Speaker1: Listen to the first sentence. Speaker1: Ik drink koffie met melk. Speaker1: I drink coffee with milk. Speaker1: Listen to the second sentence. Speaker1: Zij praat met de dokter. Speaker1: She talks with the doctor. Speaker1: Listen to the third sentence. Speaker1: Wij eten pizza met kaas. Speaker1: We eat pizza with cheese. Speaker1: Remember: MET means with. Speaker1: Great job learning these words today! Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we deze voorzetsels. Speaker1: Today we learned these prepositions. Speaker1: In, op, aan, en bij. Speaker1: Van, naar, voor, en met. Speaker1: Practice these sentences every day! Speaker1: See you next time! Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer!	6	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_professions_basic	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #21: Basic Professions | A1 Beginners	Beroepen in het Nederlands	Basic Professions in Dutch	In this lesson we cover: Beroepen in het Nederlands (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Hij is een leraar.\n• De dokter werkt in het ziekenhuis.\n• De kok maakt lekker eten.\n• De politieagent helpt mensen op straat.\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• leraar — teacher\n• dokter — doctor\n• kok — cook\n• politieagent — police officer\n• verpleegkundige — nurse\n• ingenieur — engineer\n• bakker — baker\n• chauffeur — driver\n• verkoper — salesperson\n• student — student\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Stating Your Profession in Dutch: To state a profession in Dutch, use 'zijn' (to be) + 'een' + profession name, or simply 'zijn' + profession name.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Hij is een leraar.\n\nMini Examples\n- Hij is een leraar.\n- Ik ben verpleegkundige.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	SBmn-Ys8lpA	311	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Welcome to today's Dutch vocabulary lesson. Speaker1: Today, we are going to learn common professions in Dutch. Speaker1: Learning names of jobs will help you talk about work and daily life. Speaker1: Are you ready to begin? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Our first profession is teacher, which in Dutch is leraar. Speaker1: Hij is een leraar. Speaker1: He is a teacher. Speaker1: De leraar helpt de kinderen. Speaker1: The teacher helps the children. Speaker1: Next, we have doctor, which is dokter in Dutch. Speaker1: Zij is een dokter. Speaker1: She is a doctor. Speaker1: De dokter werkt in het ziekenhuis. Speaker1: The doctor works in the hospital. Speaker1: Our third word is cook, which is kok in Dutch. Speaker1: Hij is een goede kok. Speaker1: He is a good cook. Speaker1: De kok maakt lekker eten. Speaker1: The cook makes delicious food. Speaker1: Next is police officer, called politieagent in Dutch. Speaker1: Zij is een vriendelijke politieagent. Speaker1: She is a friendly police officer. Speaker1: De politieagent helpt mensen op straat. Speaker1: The police officer helps people on the street. Speaker1: Now, let's learn nurse, which is verpleegkundige. Speaker1: Ik ben een verpleegkundige. Speaker1: I am a nurse. Speaker1: De verpleegkundige zorgt voor zieke mensen. Speaker1: The nurse takes care of sick people. Speaker1: Our next profession is engineer, which is ingenieur in Dutch. Speaker1: Hij is een slimme ingenieur. Speaker1: He is a smart engineer. Speaker1: De ingenieur ontwerpt een mooie brug. Speaker1: The engineer designs a beautiful bridge. Speaker1: Next, we have baker, which is bakker. Speaker1: Zij is een vriendelijke bakker. Speaker1: She is a friendly baker. Speaker1: De bakker bakt elke dag brood. Speaker1: The baker bakes bread every day. Speaker1: Our eighth word is driver, which is chauffeur in Dutch. Speaker1: Hij is een fijne chauffeur. Speaker1: He is a nice driver. Speaker1: De chauffeur rijdt met de bus. Speaker1: The driver drives the bus. Speaker1: Now let's learn salesperson, which is verkoper. Speaker1: Zij is een actieve verkoper. Speaker1: She is an active salesperson. Speaker1: De verkoper werkt in een winkel. Speaker1: The salesperson works in a shop. Speaker1: And finally, student, which is student in Dutch. Speaker1: Ik ben een ijverige student. Speaker1: I am a diligent student. Speaker1: De student studeert aan de school. Speaker1: The student studies at the school. Speaker1: Great job today! Let's do a quick recap of the words we learned. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: leraar, dokter, kok. Speaker1: Today we learned: teacher, doctor, cook. Speaker1: Ook politieagent, verpleegkundige en ingenieur. Speaker1: Also police officer, nurse, and engineer. Speaker1: En ten slotte bakker, chauffeur en verkoper. Speaker1: And finally baker, driver, and salesperson. Speaker1: Keep practicing these profession names! Speaker1: Bedankt voor het kijken en tot ziens! Speaker1: Thanks for watching and see you soon!	23	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_pronouns	A1A2:start_here	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #7: Personal Pronouns | A1 Beginners	Persoonlijke Voornaamwoorden		In this lesson we cover: Persoonlijke Voornaamwoorden (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik lees een goed boek.\n• Jij drinkt warme thee.\n• Hij werkt in een winkel.\n• Zij zingt een mooi lied.\n• Wij eten samen een appel.\n• Jullie luisteren naar de muziek.\n• Zij wonen in een huis.\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• ik — I\n• jij — you (singular)\n• hij — he\n• zij — she / they\n• wij — we\n• jullie — you (plural)\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Dutch Subject Pronouns: In Dutch, personal pronouns are used to replace nouns acting as subjects. Note that 'zij' can mean both 'she' (singular) and 'they' (plural), depending on the verb conjugation.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Zij slaapt (She sleeps)\n\nMini Examples\n- Zij slaapt (She sleeps)\n- Zij slapen (They sleep)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	YvJ1Os9M7gE	\N	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn personal pronouns. Speaker1: Pronouns help us talk about people. Speaker1: They are very useful words. Speaker1: We will practice step by step. Speaker1: Are you ready to begin? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's start with the first word. Speaker1: The first pronoun is IK. Speaker1: IK means I in English. Speaker1: Listen to the first example sentence. Speaker1: Ik lees een goed boek. Speaker1: This means: I read a book. Speaker1: Listen to the second example sentence. Speaker1: [curious] Ik drink een glas water. Speaker1: This means: I drink water. Speaker1: Listen to the third example sentence. Speaker1: Ik woon in een stad. Speaker1: This means: I live in a city. Speaker1: Remember, IK means I. Speaker1: [curious] The next pronoun is JIJ. Speaker1: JIJ means you in English. Speaker1: We use JIJ for one person. Speaker1: Listen to the first example sentence. Speaker1: Jij leest een leuk boek. Speaker1: This means: You read a book. Speaker1: [amazed] Jij drinkt warme thee. Speaker1: This means: You drink warm tea. Speaker1: Listen to the third example sentence. Speaker1: Jij woont in Nederland. Speaker1: This means: You live in Holland. Speaker1: Remember, JIJ means you. Speaker1: [serious] Now we look at HIJ. Speaker1: HIJ means he in English. Speaker1: Listen to the first example sentence. Speaker1: Hij werkt in een winkel. Speaker1: This means: He works in a shop. Speaker1: Listen to the second example sentence. Speaker1: Hij fietst naar het werk. Speaker1: This means: He cycles to work. Speaker1: Listen to the third example sentence. Speaker1: Hij kookt een lekkere soep. Speaker1: This means: He cooks tasty soup. Speaker1: Remember, HIJ means he. Speaker1: [curious] Next, we have ZIJ. Speaker1: ZIJ can mean she in English. Speaker1: Listen to the first example sentence. Speaker1: Zij slaapt in het bed. Speaker1: This means: She sleeps in the bed. Speaker1: Listen to the second example sentence. Speaker1: [amazed] Zij zingt een mooi lied. Speaker1: This means: She sings a song. Speaker1: Listen to the third example sentence. Speaker1: Zij leert de Nederlandse taal. Speaker1: This means: She learns the Dutch language. Speaker1: Remember, ZIJ means she. Speaker1: Now let's learn WIJ. Speaker1: WIJ means we in English. Speaker1: Listen to the first example sentence. Speaker1: Wij eten samen een appel. Speaker1: This means: We eat an apple. Speaker1: Listen to the second example sentence. Speaker1: [whispers] Wij lopen in het park. Speaker1: This means: We walk in the park. Speaker1: Listen to the third example sentence. Speaker1: Wij praten in de klas. Speaker1: This means: We talk in the class. Speaker1: Remember, WIJ means we. Speaker1: [serious] Next we have JULLIE. Speaker1: JULLIE means you all in English. Speaker1: We use it for many people. Speaker1: Listen to the first example sentence. Speaker1: Jullie luisteren naar de muziek. Speaker1: This means: You all listen to music. Speaker1: Listen to the second example sentence. Speaker1: Jullie maken een mooie tekening. Speaker1: This means: You all make a drawing. Speaker1: Listen to the third example sentence. Speaker1: Jullie spelen in de tuin. Speaker1: This means: You all play inside. Speaker1: Remember, JULLIE means you all. Speaker1: [curious] Finally, we have ZIJ again. Speaker1: Here, ZIJ means they. Speaker1: Listen to the first example sentence. Speaker1: Zij wonen in een huis. Speaker1: This means: They live in a house. Speaker1: Listen to the second example sentence. Speaker1: [amazed] Zij drinken een kop koffie. Speaker1: This means: They drink coffee. Speaker1: Listen to the third example sentence. Speaker1: Zij lachen om een grap. Speaker1: This means: They laugh at a joke. Speaker1: Remember, ZIJ also means they. Speaker1: Great job on all seven words! Speaker1: Let's do a short recap now. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we deze woorden. Speaker1: Today we learned these words. Speaker1: Ik en jij. Speaker1: I and you. Speaker1: Hij en zij. Speaker1: He and she. Speaker1: Wij en jullie. Speaker1: We and you all. Speaker1: En ook zij. Speaker1: And also they. Speaker1: Practice these words every day! Speaker1: [gasp] You are doing fantastic! Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer! Speaker1: See you next time!	2	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_quantities	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #25: Quantity Words | A1 Beginners	Hoeveelheden	Quantity Words	In this lesson we cover: Hoeveelheden (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• veel water\n• weinig tijd\n• meer koffie\n• minder suiker\n• genoeg geld\n• heel goed\n• erg koud\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• veel — much / many / a lot\n• weinig — little / few\n• meer — more\n• minder — less / fewer\n• genoeg — enough\n• heel — very\n• erg — very\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Dutch Quantity Words: Quantity words like 'veel' (much/many), 'weinig' (little/few), 'meer' (more), 'minder' (less), and 'genoeg' (enough) are placed directly before nouns or verbs to indicate amounts.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik drink veel water.\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik drink veel water.\n- Wij hebben weinig tijd.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	HD3C58G5-28	149	Speaker1: Welcome to our Dutch lesson! Speaker1: Today we learn quantity words. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Our first word is VEEL. Speaker1: VEEL means a lot or many. Speaker1: Ik drink veel water. Speaker1: Zij heeft veel boeken. Speaker1: Next, we have WEINIG. Speaker1: WEINIG means little or few. Speaker1: Hij slaapt weinig uren. Speaker1: Wij hebben weinig tijd. Speaker1: Now we learn MEER. Speaker1: MEER means more in English. Speaker1: Ik wil meer koffie. Speaker1: Jij koopt meer appels. Speaker1: The opposite is MINDER. Speaker1: MINDER means less or fewer. Speaker1: Hij eet minder suiker. Speaker1: Zij werkt minder uren. Speaker1: Our next word is GENOEG. Speaker1: GENOEG means enough. Speaker1: Ik heb genoeg geld. Speaker1: Er is genoeg eten. Speaker1: Now let's practice HEEL. Speaker1: HEEL means very. Speaker1: Dit is heel goed. Speaker1: Het is heel warm. Speaker1: Another word is ERG. Speaker1: ERG also means very. Speaker1: Het is erg koud. Speaker1: Dat is erg leuk. Speaker1: Let's review our new words. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: veel, weinig, meer. Speaker1: En ook minder, genoeg, heel, erg. Speaker1: Great job today! Speaker1: See you next time!	22	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_question_words	A1A2:start_here	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #6: Question Words | A1 Beginners	Vraagwoorden in het Nederlands	Dutch Question Words	In this lesson we cover: Vraagwoorden in het Nederlands (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Wie is die man?\n• Wat doe je nu?\n• Waar is het station?\n• Wanneer komt de trein?\n• Hoe gaat het vandaag?\n• Waarom lach je zo?\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• wie — who\n• wat — what\n• waar — where\n• wanneer — when\n• hoe — how\n• waarom — why\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Dutch Question Words Structure: In Dutch, question words come at the very beginning of the sentence, followed immediately by the verb and then the subject.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Waar is het station?\n\nMini Examples\n- Waar is het station?\n- Wat doe je nu?\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	rS-ju1BIipU	\N	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn Dutch question words. Speaker1: These words help you ask questions. Speaker1: Question words are very useful. Speaker1: Are you ready to begin? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Our first word is WIE. Speaker1: WIE means who in English. Speaker1: Let me show you three examples. Speaker1: Wie is die man? Speaker1: Who is that man? Speaker1: Wie leest een boek? Speaker1: Who reads a book? Speaker1: Wie heeft het geld? Speaker1: Who has the money? Speaker1: So, WIE means who. Speaker1: Our next word is WAT. Speaker1: WAT means what in English. Speaker1: Here are three simple examples. Speaker1: Wat doe je nu? Speaker1: What do you do now? Speaker1: Wat is jouw naam? Speaker1: What is your name? Speaker1: Wat eet je graag? Speaker1: What do you like to eat? Speaker1: Remember: WAT means what. Speaker1: Now we look at WAAR. Speaker1: WAAR means where in English. Speaker1: Listen to these example sentences. Speaker1: Waar woont de man? Speaker1: Where does the man live? Speaker1: Waar is het station? Speaker1: Where is the station? Speaker1: Waar werk je vandaag? Speaker1: Where do you work today? Speaker1: Great job! Speaker1: WAAR means where. Speaker1: Our fourth word is WANNEER. Speaker1: WANNEER means when in English. Speaker1: Let's practice with three sentences. Speaker1: Wanneer komt de trein? Speaker1: When does the train come? Speaker1: Wanneer eten we samen? Speaker1: When do we eat together? Speaker1: Wanneer begint het feest? Speaker1: When does the party start? Speaker1: So, WANNEER means when. Speaker1: Next, we have HOE. Speaker1: HOE means how in English. Speaker1: Here are our three examples. Speaker1: Hoe gaat het vandaag? Speaker1: How are you doing today? Speaker1: Hoe heet deze man? Speaker1: What is this man's name? Speaker1: Hoe maak je dat? Speaker1: How do you make that? Speaker1: HOE means how. Speaker1: Our final word is WAAROM. Speaker1: WAAROM means why in English. Speaker1: Let's look at three examples. Speaker1: Waarom lach je zo? Speaker1: Why do you laugh so? Speaker1: Waarom is hij hier? Speaker1: Why is he here? Speaker1: Waarom slaap je nu? Speaker1: Why do you sleep now? Speaker1: WAAROM means why. Speaker1: Now let me review all words. Speaker1: Let me repeat each word again. Speaker1: Wie is voor personen. Speaker1: Wie is for people. Speaker1: Wat is voor dingen. Speaker1: Wat is for things. Speaker1: Waar is voor plaatsen. Speaker1: Waar is for places. Speaker1: Wanneer is voor tijd. Speaker1: Wanneer is for time. Speaker1: Hoe is voor manier. Speaker1: Hoe is for manner. Speaker1: Waarom is voor reden. Speaker1: Waarom is for reason. Speaker1: You did a fantastic job today! Speaker1: Keep practicing every day! Speaker1: See you in the next lesson! Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer! Speaker1: Goodbye!	10	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_seasons	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #5: The Seasons | A1 Beginners	De Seizoenen	The Seasons	In this lesson we cover: De Seizoenen (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• de lente\n• de zomer\n• de herfst\n• de winter\n• het weer\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• de lente — spring\n• de zomer — summer\n• de herfst — autumn / fall\n• de winter — winter\n• het weer — the weather\n• de zon — the sun\n• de regen — the rain\n• de wind — the wind\n• de sneeuw — the snow\n• de wolk — the cloud\n• warm — warm\n• koud — cold\n• nat — wet\n• droog — dry\n• de paraplu — the umbrella\n• de jas — the coat / jacket\n• de storm — the storm\n• de schaduw — the shade / shadow\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Nouns and Gender (de / het): All four seasons in Dutch take the definite article 'de': de lente, de zomer, de herfst, de winter. Most weather elements also take 'de' (de zon, de regen, de wind, de sneeuw, de wolk), while weather itself takes 'het' (het weer).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- de zomer is warm\n\nMini Examples\n- de zomer is warm\n- het weer is goed\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	Zyuzq7mgA7U	329	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we are going to learn about the seasons and weather in Dutch. Speaker1: Learning weather words will help you talk about daily life easily. Speaker1: Are you ready to practice? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: First, let's learn the four main seasons. Speaker1: Our first word is de lente, which means spring. Speaker1: Het is lente in Nederland. Speaker1: De bloemen bloeien in de lente. Speaker1: Next is de zomer, which means summer. Speaker1: De zomer is heel mooi. Speaker1: Ik ga zwemmen in de zomer. Speaker1: Our third season is de herfst, which means autumn or fall. Speaker1: De herfst is erg nat. Speaker1: Het waait hard in de herfst. Speaker1: The fourth season is de winter, which means winter. Speaker1: De winter is koud. Speaker1: Het sneeuwt in de winter. Speaker1: Now, let's learn general weather vocabulary. Speaker1: The word het weer means the weather. Speaker1: Het weer is vandaag goed. Speaker1: Wat is het weer vandaag? Speaker1: Next, de zon means the sun. Speaker1: De zon schijnt vandaag fel. Speaker1: Ik zie de zon opkomen. Speaker1: The word de regen means the rain. Speaker1: De regen valt op de straat. Speaker1: Ik loop door de regen. Speaker1: Next is de wind, which means the wind. Speaker1: De wind is heel sterk. Speaker1: Ik voel de koude wind. Speaker1: The word de sneeuw means the snow. Speaker1: De sneeuw is erg koud. Speaker1: De kinderen spelen in de sneeuw. Speaker1: Next, de wolk means the cloud. Speaker1: Ik zie een grote wolk. Speaker1: De wolk is heel grijs. Speaker1: Now let's practice adjectives used to describe weather. Speaker1: The word warm means warm or hot. Speaker1: Het is vandaag erg warm. Speaker1: De thee is lekker warm. Speaker1: The word koud means cold. Speaker1: De wind is erg koud. Speaker1: Ik vind het ijs koud. Speaker1: The word nat means wet. Speaker1: Mijn jas is erg nat. Speaker1: De straat is vandaag nat. Speaker1: The word droog means dry. Speaker1: De grond is weer droog. Speaker1: Binnen blijven wij droog. Speaker1: Let's learn a few useful items for different weather conditions. Speaker1: The word de paraplu means the umbrella. Speaker1: Ik neem een paraplu mee. Speaker1: De paraplu is erg groot. Speaker1: The word de jas means the coat or jacket. Speaker1: Ik trek een jas aan. Speaker1: Deze jas is heel warm. Speaker1: The word de storm means the storm. Speaker1: Er komt een grote storm. Speaker1: De storm maakt veel lawaai. Speaker1: Finally, de schaduw means the shade or shadow. Speaker1: We zitten in de schaduw. Speaker1: De schaduw geeft wat koelte. Speaker1: Great job! Let's do a quick spoken recap of what we learned today. Speaker1: Today we learned: de lente, de zomer, de herfst, de winter, het weer, de zon, de regen, de wind, de sneeuw, de wolk, warm, koud, nat, droog, de paraplu, de jas, de storm, and de schaduw. Speaker1: Keep practicing these weather words every day! Speaker1: See you in the next Dutch lesson. Goodbye!	21	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_shopping_words	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #24: Shopping Words | A1 Beginners	Winkelwoorden	Dutch Shopping Words	In this lesson we cover: Winkelwoorden (A1-A2 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik ga naar de winkel\n• Wat is de prijs?\n• Ik betaal met een kaart\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• de winkel — the shop\n• kopen — to buy\n• de kassa — the checkout\n• de prijs — the price\n• betalen — to pay\n• duur — expensive\n• goedkoop — cheap\n• de korting — the discount\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Verbs in present tense: kopen and betalen: In Dutch, regular verbs drop -en to form the stem. Use the stem for 'ik' (koop, betaal) and full infinitive for 'wij' (kopen, betalen).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik koop een mooi boek.\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik koop een mooi boek.\n- Wij kopen vers brood.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	S9NGflX84s4	265	Speaker1: Hello and welcome to Dutch class! Speaker1: Today we learn common shopping words. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Our first word is winkel. Speaker1: Winkel means shop. Speaker1: Ik ga naar de winkel. Speaker1: I go to the shop. Speaker1: De winkel is open. Speaker1: The shop is open. Speaker1: Our second word is kopen. Speaker1: Kopen means to buy. Speaker1: Ik koop een mooi boek. Speaker1: I buy a beautiful book. Speaker1: Wij kopen vers brood. Speaker1: We buy fresh bread. Speaker1: Our third word is kassa. Speaker1: Kassa means checkout. Speaker1: Waar is de kassa? Speaker1: Where is the checkout? Speaker1: Ik sta bij de kassa. Speaker1: I stand at the checkout. Speaker1: Our fourth word is prijs. Speaker1: Prijs means price. Speaker1: Wat is de prijs? Speaker1: What is the price? Speaker1: De prijs is tien euro. Speaker1: The price is ten euros. Speaker1: Our fifth word is betalen. Speaker1: Betalen means to pay. Speaker1: Ik betaal met een kaart. Speaker1: I pay with a card. Speaker1: Betaal jij met contant geld? Speaker1: Do you pay with cash? Speaker1: Our sixth word is duur. Speaker1: Duur means expensive. Speaker1: Die jas is erg duur. Speaker1: That jacket is very expensive. Speaker1: Het boek is niet duur. Speaker1: The book is not expensive. Speaker1: Our seventh word is goedkoop. Speaker1: Goedkoop means cheap. Speaker1: Deze appels zijn goedkoop. Speaker1: These apples are cheap. Speaker1: De melk is heel goedkoop. Speaker1: The milk is very cheap. Speaker1: Our eighth word is korting. Speaker1: Korting means discount. Speaker1: Er is vandaag korting. Speaker1: There is a discount today. Speaker1: Ik krijg tien procent korting. Speaker1: I get ten percent discount. Speaker1: Let's review what we learned today. Speaker1: Wij kennen nu al deze woorden. Speaker1: We know all these words now. Speaker1: You did a fantastic job today! Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer! Speaker1: Until next time!	18	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_time_telling	A1A2:common_words	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #15: Telling Time | A1 Beginners	Hoe laat is het?	Telling Time in Dutch	In this lesson we cover: Hoe laat is het? (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Hoe laat is het?\n• Het is één uur.\n• Het is half twee.\n• Het is kwart over drie.\n• Het is kwart voor vier.\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• hoe laat — what time\n• het uur — the hour / o'clock\n• half — half past (halfway to)\n• kwart over — quarter past\n• kwart voor — quarter to\n• laat — late\n• vroeg — early\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Half Hours in Dutch: In Dutch, 'half' refers to the upcoming hour, not the past hour. For example, 'half twee' means 01:30 (halfway to two).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Het is half twee. (It is 1:30)\n\nMini Examples\n- Het is half twee. (It is 1:30)\n- Het is half drie. (It is 2:30)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	R7aUCqvT1JI	\N	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today, we will learn how to tell the time in Dutch. Speaker1: Telling time is very practical and easy once you know the pattern. Speaker1: Let's begin with our main question. Speaker1: Hoe laat is het? Speaker1: What time is it? Speaker1: You can ask this anytime you want to know the time. Speaker1: Hoe laat is het nu? Speaker1: What time is it now? Speaker1: Great! Now let's learn how to answer. Speaker1: To express full hours, we use the word UUR. Speaker1: Uur means hour or o'clock. Speaker1: Het is één uur. Speaker1: It is one o'clock. Speaker1: Notice how we say HET IS before the number. Speaker1: Het is twee uur. Speaker1: It is two o'clock. Speaker1: Let's try another full hour. Speaker1: Het is drie uur. Speaker1: It is three o'clock. Speaker1: And another one. Speaker1: Het is vier uur. Speaker1: It is four o'clock. Speaker1: Let's practice five o'clock. Speaker1: Het is vijf uur. Speaker1: It is five o'clock. Speaker1: Now let's look at half hours. Speaker1: In Dutch, HALF means half hour BEFORE the next hour. Speaker1: This is very important to remember! Speaker1: So, HALF TWEE means half an hour before two, which is one thirty. Speaker1: Het is half twee. Speaker1: It is half past one. Speaker1: Let's try another half hour example. Speaker1: Het is half drie. Speaker1: It is half past two. Speaker1: Remember, half drie means halfway to three. Speaker1: Het is half vier. Speaker1: It is half past three. Speaker1: Let's do one more half hour. Speaker1: Het is half vijf. Speaker1: It is half past four. Speaker1: Now let's move to quarter past an hour. Speaker1: We use the expression KWART OVER. Speaker1: Kwart over means quarter past. Speaker1: Het is kwart over één. Speaker1: It is quarter past one. Speaker1: Let's practice quarter past two. Speaker1: Het is kwart over twee. Speaker1: It is quarter past two. Speaker1: And quarter past three. Speaker1: Het is kwart over drie. Speaker1: It is quarter past three. Speaker1: Next, let's learn how to say quarter to. Speaker1: We use the expression KWART VOOR. Speaker1: Kwart voor means quarter to. Speaker1: Het is kwart voor één. Speaker1: It is quarter to one. Speaker1: Let's try quarter to two. Speaker1: Het is kwart voor twee. Speaker1: It is quarter to two. Speaker1: And quarter to three. Speaker1: Het is kwart voor drie. Speaker1: It is quarter to three. Speaker1: Let's talk about being early or late. Speaker1: The word for late in Dutch is LAAT. Speaker1: Ik ben te laat. Speaker1: I am too late. Speaker1: The word for early in Dutch is VROEG. Speaker1: Jij bent heel vroeg. Speaker1: You are very early. Speaker1: Let's combine what we learned in practical sentences. Speaker1: De trein komt om acht uur. Speaker1: The train comes at eight o'clock. Speaker1: Notice the word OM, which means AT for time. Speaker1: We eten om half zeven. Speaker1: We eat at half past six. Speaker1: Let's do a quick recap of today's key words. Speaker1: Vandaag leerden we: hoe laat is het, uur, half, kwart over, en kwart voor. Speaker1: You did a fantastic job today! Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer! Speaker1: Until next time!	13	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
cw_top_verbs	A1A2:start_here	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Common Words #9: Top 10 Dutch Verbs | A1 Beginners	De top 10 werkwoorden	Top 10 Dutch Verbs	In this lesson we cover: De top 10 werkwoorden (A1 - Common Words)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik ben ... (I am ...)\n• Ik heb ... (I have ...)\n• Ik ga ... (I go ...)\n• Ik kan ... (I can ...)\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• zijn — to be\n• hebben — to have\n• gaan — to go\n• komen — to come\n• doen — to do\n• zeggen — to say\n• zien — to see\n• weten — to know\n• willen — to want\n• kunnen — can / to be able to\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Basic Present Tense Conjugation: In Dutch, the verb changes according to the subject pronoun (ik, jij, hij/zij, wij/jullie/zij).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik ben (I am) / Wij zijn (We are)\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik ben (I am) / Wij zijn (We are)\n- Ik heb (I have) / Zij hebben (They have)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	MxtVTc3Q0tE	\N	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson! Speaker1: Today, we will learn ten essential Dutch verbs. Speaker1: Verbs are action words that help you build sentences. Speaker1: Knowing these ten verbs will help you start speaking Dutch right away. Speaker1: Are you ready to learn? Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Our first verb is ZIJN, which means to be. Speaker1: Let's look at an example sentence with ZIJN. Speaker1: Ik ben vandaag erg blij. Speaker1: I am very happy today. Speaker1: Let's listen to another sentence with ZIJN. Speaker1: Wij zijn nu samen thuis. Speaker1: We are together at home now. Speaker1: Remember: ZIJN means to be. Speaker1: Our second verb is HEBBEN, which means to have. Speaker1: Here is an example sentence with HEBBEN. Speaker1: Ik heb een kleine kat. Speaker1: I have a small cat. Speaker1: Let's try another example sentence. Speaker1: Zij hebben een mooi huis. Speaker1: They have a beautiful house. Speaker1: HEBBEN means to have. Speaker1: Next is the verb GAAN, which means to go. Speaker1: Here is how we use GAAN in a sentence. Speaker1: Ik ga nu naar school. Speaker1: I am going to school now. Speaker1: And another example with GAAN. Speaker1: Wij gaan samen naar buiten. Speaker1: We go outside together. Speaker1: Remember: GAAN means to go. Speaker1: The fourth verb is KOMEN, which means to come. Speaker1: Here is an example sentence. Speaker1: Hij komt snel naar huis. Speaker1: He comes home quickly. Speaker1: Let's listen to another sentence with KOMEN. Speaker1: Waar kom jij vandaag vandaan? Speaker1: Where do you come from today? Speaker1: KOMEN means to come. Speaker1: Next, we have the verb DOEN, which means to do. Speaker1: Here is a simple sentence using DOEN. Speaker1: Wat doe jij vandaag? Speaker1: What are you doing today? Speaker1: Let's hear another example with DOEN. Speaker1: Ik doe mijn werk goed. Speaker1: I do my job well. Speaker1: DOEN means to do. Speaker1: Our sixth verb is ZEGGEN, which means to say. Speaker1: Here is an example sentence with ZEGGEN. Speaker1: Wat zeg jij nu? Speaker1: What do you say now? Speaker1: Here is another sentence with ZEGGEN. Speaker1: Zij zegt hallo tegen mij. Speaker1: She says hello to me. Speaker1: ZEGGEN means to say. Speaker1: The seventh verb is ZIEN, which means to see. Speaker1: Let's hear an example sentence. Speaker1: Ik zie het grote huis. Speaker1: I see the big house. Speaker1: And another sentence using ZIEN. Speaker1: Zie jij die mooie vogel? Speaker1: Do you see that beautiful bird? Speaker1: ZIEN means to see. Speaker1: Next is WETEN, which means to know. Speaker1: Here is a sentence with WETEN. Speaker1: Ik weet het antwoord wel. Speaker1: I know the answer. Speaker1: Let's listen to another example sentence. Speaker1: Hij weet waar wij wonen. Speaker1: He knows where we live. Speaker1: WETEN means to know a fact or information. Speaker1: Verb number nine is WILLEN, which means to want. Speaker1: Here is an example with WILLEN. Speaker1: Ik wil graag koffie drinken. Speaker1: I would like to drink coffee. Speaker1: Let's try another sentence with WILLEN. Speaker1: Wij willen samen een koekje. Speaker1: We want a cookie together. Speaker1: WILLEN means to want. Speaker1: Our tenth verb is KUNNEN, which means can or to be able to. Speaker1: Here is an example sentence using KUNNEN. Speaker1: Ik kan goed Nederlands spreken. Speaker1: I can speak Dutch well. Speaker1: Let's hear another example with KUNNEN. Speaker1: Kan jij mij even helpen? Speaker1: Can you help me for a moment? Speaker1: KUNNEN means can or to be able to. Speaker1: Great job! Let's review the ten verbs we learned today. Speaker1: Listen carefully to each verb in a short sentence. Speaker1: Ik ben erg gelukkig. Speaker1: ZIJN means to be. Speaker1: Ik heb een boek. Speaker1: HEBBEN means to have. Speaker1: Ik ga naar huis. Speaker1: GAAN means to go. Speaker1: Ik kom snel aan. Speaker1: KOMEN means to come. Speaker1: Ik doe mijn best. Speaker1: DOEN means to do. Speaker1: Ik zeg het weer. Speaker1: ZEGGEN means to say. Speaker1: Ik zie de zon. Speaker1: ZIEN means to see. Speaker1: Ik weet de weg. Speaker1: WETEN means to know. Speaker1: Ik wil meer leren. Speaker1: WILLEN means to want. Speaker1: Ik kan het doen. Speaker1: KUNNEN means can. Speaker1: That concludes our lesson on the top ten Dutch verbs. Speaker1: Keep practicing these verbs every day! Speaker1: See you next time! Speaker1: Tot de volgende keer!	8	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
