grammar_zijn_hebben	A1A2:start_here	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Grammar #3: Zijn & Hebben (To Be & To Have) | A1 Beginners	Zijn en Hebben	The Verbs To Be and To Have in Dutch	In this lesson we cover: Zijn en Hebben (A1 - Grammar)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• ik ben\n• jij bent\n• hij is\n• ik heb\n• jij hebt\n• hij heeft\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• zijn — to be\n• hebben — to have\n• ik ben — I am\n• jij bent — you are\n• hij is — he is\n• ik heb — I have\n• jij hebt — you have\n• hij heeft — he has\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Irregular Verbs: Zijn and Hebben: In Dutch, 'zijn' (to be) and 'hebben' (to have) are irregular verbs in the present tense. Singular forms change with each pronoun, while all plural pronouns (wij, jullie, zij) use the standard infinitive verb.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik ben / Ik heb\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik ben / Ik heb\n- Jij bent / Jij hebt\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	TyGN6EA7Sig	\N	Speaker1: Imagine you are at a Dutch party. Speaker1: You want to say who you are. Speaker1: Or say what you have with you. Speaker1: You need two very important verbs. Speaker1: These are ZIJN and HEBBEN. Speaker1: In English: TO BE and TO HAVE. Speaker1: Just like English, they are irregular. Speaker1: Let's learn them step by step. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: First, the verb ZIJN, meaning TO BE. Speaker1: For I AM, we say IK BEN. Speaker1: Ik ben blij. Speaker1: I am happy. Speaker1: For YOU ARE, we say JIJ BENT. Speaker1: Jij bent hier. Speaker1: You are here. Speaker1: For HE IS, we say HIJ IS. Speaker1: Hij is thuis. Speaker1: He is at home. Speaker1: For SHE IS, we say ZIJ IS. Speaker1: Zij is moe. Speaker1: She is tired. Speaker1: Plural forms use the full verb ZIJN. Speaker1: Wij zijn klaar. Speaker1: We are ready. Speaker1: Time for a quick prompt! Speaker1: How would you