vocab_city_navigation	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Vocabulary #12: Navigating the City | A1 Beginners	De Stad Navigeren	Navigating the City	In this lesson we cover: De Stad Navigeren (A1-A2 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• de straat\n• het plein\n• de brug\n• de tunnel\n• het kruispunt\n• het stoplicht\n• oversteken\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• straat — street\n• plein — square\n• brug — bridge\n• tunnel — tunnel\n• kruispunt — intersection\n• stoplicht — traffic light\n• oversteken — to cross\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Articles with city nouns (de vs het): In Dutch, nouns take 'de' or 'het'. 'De straat', 'de brug', and 'de tunnel' take 'de', while 'het plein' and 'het kruispunt' take 'het'.\n- Separable verb: oversteken: The verb 'oversteken' (to cross) splits in present tense main clauses. 'Over' moves to the end of the clause.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- de straat\n- Wij steken de straat over.\n\nMini Examples\n- de straat\n- het plein\n- Wij steken de straat over.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	IauD4QNqkng	265	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson! Speaker1: Today we learn city navigation words. Speaker1: Navigating a city is very useful. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Our first word is STRAAT. Speaker1: Straat means street in English. Speaker1: De straat is lang. Speaker1: The street is long. Speaker1: Dit is onze straat. Speaker1: This is our street. Speaker1: Remember: STRAAT = street. Speaker1: Our next word is PLEIN. Speaker1: Plein means square in English. Speaker1: Het plein is groot. Speaker1: The square is big. Speaker1: Wij staan op het plein. Speaker1: We stand on the square. Speaker1: Remember: PLEIN = square. Speaker1: Our third word is BRUG. Speaker1: Brug means bridge in English. Speaker1: De brug is oud. Speaker1: The bridge is old. Speaker1: Wij gaan over de brug. Speaker1: We go over the bridge. Speaker1: Remember: BRUG = bridge. Speaker1: Our fourth word is TUNNEL. Speaker1: Tunnel means tunnel in English. Speaker1: De tunnel is donker. Speaker1: The tunnel is dark. Speaker1: Rij door de tunnel. Speaker1: Drive through the tunnel. Speaker1: Remember: TUNNEL = tunnel. Speaker1: Our fifth word is KRUISPUNT. Speaker1: Kruispunt means intersection in English. Speaker1: Dit is een kruispunt. Speaker1: This is an intersection. Speaker1: Stop bij het kruispunt. Speaker1: Stop at the intersection. Speaker1: Remember: KRUISPUNT = intersection. Speaker1: Our sixth word is STOPLICHT. Speaker1: Stoplicht means traffic light in English. Speaker1: Het stoplicht is rood. Speaker1: The traffic light is red. Speaker1: Het stoplicht is groen. Speaker1: The traffic light is green. Speaker1: Remember: STOPLICHT = traffic light. Speaker1: Our final word is OVERSTEKEN. Speaker1: Oversteken means to cross in English. Speaker1: Wij steken over. Speaker1: We cross over. Speaker1: Veilig oversteken is belangrijk. Speaker1: Crossing safely is important. Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: één. STRAAT = street. Speaker1: twee. PLEIN = square. Speaker1: drie. BRUG = bridge. Speaker1: vier. TUNNEL = tunnel. Speaker1: vijf. KRUISPUNT = intersection. Speaker1: zes. STOPLICHT = traffic light. Speaker1: zeven. OVERSTEKEN = to cross. Speaker1: Great work today!	13	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
vocab_clothing	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Vocabulary #7: Clothing | A1 Beginners	Kleding	Clothing Vocabulary	In this lesson we cover: Kleding (A1 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik draag een broek.\n• Het shirt is wit.\n• De jas is warm.\n• De schoenen zijn nieuw.\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• de broek — the pants\n• het shirt — the shirt\n• de jas — the coat\n• de schoenen — the shoes\n• de trui — the sweater\n• de jurk — the dress\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Nouns and Gender (De vs. Het): In Dutch, most clothing items use 'de', except for English loanwords like 'het shirt'.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- de broek (the pants)\n\nMini Examples\n- de broek (the pants)\n- de jas (the coat)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	cS6Y4a5mUOc	\N	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn vocabulary about clothing. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Our first word is BROEK. Speaker1: Broek means pants or trousers. Speaker1: Ik draag een broek. Speaker1: I wear pants. Speaker1: De broek is blauw. Speaker1: The pants are blue. Speaker1: Remember: BROEK means pants. Speaker1: Our next word is SHIRT. Speaker1: Shirt means shirt in English. Speaker1: Het shirt is wit. Speaker1: The shirt is white. Speaker1: Jij koopt een shirt. Speaker1: You buy a shirt. Speaker1: Our third word is JAS. Speaker1: Jas means coat or jacket. Speaker1: Ik pak mijn jas. Speaker1: I grab my coat. Speaker1: De jas is warm. Speaker1: The coat is warm. Speaker1: Our fourth word is SCHOENEN. Speaker1: Schoenen means shoes in English. Speaker1: Ik heb mooie schoenen. Speaker1: I have nice shoes. Speaker1: De schoenen zijn nieuw. Speaker1: The shoes are new. Speaker1: Next, we have TRUI. Speaker1: Trui means sweater in English. Speaker1: De trui is zacht. Speaker1: The sweater is soft. Speaker1: 