vocab_clothing_shopping	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	Clothing and Shopping - Vocabulary | Dutch in 5 minutes | A1-A2 Beginners	Kleding en Winkelen	Clothing and Shopping	In this lesson we cover: Kleding en Winkelen (A1-A2 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Welke maat zoekt u?\n• Mag ik deze broek passen?\n• Waar is de kassa?\n• Kan ik dit shirt ruilen?\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• maat — size\n• passen — to try on\n• kassa — cash register\n• kortingsbon — discount coupon\n• ruilen — to exchange\n• te groot — too big\n• te klein — too small\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Using 'te' before adjectives: In Dutch, 'te' placed before an adjective means 'too', as in 'te groot' (too big) or 'te klein' (too small).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Deze broek is te klein. (These trousers are too small.)\n\nMini Examples\n- Deze broek is te klein. (These trousers are too small.)\n- Het hemd is te groot. (The shirt is too big.)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	mqFbn5mQ6Ow	200	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch vocabulary lesson! Speaker1: Today we learn words about clothing shopping. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Shopping for clothes is fun and useful. Speaker1: Our first word is MAAT. Speaker1: Maat means size in English. Speaker1: Welke maat zoekt u? Speaker1: Which size are you looking for? Speaker1: Ik zoek een kleine maat. Speaker1: I am looking for a small size. Speaker1: Our second word is PASSEN. Speaker1: Passen means to try on in English. Speaker1: Mag ik deze broek passen? Speaker1: May I try on these trousers? Speaker1: U kunt hier passen. Speaker1: You can try on here. Speaker1: Our third word is KASSA. Speaker1: Kassa means cash register in English. Speaker1: Waar is de kassa? Speaker1: Where is the cash register? Speaker1: De kassa is daar. Speaker1: The cash register is over there. Speaker1: Our fourth word is KORTINGSBON. Speaker1: Kortingsbon means discount coupon in English. Speaker1: Ik heb een kortingsbon. Speaker1: I have a discount coupon. Speaker1: Gebruik de kortingsbon hier. Speaker1: Use the discount coupon here. Speaker1: Our fifth word is RUILEN. Speaker1: Ruilen means to exchange in English. Speaker1: Kan ik dit shirt ruilen? Speaker1: Can I exchange this shirt? Speaker1: U kunt dit shirt ruilen. Speaker1: You can exchange this shirt. Speaker1: Our sixth phrase is TE GROOT. Speaker1: Te groot means too big in English. Speaker1: Deze jas is te groot. Speaker1: This jacket is too big. Speaker1: Het hemd is te groot. Speaker1: The shirt is too big. Speaker1: Our seventh phrase is TE KLEIN. Speaker1: Te klein means too small in English. Speaker1: Deze schoenen zijn te klein. Speaker1: These shoes are too small. Speaker1: De broek is te klein. Speaker1: The trousers are too small. Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: één. MAAT = size. Speaker1: twee. PASSEN = to try on. Speaker1: drie. KASSA = cash register. Speaker1: vier. KORTINGSBON = discount coupon. Speaker1: vijf. RUILEN = to exchange. Speaker1: zes. TE GROOT = too big. Speaker1: zeven. TE KLEIN = too small. Speaker1: Great work today!	20	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
vocab_alphabet_pronunciation	A1A2:start_here	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Vocabulary #2: The Alphabet & Pronunciation | A1 Beginners	Het alfabet	The Alphabet	In this lesson we cover: Het alfabet (A1-A2 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Dit is het alfabet\n• Laten we beginnen\n• De zon schijnt vandaag\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• appel — apple\n• boek — book\n• dag — day\n• eten — to eat / food\n• geel — yellow\n• huis — house\n• ja — yes\n• kat — cat\n• man — man\n• oog — eye\n• water — water\n• zon — sun\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Pronunciation of G: The Dutch 'G' is pronounced as a soft, raspy sound produced in the back of the throat, similar to the Scottish 'ch' in 'loch'.\n- Pronunciation of J: In Dutch, the letter 'J' is always pronounced like the English 'Y' as in 'yes'.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- geel (yellow)\n- ja (yes)\n\nMini Examples\n- geel (yellow)\n- goed (good)\n- ja (yes)\n- jong (young)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	CS44PTR2JwU	272	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch vocabulary lesson! Speaker1: Today we learn about the Dutch alphabet. Speaker1: We learn words for different letters. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Our first letter is A. Speaker1: Our word is APPEL. Speaker1: Appel means apple in English. Speaker1: Dit is een appel. Speaker1: This is an apple. Speaker1: De appel is rood. Speaker1: The apple is red. Speaker1: Remember: APPEL = apple. Speaker1: Our next letter is B. Speaker1: Our word is BOEK. Speaker1: Boek means book in English. Speaker1: Ik lees een boek. Speaker1: I read a book. Speaker1: Het boek is mooi. Speaker1: The book is beautiful. Speaker1: Remember: BOEK = book. Speaker1: Our next letter is K. Speaker1: Our word is KAT. Speaker1: Kat means cat in English. Speaker1: De kat is klein. Speaker1: The cat is small. Speaker1: Ik zie de kat. Spe