h. Speaker1: Ik krijg een mooi cadeau. Speaker1: I get a beautiful gift. Speaker1: Dit cadeau is voor jou. Speaker1: This present is for you. Speaker1: Our next word is KAART. Speaker1: Kaart means card in English. Speaker1: Ik schrijf een mooie kaart. Speaker1: I write a nice card. Speaker1: De kaart ligt op tafel. Speaker1: The card is on the table. Speaker1: Our next word is UITNODIGING. Speaker1: Uitnodiging means invitation in English. Speaker1: Ik stuur een uitnodiging. Speaker1: I send an invitation. Speaker1: Bedankt voor de uitnodiging! Speaker1: Thank you for the invitation! Speaker1: Our last word is GELUKKIG. Speaker1: Gelukkig means happy in English. Speaker1: Ik ben vandaag heel gelukkig. Speaker1: I am very happy today. Speaker1: Iedereen is hier gelukkig. Speaker1: Everyone is happy here. Speaker1: Great work today! Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: één. VERJAARDAG = birthday. Speaker1: twee. FEEST = party. Speaker1: drie. TAART = cake. Speaker1: vier. CADEAU = gift. Speaker1: vijf. KAART = card. Speaker1: zes. UITNODIGING = invitation. Speaker1: zeven. GELUKKIG = happy. Speaker1: Great job learning Dutch! Speaker1: See you next time!	15	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
vocab_cooking	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	Cooking and the kitchen - Vocabulary | Dutch in 5 minutes | A1-A2 Beginners	Koken en de keuken	Cooking and the kitchen	In this lesson we cover: Koken en de keuken (A1-A2 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• koken in de keuken\n• het eten op het bord\n• een scherp mes\n• een goed recept\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• koken — to cook\n• bakken — to bake / fry\n• snijden — to cut\n• pan — pan\n• pot — pot\n• mes — knife\n• bord — plate\n• vork — fork\n• lepel — spoon\n• recept — recipe\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Nouns with 'de' and 'het': In Dutch, every noun is either a 'de' word or a 'het' word. Kitchen utensils vary: 'de pan', 'de pot', 'de vork', 'de lepel', but 'het mes', 'het bord', 'het recept'.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- de pan (the pan)\n\nMini Examples\n- de pan (the pan)\n- het mes (the knife)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	O5SJMDsfINA	284	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch vocabulary lesson! Speaker1: Today we learn words about cooking. Speaker1: Cooking in the kitchen is fun! Speaker1: Let's begin with our lesson. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's start! Speaker1: Our first word is KOKEN. Speaker1: Koken means to cook. Speaker1: Ik ga vandaag koken. Speaker1: I am going to cook today. Speaker1: Wij koken in de keuken. Speaker1: We cook in the kitchen. Speaker1: Our second word is BAKKEN. Speaker1: Bakken means to bake or fry. Speaker1: Ik bak een lekker brood. Speaker1: I bake a delicious bread. Speaker1: Hij bakt een eitje. Speaker1: He fries an egg. Speaker1: Next, we have SNIJDEN. Speaker1: Snijden means to cut. Speaker1: Ik snijd de groenten. Speaker1: I cut the vegetables. Speaker1: Zij snijdt het verse brood. Speaker1: She cuts the fresh bread. Speaker1: Now let's learn kitchen tools. Speaker1: Our fourth word is PAN. Speaker1: Pan means pan. Speaker1: De pan staat op het vuur. Speaker1: The pan is on the stove. Speaker1: Soep zit in de pan. Speaker1: Soup is in the pan. Speaker1: The fifth word is POT. Speaker1: Pot means pot or jar. Speaker1: De pot is erg groot. Speaker1: The pot is very big. Speaker1: Er zit suiker in de pot. Speaker1: There is sugar in the pot. Speaker1: Our sixth word is MES. Speaker1: Mes means knife. Speaker1: Het mes is heel scherp. Speaker1: The knife is very sharp. Speaker1: Ik gebruik een scherp mes. Speaker1: I use a sharp knife. Speaker1: The seventh word is BORD. Speaker1: Bord means plate. Speaker1: Het eten staat op het bord. Speaker1: The food is on the plate. Speaker1: Dit bord is schoon. Speaker1: This plate is clean. Speaker1: Next is the word VORK. Speaker1: Vork means fork. Speaker1: Ik eet met een vork. Speaker1: I eat with a fork. Speaker1: De vork liegt op tafel. Speaker1: The fork lies on the table. Speaker1: The ninth word is LEPEL. Speaker1: Lepel means spoon. Speaker1: Ik gebruik een kleine lepel. Speaker1: I use a small spoon. Speaker1: De soep vraagt om een lepel. Speaker1: The soup needs a spoon. Speaker1: Our last word is RECEPT. Speaker1: Recept means recipe. Speaker1: Ik lees een goed recept. Speaker1: I read a good recipe. Speaker1: Dit recept is heel makkelijk. Speaker1: This recipe is very easy. Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: één. KOKEN = to cook. Speaker1: twee. BAKKEN = to bake. Speaker1: drie. SNIJDEN = to cut. Speaker1: vier. PAN = pan. Speaker1: vijf. POT = pot. Speaker1: zes. MES = knife. Speaker1: zeven. BORD = plate. Speaker1: acht. VORK = fork. Speaker1: negen. LEPEL = spoon. Speaker1: tien. RECEPT = recipe. Speaker1: Great work learning Dutch cooking words! Speaker1: See you next time!	23	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
vocab_digital_daily	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	Digital Daily Life - Vocabulary | Dutch in 5 minutes | A1-A2 Beginners	Digitaal Dagelijks Leven	Digital Daily Life	In this lesson we cover: Digitaal Dagelijks Leven (A1-A2 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik gebruik mijn telefoon\n• Het internet is erg snel\n• Ik stuur een kort bericht\n• Ik moet mijn telefoon opladen\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• telefoon — phone\n• internet — internet\n• app — app\n• bericht — message\n• e-mail — email\n• opladen — to charge\n• wifi — wifi\n• wachtwoord — password\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Separable Verbs: opladen: The verb 'opladen' is a separable verb. In standard present tense sentences, 'laad' comes earlier and 'op' goes to the end of the clause.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik laad mijn telefoon op.\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik laad mijn telefoon op.\n- De batterij laadt nu op.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	4OY37b3Xxjo	313	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Speaker1: Welcome to Dutch class! Speaker1: Today we learn digital words. Speaker1: Are you ready? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Word number one is TELEFOON. Speaker1: Telefoon means phone in English. Speaker1: Let's hear an example. Speaker1: Ik gebruik mijn telefoon. Speaker1: I use my phone. Speaker1: Mijn telefoon is nieuw. Speaker1: My phone is new. Speaker1: Word number two is INTERNET. Speaker1: Internet means internet in English. Speaker1: Here is the first sentence. Speaker1: Het internet is erg snel. Speaker1: The internet is very fast. Speaker1: Ik zit op het internet. Speaker1: I am on the internet. Speaker1: Word number three is APP. Speaker1: App means app in English. Speaker1: Let's see a sentence. Speaker1: Ik open een nieuwe app. Speaker1: I open a new app. Speaker1: Deze app is erg handig. Speaker1: This app is very useful. Speaker1: Word number four is BERICHT. Speaker1: Bericht means message in English. Speaker1: Listen to this sentence. Speaker1: Ik stuur een kort bericht. Speaker1: I send a short message. Speaker1: Hij leest het bericht nu. Speaker1: He reads the message now. Speaker1: Word number five is E-MAIL. Speaker1: E-mail means email in English. Speaker1: Here is an example. Speaker1: Ik schrijf een e-mail. Speaker1: I write an email. Speaker1: De e-mail is erg belangrijk. Speaker1: The email is very important. Speaker1: Word number six is OPLADEN. Speaker1: Opladen means to charge. Speaker1: Let's practice a sentence. Speaker1: Ik moet mijn telefoon opladen. Speaker1: I must charge my phone. Speaker1: De batterij laadt nu op. Speaker1: The battery charges now. Speaker1: Word number seven is WIFI. Speaker1: Wifi means wifi in English. Speaker1: Listen carefully now. Speaker1: Is er hier snelle wifi? Speaker1: Is there fast wifi here? Speaker1: De wifi werkt heel goed. Speaker1: The wifi works very well. Speaker1: Word number eight is WACHTWOORD. Speaker1: Wachtwoord means password in English. Speaker1: Here is our last sentence. Speaker1: Mijn wachtwoord is geheim. Speaker1: My password is secret. Speaker1: Vul het juiste wachtwoord in. Speaker1: Enter the correct password. Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: één. TELEFOON = phone. Speaker1: twee. INTERNET = internet. Speaker1: drie. APP = app. Speaker1: vier. BERICHT = message. Speaker1: vijf. E-MAIL = email. Speaker1: zes. OPLADEN = to charge. Speaker1: zeven. WIFI = wifi. Speaker1: acht. WACHTWOORD = password. Speaker1: Great work today!	19	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
vocab_food_drinks	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Vocabulary #4: Food & Drinks | A1 Beginners	Eten en Drinken	Food and Drinks	In this lesson we cover: Eten en Drinken (A1 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik eet brood\n• Ik drink melk\n• Koffie met melk\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• brood — bread\n• kaas — cheese\n• melk — milk\n• water — water\n• koffie — coffee\n• thee — tea\n• appel — apple\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Basic Food Verbs: Eten & Drinken: In Dutch, 'eten' means 'to eat' and 'drinken' means 'to drink'. For the subject 'ik' (I), use 'eet' and 'drink'.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik eet brood. (I eat bread.)\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik eet brood. (I eat bread.)\n- Ik drink water. (I drink water.)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	DhkRQh6keoE	\N	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn food and drink words. Speaker1: Welkom bij de les! Speaker1: Are you ready to learn Dutch? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Our first word is BROOD. Speaker1: Brood means bread in English. Speaker1: Let me use it in a sentence. Speaker1: Ik eet brood. Speaker1: Het brood is vers. Speaker1: Our second word is KAAS. Speaker1: Kaas means cheese. Speaker1: The Dutch love cheese! Speaker1: Ik hou van kaas. Speaker1: De kaas is lekker. Speaker1: Hij eet brood met kaas. Speaker1: Next, we have MELK. Speaker1: Melk means milk. Speaker1: Ik drink melk. Speaker1: De melk is koud. Speaker1: Zij drinkt verse melk. Speaker1: Our fourth word is WATER. Speaker1: Water is spelled the same! Speaker1: Ik drink graag water. Speaker1: Het water is koud. Speaker1: Geef mij water. Speaker1: Now let's look at KOFFIE. Speaker1: Koffie means coffee. Speaker1: Ik drink koffie. Speaker1: De koffie is heet. Speaker1: Koffie met melk is lekker. Speaker1: Next word is THEE. Speaker1: Thee means tea. Speaker1: Ik wil graag thee. Speaker1: De thee is warm. Speaker1: Drink jij thee? Speaker1: Our last word is APPEL. Speaker1: Appel means apple. Speaker1: Ik eet een appel. Speaker1: De appel is groen. Speaker1: Een appel is gezond. Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: één. BROOD = bread. Speaker1: twee. KAAS = cheese. Speaker1: drie. MELK = milk. Speaker1: vier. WATER = water. Speaker1: vijf. KOFFIE = coffee. Speaker1: zes. THEE = tea. Speaker1: zeven. APPEL = apple. Speaker1: Great work today! Speaker1: See you next time!	3	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
vocab_greetings	A1A2:start_here	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Vocabulary #1: Greetings & Farewells | A1 Beginners	Groeten en Afscheid	Greetings and Farewells	In this lesson we cover: Groeten en Afscheid (A1 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Hallo\n• Goedemorgen\n• Goedemiddag\n• Goedenavond\n• Dag\n• Tot ziens\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• hallo — hello\n• goedemorgen — good morning\n• goedemiddag — good afternoon\n• goedenavond — good evening\n• dag — bye / hello\n• tot ziens — goodbye\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Time-based Greetings: In Dutch, standard formal and informal greetings often incorporate the time of day: 'morgen' (morning), 'middag' (afternoon), and 'avond' (evening).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Goedemorgen! (Good morning!)\n\nMini Examples\n- Goedemorgen! (Good morning!)\n- Goedemiddag! (Good afternoon!)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	8InB_HLYEv4	\N	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson! Speaker1: Today we learn greetings. Speaker1: Greetings are useful every day. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let us begin! Speaker1: Our first word is HALLO. Speaker1: Hallo means hello in English. Speaker1: Let's hear it in a sentence. Speaker1: Hallo, ik ben Jan. Speaker1: Hello, I am Jan. Speaker1: Hallo, hoe gaat het? Speaker1: Hello, how are you? Speaker1: Remember: HALLO equals hello. Speaker1: Our next word is GOEDEMORGEN. Speaker1: Goedemorgen means good morning. Speaker1: You say this in the morning. Speaker1: Goedemorgen, fijne dag! Speaker1: Good morning, have a nice day! Speaker1: Ik zeg goedemorgen. Speaker1: I say good morning. Speaker1: Remember: GOEDEMORGEN equals good morning. Speaker1: Our next word is GOEDEMIDDAG. Speaker1: Goedemiddag means good afternoon. Speaker1: You say this in the afternoon. Speaker1: Goedemiddag, meneer Bakker! Speaker1: Good afternoon, Mr. Bakker! Speaker1: Goedemiddag, hoe is het? Speaker1: Good afternoon, how is it? Speaker1: Remember: GOEDEMIDDAG equals good afternoon. Speaker1: Our next word is GOEDENAVOND. Speaker1: Goedenavond means good evening. Speaker1: You say this in the evening. Speaker1: Goedenavond, welkom hier! Speaker1: Good evening, welcome here! Speaker1: Goedenavond allemaal! Speaker1: Good evening everyone! Speaker1: Remember: GOEDENAVOND equals good evening. Speaker1: Our next word is DAG. Speaker1: Dag means bye or hello. Speaker1: It is a short greeting. Speaker1: Dag, tot morgen! Speaker1: Bye, see you tomorrow! Speaker1: Ik zeg dag. Speaker1: I say bye. Speaker1: Remember: DAG equals bye. Speaker1: Our next word is TOT ZIENS. Speaker1: Tot ziens means goodbye. Speaker1: You say this when leaving. Speaker1: Tot ziens, fijne dag! Speaker1: Goodbye, have a nice day! Speaker1: Tot ziens en bedankt! Speaker1: Goodbye and thank you! Speaker1: Remember: TOT ZIENS equals goodbye. Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: één. HALLO = hello. Speaker1: twee. GOEDEMORGEN = good morning. Speaker1: drie. GOEDEMIDDAG = good afternoon. Speaker1: vier. GOEDENAVOND = good evening. Speaker1: vijf. DAG = bye. Speaker1: zes. TOT ZIENS = goodbye. Speaker1: Great work today!	1	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
vocab_health_body	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Vocabulary #14: Health & the Body | A1 Beginners	Gezondheid en het Lichaam	Health and the Body	In this lesson we cover: Gezondheid en het Lichaam (A1-A2 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• ziek zijn\n• pijn hebben\n• koorts hebben\n• pillen nemen\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• ziek — sick\n• pijn — pain\n• koorts — fever\n• hoesten — to cough\n• pillen — pills\n• recept — prescription\n• apotheker — pharmacist\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Using 'zijn' vs 'hebben' for illness: Use 'zijn' with adjectives like 'ziek' (Ik ben ziek), but use 'hebben' with nouns like 'pijn' or 'koorts' (Ik heb pijn / Het kind heeft koorts).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik ben ziek.\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik ben ziek.\n- Ik heb pijn.\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	nCVUN5aDtq4	239	Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn health vocabulary. Speaker1: Health is very important. Speaker1: Let's start with our lesson. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Our first word is ZIEK. Speaker1: Ziek means sick in English. Speaker1: Let's hear it in a sentence. Speaker1: Ik ben vandaag ziek. Speaker1: I am sick today. Speaker1: Ben jij ook ziek? Speaker1: Are you sick too? Speaker1: Our second word is PIJN. Speaker1: Pijn means pain in English. Speaker1: Let's hear an example sentence. Speaker1: Ik heb veel pijn. Speaker1: I have much pain. Speaker1: Waar heb je pijn? Speaker1: Where do you have pain? Speaker1: Our third word is KOORTS. Speaker1: Koorts means fever in English. Speaker1: Here is a sentence with koorts. Speaker1: Het kind heeft koorts. Speaker1: The child has a fever. Speaker1: Hij heeft hoge koorts. Speaker1: He has a high fever. Speaker1: Our fourth word is HOESTEN. Speaker1: Hoesten means to cough in English. Speaker1: Let's use it in a sentence. Speaker1: Ik moet veel hoesten. Speaker1: I have to cough a lot. Speaker1: Waarom moet je hoesten? Speaker1: Why must you cough? Speaker1: Our fifth word is PILLEN. Speaker1: Pillen means pills in English. Speaker1: Listen to the next sentence. Speaker1: Ik neem twee pillen. Speaker1: I take two pills. Speaker1: De pillen helpen goed. Speaker1: The pills help well. Speaker1: Our sixth word is RECEPT. Speaker1: Recept means prescription in English. Speaker1: Let's practice this word. Speaker1: Ik heb een recept. Speaker1: I have a prescription. Speaker1: De dokter geeft een recept. Speaker1: The doctor gives a prescription. Speaker1: Our seventh word is APOTHEKER. Speaker1: Apotheker means pharmacist in English. Speaker1: Here is our last sentence. Speaker1: De apotheker helpt mij. Speaker1: The pharmacist helps me. Speaker1: Ik spreek de apotheker. Speaker1: I speak to the pharmacist. Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: één. ZIEK = sick. Speaker1: twee. PIJN = pain. Speaker1: drie. KOORTS = fever. Speaker1: vier. HOESTEN = to cough. Speaker1: vijf. PILLEN = pills. Speaker1: zes. RECEPT = prescription. Speaker1: zeven. APOTHEKER = pharmacist. Speaker1: Great work today!	17	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
vocab_home_rooms	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Vocabulary #5: Rooms in the House | A1 Beginners	De Kamers in Huis	Rooms in the House	In this lesson we cover: De Kamers in Huis (A1 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• de keuken\n• de slaapkamer\n• de woonkamer\n• de tafel\n• de stoel\n• het bed\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• keuken — kitchen\n• slaapkamer — bedroom\n• woonkamer — living room\n• tafel — table\n• stoel — chair\n• bed — bed\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Nouns and Articles (De vs. Het): In Dutch, nouns take either 'de' or 'het'. Most rooms and furniture items taught today take 'de' (de keuken, de slaapkamer, de woonkamer, de tafel, de stoel), except 'bed', which takes 'het' (het bed).\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- de keuken (the kitchen)\n\nMini Examples\n- de keuken (the kitchen)\n- het bed (the bed)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1Dutch\n	OdgofyHVpHg	\N	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Speaker1: Welcome to today's Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn about the house. Speaker1: We will learn rooms and furniture. Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: Let's begin! Speaker1: Our first word is KEUKEN. Speaker1: Keuken means kitchen in English. Speaker1: Dit is de keuken. Speaker1: This is the kitchen. Speaker1: Ik kook in de keuken. Speaker1: I cook in the kitchen. Speaker1: Remember: KEUKEN = kitchen. Speaker1: Our second word is SLAAPKAMER. Speaker1: Slaapkamer means bedroom in English. Speaker1: Dit is de slaapkamer. Speaker1: This is the bedroom. Speaker1: Ik slaap in de slaapkamer. Speaker1: I sleep in the bedroom. Speaker1: Remember: SLAAPKAMER = bedroom. Speaker1: Our third word is WOONKAMER. Speaker1: Woonkamer means living room in English. Speaker1: Dit is de woonkamer. Speaker1: This is the living room. Speaker1: De woonkamer is groot. Speaker1: The living room is big. Speaker1: Remember: WOONKAMER = living room. Speaker1: Our fourth word is TAFEL. Speaker1: Tafel means table in English. Speaker1: Het is een grote tafel. Speaker1: It is a big table. Speaker1: De tafel staat in de keuken. Speaker1: The table is in the kitchen. Speaker1: Remember: TAFEL = table. Speaker1: Our fifth word is STOEL. Speaker1: Stoel means chair in English. Speaker1: Ik zit op een stoel. Speaker1: I sit on a chair. Speaker1: De stoel staat bij de tafel. Speaker1: The chair is by the table. Speaker1: Remember: STOEL = chair. Speaker1: Our sixth word is BED. Speaker1: Bed means bed in English. Speaker1: Het bed staat in de slaapkamer. Speaker1: The bed is in the bedroom. Speaker1: Ik lig op het bed. Speaker1: I lie on the bed. Speaker1: Remember: BED = bed. Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: één. KEUKEN = kitchen. Speaker1: twee. SLAAPKAMER = bedroom. Speaker1: drie. WOONKAMER = living room. Speaker1: vier. TAFEL = table. Speaker1: vijf. STOEL = chair. Speaker1: zes. BED = bed. Speaker1: Great work today! Speaker1: See you next time!	7	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
