vocab_polite_expressions	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	Polite Expressions in Dutch - Vocabulary | Dutch in 5 minutes | A1-A2 Beginners	Beleefde Woorden	Polite Expressions in Dutch	In this lesson we cover: Beleefde Woorden (A1-A2 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Dank je wel voor de hulp.\n• Een appel voor jou, alsjeblieft.\n• Excuseer, waar is de trein?\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• alsjeblieft — please / here you go (informal)\n• alstublieft — please / here you go (formal)\n• dank je wel — thank you (informal)\n• dank u wel — thank you (formal)\n• graag — gladly / with pleasure\n• graag gedaan — you're welcome\n• sorry — sorry\n• excuseer — excuse me\n• geen dank — don't mention it / you're welcome\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Formal vs. Informal Politeness: In Dutch, politeness depends highly on whether you are using the informal 'je' or formal 'u'. For 'thank you', 'dank je wel' is informal, while 'dank u wel' is formal. Similarly, 'alsjeblieft' contains 'je' and is informal, whereas 'alstublieft' contains 'u' and is formal.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Dank je wel, mijn vriend. (Thank you, my friend. - Informal)\n\nMini Examples\n- Dank je wel, mijn vriend. (Thank you, my friend. - Informal)\n- Dank u wel, meneer. (Thank you, sir. - Formal)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	KOaXktaXeHc	223	Speaker1: Welcome to our Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn polite words. Speaker1: Laten we nu beginnen! Speaker1: Our first word is DANK JE WEL. Speaker1: This is informal for thank you. Speaker1: Let us hear an example. Speaker1: Dank je wel voor de hulp. Speaker1: Ik zeg dank je wel. Speaker1: Now, let's learn the formal version. Speaker1: The formal version is DANK U WEL. Speaker1: Use this with teachers or strangers. Speaker1: Dank u wel, meneer Bakker. Speaker1: Wij zeggen dank u wel. Speaker1: Our next word is ALSJEBLIEFT. Speaker1: This means please or here you go. Speaker1: It is used in informal situations. Speaker1: Een appel voor jou, alsjeblieft. Speaker1: Help mij, alsjeblieft. Speaker1: Now, the formal version is ALSTUBLIEFT. Speaker1: Use this with people you respect. Speaker1: Koffie voor u, alstublieft. Speaker1: Spreekt u langzaam, alstublieft. Speaker1: Excellent. Our next word is GRAAG. Speaker1: Graag means gladly or with pleasure. Speaker1: It is used to accept offers. Speaker1: Ik wil graag een thee. Speaker1: Ja, ik doe dat graag. Speaker1: Now we look at GRAAG GEDAAN. Speaker1: It means you are welcome. Speaker1: Graag gedaan, tot ziens! Speaker1: Next, we have the word SORRY. Speaker1: Sorry means sorry in Dutch too. Speaker1: Sorry, ik ben te laat. Speaker1: Het spijt me, sorry hoor. Speaker1: Another polite word is EXCUSEER. Speaker1: Excuseer means excuse me. Speaker1: Use it to get someone's attention. Speaker1: Excuseer, waar is de trein? Speaker1: Excuseer mij, mag ik erlangs? Speaker1: Our last phrase is GEEN DANK. Speaker1: Geen dank means no thanks needed. Speaker1: It is another way to say welcome. Speaker1: Geen dank, mijn vriend. Speaker1: Geen dank, graag gedaan hoor. Speaker1: You are doing very well. Speaker1: Let's recap today's words. Speaker1: Nummer één. Speaker1: ALSJEBLIEFT = please. Speaker1: Nummer twee. Speaker1: ALSTUBLIEFT = please (formal). Speaker1: Nummer drie. Speaker1: DANK JE WEL = thank you. Speaker1: Nummer vier. Speaker1: DANK U WEL = thank you (formal). Speaker1: Nummer vijf. Speaker1: GRAAG = gladly. Speaker1: Nummer zes. Speaker1: GRAAG GEDAAN = you are welcome. Speaker1: Nummer zeven. Speaker1: SORRY = sorry. Speaker1: Nummer acht. Speaker1: EXCUSEER = excuse me. Speaker1: Nummer negen. Speaker1: GEEN DANK = you are welcome. Speaker1: Great work today!	22	f	\N	2026-08-18 18:51:29.654518+00
vocab_sports_hobbies	A1A2:vocabulary	A1A2	🇳🇱 Dutch Vocabulary #16: Sports & Hobbies | A1 Beginners	Sporten en Hobby's	Sports and Hobbies	In this lesson we cover: Sporten en Hobby's (A1-A2 - Vocabulary)\n\nLearn Dutch from scratch with a clear, slow narrated lesson designed for complete beginners.\n\n---\n\n🗣 Key Phrases\n• Ik hou van voetbal\n• Ik ga graag zwemmen\n• Ik fiets elke dag\n• Ik lees een boek\n• Ik luister naar muziek\n\n---\n\n📖 Vocabulary\n• voetbal — football\n• zwemmen — swimming\n• fietsen — cycling\n• lezen — reading\n• koken — cooking\n• muziek — music\n• schilderen — painting\n\n---\n\n📝 Grammar Notes\n- Verbs as Hobbies: In Dutch, activity verbs like 'fietsen' (to cycle), 'zwemmen' (to swim), and 'lezen' (to read) are used in the present tense to express your hobbies.\n\nPattern Breakdown\n- Ik fiets elke dag. (I cycle every day.)\n\nMini Examples\n- Ik fiets elke dag. (I cycle every day.)\n- Ik lees een boek. (I read a book.)\n\n---\n\n#LearnDutch #Dutch #DutchForBeginners #DutchIn5Minutes #Nederlandse #DutchLesson #A1-A2Dutch\n	sVNVYw8h4iQ	235	Speaker1: Hello everyone! Speaker1: Welcome to your Dutch lesson. Speaker1: Today we learn sports and hobbies. Speaker1: Are you ready? Speaker1: Laten we beginnen! Speaker1: We will learn seven words. Speaker1: Our first word is VOETBAL. Speaker1: Voetbal means football. Speaker1: Let's hear it in a sentence. Speaker1: Ik hou van voetbal. Speaker1: Wij spelen samen voetbal. Speaker1: Remember: VOETBAL = football. Speaker1: Our second word is ZWEMMEN. Speaker1: Zwemmen means swimming. Speaker1: Ik ga graag zwemmen. Speaker1: Het water is heel warm. Speaker1: Remember: ZWEMMEN = swimming. Speaker1: Our third word is FIETSEN. Speaker1: Fietsen means cycling. Speaker1: Ik fiets naar school. Speaker1: Ik fiets elke dag. Speaker1: Remember: FIETSEN = cycling. Speaker1: Now let's talk about hobbies. Speaker1: Our fourth word is LEZEN. Speaker1: Lezen means reading. Speaker1: Ik lees een goed boek. Speaker1: Hi